regress\artifacts\lcashiftub\pass2_c7d7414\codex.txt:1254:GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\ollama_glm.txt:25:**Q6.** [REASONED] The letter is right; keep them separate. Determinism-across-runs is sound for `random()`-shaped defects (the packet quotes differing rung-3 values, so this fork's `random()` varies across runs) and **unsound for stale-stack defects**: stale stack is run-invariant, so a live leak yields a green determinism row. Counterexample needing no files: if that frame's `buf[4]` is always `0x5A` from the same prior call sequence, the destination is fully deterministic and the leak fully live. Merging installs the vacuity floor's exact failure mode into the round design. `sgirandomcolour` is also doubly unready: no witness, and its liveness depends on `fatal()`'s semantics, which the packet does not contain.
regress\artifacts\lcashiftub\pass2_c7d7414\codex.txt:1255:GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:918:  **Keep the letter: separate.** The proposed shared oracle — "destination deterministic across runs" — is **vacuous for sgirebufuninit and only toothed for sgirandomcolour, proven from the tree**: `srandom(tv_sec ^ getpid() ^ tv_usec)` runs at startup (`src/core/main.c:688-691`), so `random()` varies per run, but stale-stack reuse under a fixed guest program is run-*deterministic* — the buggy build passes a determinism oracle. One shared oracle that is weak for one member is the vacuity floor's problem, as the brief itself says. `sgirandomcolour` also has no witness and the `fatal()` at :313 falls through to the write (confirmed — `fatal` does not exit), so it is reachable but unwitnessed. Separate rounds; if ever merged, the detector needs the value-oracle anyway, which is two oracles in everything but name.
