on-sweep scores). Vectors 0..3's tail arms \u2014 disclosed (m88kexchwtraps). \n\n4. Operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read: e.g., swapping operands in the PSR write (MODE/IND/SFD1/SFRZ bits) \u2014 M_NOSFRZ, M_NOIND, M_NOSFD1, M_NOMODE, M_CARRYCLR all measured non-sweep. EPSR swaps \u2014 M_NOEPSR, M_EPSRAFTER. Shadows \u2014 M_NOSHADOW, M_SNIPX, etc.\n\n5. Joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them: {class} x {MODE} closure, {SFRZ, is_trap} x {class} closure, {SFRZ} x {PSR bit} at fallback, etc.\n\nSo the question is whether there's a HOLE. Let me hunt.\n\nThe disclosed gap that jumps out: \"an exemption of a single residual vector other than 11/50/127 from the fallback (a value the body never tests as a case)\". So e.g., mutant: fallback `if (vector < 128 && !implemented)` \u2192 add `&& vector != 60` \u2014 exempts vector 60 from the fallback. No row names 60. Clean sweep on v10. But it's DISCLOSED. Disclosed gaps are not floor breaches (by the panel's convention, presumably \u2014 the chain closes on zero floor breaches SURVIVING ADJUDICATION; disclosed ones are adjudicated). So that doesn't count.\n\nBut wait \u2014 is it disclosed for the COMPOSITE? The docstring says \"Does NOT catch: ... vectors 11..113 and 116..127 (fallback halts by...\" hmm, the diff shows \"vectors 11..113 and 116..127 (fallback halts by\" \u2014 truncated in the diff (the line continues outside the hunk). And the \"still disclosed\" list includes \"an exemption of a single residual vector other than 11/50/127 from the fallback\". So yes disclosed.\n\nNow, let me look for UNdisclosed holes.\n\n**Hole candidate 1: switch 1's class labels for vectors 116..127.** Covered by the same residual disclosure? The disclosure says \"an exemption of a single residual vector other than 11/50/127 from the fallback (a value the body never tests as a case)\". A switch-1 label change for vector 60 (mapping 60 to a class) \u2014 that IS an exemption from the fallback (if implemented becomes true). Disclosed. OK.\n\n**Hole candidate 2: the re-route's `is_trap` for vectors 0..3.** \"0..3 are the hardware-event tails, m88kexchwtraps\" \u2014 disclosed. Traps naming 0..3? Hardware events, no rows; disclosed.\n\n**Hole candidate 3: SFU1 (114/115).** First-exception SFU1 disclosed (m88kexcsfu1). SFU1_IMPRECISE 115 disclosed. sfu1h/usfu1 pin 114 at the fallback (second fault). What about the fallback's handling of 115? sfu1h's handler raises SFU1 (114)... 115 likewise disclosed.\n\n**Hole candidate 4: the refatal channel itself.** REFATAL = \"SFRZ already set in PSR\". A mutant that changes the diagnostic TEXT \u2014 e.g., prints a different message \u2014 would sweep if no row checks the exact text. Does any row pin the re-route's diagnostic text? The refatal channel checks presence/absence of the substring \"SFRZ already set in PSR\". A mutant changing the text to \"[ SFRZ set in PSR => ERROR ]\" would: err* rows expect REFATAL in tr \u2192 absent \u2192 bad=refatal \u2192 caught! Wait: REFATAL_EXPECT[error]=True; if the mutant prints different text, `(REFATAL in tr) != True` \u2192 bad. So text mutants are caught for err* rows. Good \u2014 the channel pins the text via presence on err* rows.\n\n**Hole candidate 5: fatal() being a plain print.** Premise 3 says fatal is a plain print with no early-out. A mutant making fatal actually stop the cpu \u2014 would err* rows still deliver? If fatal stopped the cpu, the err* rows would STOP instead of DELIVER \u2192 caught by 41/41. But that's product code outside the composite's edited lines? The composite = #502 fix + probe v10. fatal is in debugmsg.c \u2014 not part of the diff. Out of enumeration scope (\"predicates dominating the diff's edited lines\").\n\n**Hole candidate 6: the `update_shadow_regs = false` placement.** In the correct code, update_shadow_regs = false happens for ANY SFRZ-set exception (trap or not). For traps with SFRZ set (trapfrozen*), shadows frozen \u2014 pinned by BADREGS asserts (want dict: frozen shadow values). For err* rows, also frozen. What about a mutant that sets update_shadow_regs = false only when !is_trap? Then trapfrozen* rows would have UNFROZEN shadows \u2014 second fault updates shadows to second-fault values \u2014 BADREGS \u2192 caught (M_SFRZ_TRAP_UPDATE 28/29 agy \u2014 this is that mutant, caught).\n\nWhat about update_shadow_regs = false only when is_trap? Then err* rows get updated shadows \u2192 BADREGS \u2192 caught (the M_SFRZTRAP polarity-ish? no \u2014 this is \"update only in trap arm\"; err* rows assert frozen shadows \u2192 caught).\n\n**Hole candidate 7: The fallback's stop semantics.** \"every cpu stopped, return\" \u2014 HALT requires \"the fallback diagnostic naming the row's vector AND `All machines stopped.`\". A mutant that stops only the current cpu? M_NOSTOP 21/23 \u2014 caught. Hmm wait, \"every cpu stopped\" \u2014 the diagnostic and stop. What about the ORDER (print before stop)? Any row care? If a mutant printed after stopping... the transcript would still contain both lines probably. Not a floor question likely.\n\n**Hole candidate 8: the re-route ordering relative to the fallback.** Premise 2: fallback BEFORE re-route. A mutant that swaps the order (re-route first, then fallback): for err* rows, re-route sets vector = ERROR = 10... wait, err* rows: second fault with SFRZ set, non-trap, class X (say OVERFLOW=9). Re-route: vector = ERROR (10), fatal printed. THEN fallback: vector < 128 (10 < 128) && !implemented \u2014 is ERROR implemented? Yes (class 10 exists). So no fallback. Delivers 0x50. Same result! Hmm \u2014 for err* rows, swapping fallback/re-route order gives the same outcome.\n\nWhat about resid rows with SFRZ set? sfu1h: SFRZ set (second fault SFU1 114, non-trap). Re-route first: vector = ERROR (10), fatal printed. Then fallback: 10 < 128 && !implemented(ERROR)? implemented \u2192 no fallback. Delivers to 0x50. But sfu1h expects HALT naming 0x072 \u2192 caught? Wait \u2014 but sfu1h expects the re-route... hold on. sfu1h: DIVU first fault SFU1? Let me re-read: \"sfu1h, DIVU_R2_R3_R0, {\"r3\": 0x10, \"r5\": 0x60, \"r4\": 0x10}, V_SFU1, 0x10004, PSR_RESET, [DIVU_R6_R5_R4]  # handler at 0x40: a legal divu with SFD1 set -> SFU1 (114) -> fallback HALT\". Hmm wait \u2014 handler at 0x40? \"handler at 0x40\" \u2014 the handler words planted at... the comment says \"handler at 0x40\" but earlier \"the row's handler words at `8 * handler_at` (default 0x40)\". Default handler_at is... 0x40/8 = 8? So default handler planted at vector 8's entry (0x40). Hmm wait, that doesn't sound right either. \"the row's handler words at `8 * handler_at` (default 0x40)\" \u2014 so handler_at default = 8, and handler words planted at 8*8 = 0x40. Interesting: so the handler is planted at the entry of vector 8 (0x40), NOT at the row's expected vector entry. Wait, that seems odd. Let me re-read premise 5: \"The probe plants `br .` at every entry of every vector in VECTORS (now incl. 11 and 127), the row's handler words at `8 * handler_at` (default 0x40), a breakpoint at the expected entry, then `continue`\".\n\nHmm \u2014 so handler words go at 8*handler_at with default 0x40?? That means handler_at defaults to... wait \"8 * handler_at (default 0x40)\" \u2014 the default RESULT is 0x40, so handler_at default = 8. So by default the handler is planted at vector 8's entry (0x40). But then when the exception delivers to the row's expected vector (e.g., DIV0 = 0x40?)... Hmm wait. What is V_DIV0? Let me compute: trapfrozen8 comment: \"first fault add 7fffffff+1 (handler planted at 0x48), handler tb1 0,r3,8: the frozen trap naming DIV0 delivers to 0x40\". So DIV0's entry is 0x40 \u2192 DIV0 = vector 8? Wait 8*8=0x40. Hmm, but then OVF = 9 (0x48). So in this m88k encoding: vector 7 = trapfrozen's class (0x38)... 0x38/8 = 7. DIV0 = 8? Hmm, that's unusual \u2014 in real m88k, exceptions: 1 misalign?... doesn't matter, this fork's mapping is inlined: entries 0x38 (7), 0x40 (8=DIV0), 0x48 (9=OVF), 0x50 (10=ERROR), 0x58 (11), 0x190 (50), 0x3f8 (127), 0x390 (114=SFU1: 8*114=0x390), 0x400 (128=USER0).\n\nWait, but the docstring earlier said \"MISALIGNED error, OVERFLOW errovf, DIV0 errdiv0\" and classes {1..10}: priv=3 (0x30: \"privsuptb ... 0x30\"), MISALIGN=1 (0x08?), UNIMPL=2 (0x10?), PRIV=3 (0x30 \u2014 privsuptb expects 0x30? \"tb1 0,r3,6 (f003d806) in SUPERVISOR mode: the trap route into PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION ... 0x30\"). Hmm wait: privsuptb is tb1 naming vector 6 \u2192 PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION \u2192 0x30 \u2192 0x30/8 = 6? That doesn't work: 6*8=0x30. Hmm so PRIVILEGE = 6?? But then \"DIV0 = 8\"... Let me recompute: 0x30/8 = 6. So the tb1 naming 6 in supervisor delivers to PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION at 0x30. So vector 6 = PRIVILEGE? Hmm, but wait \u2014 \"tb1 0,r3,6 (f003d806) in SUPERVISOR mode: the trap route into PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION.\" Hmm, this says the trap naming 6 routes into PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION. Hmm, that's odd \u2014 naming vector 6 gives PRIVILEGE? That must mean: a tb1 naming a vector < 128 in supervisor... no wait. Hmm, actually maybe: the tb1 instruction with vector 6 \u2014 the vector field names where to go, but... \"the trap route into PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION\" \u2014 hmm. Actually maybe in this emulator, tb1 naming 6... no wait, privtb: \"privtb is NOT that route -- the user-mode filter in X(tb0) raises PRIVILEGE with is_trap == 0\". So privtb: user-mode tb0 \u2192 PRIVILEGE raised (is_trap=0). privsuptb: SUPERVISOR tb1 naming 6 \u2192 routes into PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION. Hmm, why would a supervisor trap name 6 and get PRIVILEGE? Maybe vector 6 IS the PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION vector, and a tb1 naming it just delivers there (as a trap, is_trap=1). So PRIVILEGE = 6, entry 0x30. OK so classes: MISALIGN=1? UNIMPL=2? Hmm the docstring: \"MISALIGNED error, OVERFLOW errovf, DIV0 errdiv0, PRIVILEGE errpriv, UNIMPLEMENTED errunimpl; BOUNDS and ERROR have no non-trap route\". And trapfrozen(4/5/6/9): \"the same shape naming 4, 5, 6, 9\" \u2014 wait trapfrozen4/5/6 name vectors 4, 5, 6, 9?? And trapfrozen names 7, trapfrozen8 names 8 (DIV0: \"the frozen trap naming DIV0 delivers to 0x40\" \u2014 wait, \"handler tb1 0,r3,8: the frozen trap naming DIV0 delivers to 0x40\". Hmm, TB1_0_R3_8 names vector 8. \"the frozen trap naming DIV0\" \u2014 so vector 8 = DIV0. But then trapfrozen9: \"frozen tb1 naming 9 -> 0x48\" and TB1_0_R3_9 with V_OVF... and the trapfrozen9 row's first fault is V_OVF: (\"trapfrozen9\", [DIVU_R2_R3_R0], {\"r3\": 0x11}, V_OVF, ...) \u2014 DIVU by 0x11? Hmm, DIVU_R2_R3_R0 divides r2/r3? With r3=0x11... hmm, that's not zero. Hmm wait \u2014 sfu1h has DIVU_R2_R3_R0 with r3=0x10, \"a legal divu with SFD1 set -> SFU1\". So DIVU_R2_R3_R0 with nonzero divisor is legal unless SFD1 set. And trapfrozen9: [DIVU_R2_R3_R0], {\"r3\": 0x11}, V_OVF \u2014 hmm, first fault OVF via DIVU? Maybe DIVU_R2_R3_R0 is r2/r3 and with some flags raises OVF? Or maybe the encoding... whatever. The first fault is OVF (9), handler tb1 names 9 \u2192 frozen trap naming 9 \u2192 delivered to 0x48 (9's own vector). OK.\n\nSo vectors: 7 = ? (trapfrozen names 7, delivers 0x38). Classes per docstring: {class} list \"MISALIGNED, OVERFLOW, DIV0, PRIVILEGE, UNIMPLEMENTED, BOUNDS, ERROR\" and numbers: trapfrozen4\u21924, 5\u21925, 6\u21926, 7\u21927, 8\u2192DIV0, 9\u2192OVF, 10\u2192ERROR. And privsuptb names 6 \u2192 PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION \u2192 0x30?? Wait, that contradicts: 6*8=0x30, and privsuptb \"tb1 0,r3,6 ... the trap route into PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION ... 0x30\". Hmm so vector 6 = PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION. But then trapfrozen6 = \"the same shape naming 6\" = a frozen trap naming PRIVILEGE(6) \u2192 delivers 0x30 with frozen shadows. And the class list order \"MISALIGNED error, OVERFLOW errovf, DIV0 errdiv0\" \u2014 these are err* rows: first fault MISALIGNED? no wait: \"a class-conditioned SFRZ re-route ({SFRZ set, !is_trap} x {class}: MISALIGNED error, OVERFLOW errovf, DIV0 errdiv0...)\". Hmm \u2014 so row \"error\": second fault is MISALIGNED? Or first fault MISALIGNED, second fault raises... Row \"error\" = second fault with SFRZ set of class MISALIGNED?? and it gets re-routed to ERROR (0x50). Hmm, actually \"error\" row probably: first fault something, handler faults MISALIGNED again with SFRZ set \u2192 re-route \u2192 0x50. The class label in the closure list is the class of the SECOND fault (the one re-routed). So: error=MISALIGNED-class second fault, errovf=OVERFLOW, errdiv0=DIV0, errpriv=PRIVILEGE, errunimpl=UNIMPLEMENTED. And trapfrozen rows name classes: trapfrozen(7)=? trapfrozen4, 5, 6, 8(DIV0), 9(OVF), 10(ERROR), 128(user trap). The class list for traps: \"trapfrozen (7), trapfrozen4/5/6/8/9/10/128\". So classes named by traps: 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 128. Classes 1..3 (MISALIGN? UNIMPL? ...). Hmm: 6=PRIVILEGE (from privsuptb). What are 1-5, 7? From the class list: MISALIGNED, OVERFLOW, DIV0, PRIVILEGE, UNIMPLEMENTED, BOUNDS, ERROR = 7 classes for vectors... 4,5,6,7,8,9,10: BOUNDS=4? UNIMPL=5? PRIVILEGE=6, ?=7, DIV0=8, OVF=9, ERROR=10. And 7 might be MISALIGNED? And 1..3: hardware events (m88kexchwtraps: \"vectors 0..3's switch-1 arms are unrowed: the disclosed hardware-event class\"). Hmm wait \u2014 vectors 0..3 are hardware events, so classes 4..10 are the seven: {4,5,6,7,8,9,10} = {MISALIGNED, BOUNDS?, UNIMPL?, PRIVILEGE, ?, DIV0, OVF, ERROR}. Fine \u2014 details don't matter. The point: rows cover classes 4..10 via traps (trapfrozen*), classes via non-trap err* rows: MISALIGNED (error), OVF (errovf), DIV0 (errdiv0), PRIVILEGE (errpriv), UNIMPL (errunimpl). What about BOUNDS (4?) and ERROR (10)? \"BOUNDS and ERROR have no non-trap route\" \u2014 so no err* row for BOUNDS. BOUNDS as non-trap: no route (chk instructions? apparently in this model BOUNDS has no non-trap route \u2014 hmm, chk? whatever, disclosed/stated). So {SFRZ, !is_trap} x {class} covers 5 of 7 classes; BOUNDS/ERROR no non-trap route \u2014 disclosed as a fact, not a gap.\n\nOK. Now the (d) enumeration hunt. Let me think hard about possible clean sweeps:\n\n**Candidate A: switch 1's ERROR-arm `implemented` and trapfrozen10.** M_ERRORFRZOFF caught by trapfrozen10. What about the reverse: ERROR arm conditioned on something else no row distinguishes? E.g., `implemented = !is_trap` at ERROR: disclosed (\"equivalent today, m88ktbnd\"). At BOUNDS likewise disclosed.\n\n**Candidate B: the fallback predicate conditioned on a PSR bit not settable?** resid50ind (IND clear), resid50c (C set), usfu1 (user mode), sfu1h (SFRZ set) \u2014 \"every settable PSR bit now has both sides at the fallback; MXM and BO cannot be set by stcr without stopping the cpu -- disclosed.\" So PSR-bit conditioning of the fallback is closed for settable bits, disclosed for MXM/BO. What about SFD1 at the fallback? sfu1h has SFD1 set (SFRZ set too \u2014 sfu1h: first fault SFU1 sets SFRZ; handler divu with SFD1 set \u2192 SFU1 again... wait, sfu1h's second fault: \"a legal divu with SFD1 set -> SFU1 (114) -> fallback HALT\". Hmm \u2014 sfu1h: {\"r3\": 0x10, \"r5\": 0x60, \"r4\": 0x10}, [DIVU_R2_R3_R0], V_SFU1, handler [DIVU_R6_R5_R4]. First fault: DIVU_R2_R3_R0 (r2/r3 with r3=0x10?) \u2192 SFU1? Why? SFD1 must be set... PSR_RESET = 0x80000002 = MODE | IND. SFD1 (0x8) not set at reset. Hmm \u2014 \"DIVU_R2_R3_R0\" with r3=0x10, and r5=0x60, r4=0x10. Hmm. The first fault is SFU1 (V_SFU1=114) per the row's vector field. Maybe DIVU raises SFU1 when... the instruction is divu r2,r3,r0 \u2014 dividing by r0 (r0=0 always)? Divisor r0 = 0! \"divu tests SFD1 before divisor == 0\" \u2014 so divu with SFD1 CLEAR and divisor 0 \u2192 DIV0; with SFD1 SET \u2192 SFU1 first. But at reset SFD1 is clear... hmm, then divu r2,r3,r0 \u2192 divisor r0=0 \u2192 DIV0 not SFU1. Hmm, wait \u2014 maybe DIVU_R2_R3_R0 means something else. Ugh, encodings: DIVU_R6_R5_R4 used in sfu1h's handler: \"a legal divu with SFD1 set -> SFU1 (114)\". The handler runs with PSR after first fault: exception PSR = PSR_EXC = 0x8000000b = MODE|SFD1|IND|SFRZ? 0xb = 1011 = SFD1(8)|IND(2)|SFRZ(1). YES: PSR_EXC = 0x8000000b sets SFD1 and SFRZ! So after any exception, SFD1 and SFRZ are set. That's why sfu1h's handler divu raises SFU1 (SFD1 set) \u2192 second fault SFU1 with SFRZ set \u2192 re-route?? Wait \u2014 but sfu1h expects HALT (fallback), not re-route delivery!\n\nHold on. sfu1h: first fault DIVU raising SFU1 (vector 114)? For the first fault to be SFU1, SFD1 must be set before... but PSR_RESET=0x80000002 has SFD1 clear. Hmm. Let me re-read: \"sfu1h, [DIVU_R2_R3_R0], {\"r3\": 0x10, \"r5\": 0x60, \"r4\": 0x10}, V_SFU1, 0x10004, PSR_RESET, [DIVU_R6_R5_R4] # handler at 0x40: a legal divu with SFD1 set -> SFU1 (114) -> fallback HALT\".\n\nHmm wait \u2014 \"handler at 0x40\"?? The comment says \"handler at 0x40\". But 0x40 is DIV0's entry. Hmm, and the diff hunk earlier: \"# [, handler_at: the vector whose entry the handler is planted at -- only trapfrozen8 sets it, to OVF (0x48), because its frozen\". So handler_at default = 8 \u2192 0x40. So sfu1h's handler is planted at vector 8's entry (0x40), the default. Hmm, that's weird \u2014 why plant handlers at 0x40 by default? OH WAIT. I bet the mechanism is: the first fault delivers to its vector entry; the handler words are planted AT THE DELIVERED ENTRY so the handler runs there. So handler words planted at 8*handler_at = the FIRST fault's vector entry. For sfu1h: first fault SFU1 (114) \u2192 delivered to 0x390? But handler at 0x40?? That contradicts... unless handler_at for sfu1h is different. Hmm wait, sfu1h's row: the 7th field is [DIVU_R6_R5_R4] (handler words) and there's no handler_at \u2192 default 8 \u2192 0x40. But sfu1h's first fault is SFU1=114 \u2192 entry 0x390. If the handler were planted at 0x40, the first fault would deliver to 0x390, where `br .` is planted, and the handler at 0x40 would never run... unless SFU1's first fault goes to the FALLBACK (vector 114 \u2265 128? no, 114 < 128...).\n\nHmm wait. Let me reconsider. SFU1 = 114. The fallback: `vector < 128 && !implemented`. Is SFU1 (114) implemented in switch 1? The class switch covers vectors... SFU1=114 \u2014 is there a class for it? The classes are 4..10 plus user traps 128+. SFU1 114: NOT in the class switch \u2192 not implemented \u2192 fallback \u2192 HALT. So a FIRST-exception SFU1 goes to the fallback and HALTs \u2014 that's the disclosed \"first-exception SFU1 (m88kexcsfu1)\". So sfu1h's FIRST fault can't be SFU1... but the row's vector field says V_SFU1!\n\nHmm wait, let me re-read sfu1h: maybe the first fault is DIV0 (not SFU1). \"[DIVU_R2_R3_R0], {\"r3\": 0x10, \"r5\": 0x60, \"r4\": 0x10}, V_SFU1, 0x10004, PSR_RESET\". Hmm, the vector field V_SFU1=114. Hmm. But the docstring: \"sfu1h (114: its nested SFU1 halts at the fallback)\". And EXPECT_VECTOR[\"sfu1h\"] = 0x072 (114). And EXPECT_RESULT[\"sfu1h\"] = HALT. So sfu1h's expected outcome: the FALLBACK fires naming 114. So the SECOND fault is SFU1 (114) \u2192 fallback HALT naming 0x072. Then the FIRST fault must be something that delivers normally, with the handler planted at the first fault's entry. The handler words [DIVU_R6_R5_R4] planted at 8*handler_at. For the handler to run, handler_at = the FIRST fault's vector. But the row's vector field is V_SFU1 (114)... \n\nHmm, wait. Maybe the row's \"vector\" field is the EXPECTED second-fault vector, and handler_at defaults to... Hmm, the columns: \"name, program words after MARK, regs, vector, fault_pc, epsr, handler words at the div0 entry (None = br .)\". \"handler words at the div0 entry\" \u2014 the DIV0 entry! 0x40 = 8*8 = DIV0's entry. So the handler words are planted at the DIV0 entry (0x40) by default. So the FIRST fault is DIV0 (divide by zero \u2192 vector 8 \u2192 0x40), the handler runs there. For sfu1h: first fault = DIV0? But then why does the row name V_SFU1=114 in the vector field... because the EXPECTED outcome is about the second fault: the handler's divu raises SFU1 (SFD1 now set by PSR_EXC) \u2192 SFU1 114 \u2192 not implemented \u2192 fallback \u2192 HALT naming 0x072. And the breakpoint/expected entry... but wait, the probe plants \"a breakpoint at the expected entry\" \u2014 for sfu1h the expected result is HALT, so no delivery expected. The vector field (V_SFU1) is used for EXPECT_VECTOR (the diagnostic must name 114) and maybe for the br . planting.\n\nHmm, but hold on: sfu1h's first fault: DIVU_R2_R3_R0 with r3=0x10?? Divide r2 by r3=0x10 \u2014 that's legal (nonzero divisor)! Hmm. Unless DIVU_R2_R3_R0 is divu r2, r3, r0 \u2192 divisor r0 = 0 \u2192 DIV0. The operand order in the mnemonic \"DIVU_R2_R3_R0\" = divu r2, r3, r0? MC88k divu: divu rD, rS1, rS2 \u2014 divides rS1 by rS2? Actually divu.<sf> rD,rS1,rS2 computes rS1/rS2 into rD. Hmm, so divu r2, r3, r0: r3/r0 \u2192 divide by r0 (=0) \u2192 DIV0! Yes. So DIVU_R2_R3_R0 always divides by zero \u2192 DIV0 first fault. And sfu1h's regs set r5=0x60, r4=0x10 for the HANDLER's divu (DIVU_R6_R5_R4: r6 = r5/r4 = 0x60/0x10 = 6, legal, but SFD1 set \u2192 SFU1). Consistent! And trapfrozen9: [DIVU_R2_R3_R0] with r3=0x11 \u2014 hmm, but divu r2,r3,r0 divides by r0=0 regardless of r3... the r3=0x11 is for the handler? trapfrozen9's handler is [TB1_0_R3_9] which doesn't use r3's value... hmm, whatever, maybe r3 is a red herring for some rows. Actually wait, for trapfrozen9 the first fault must be OVF (V_OVF) per the row's vector field: (\"trapfrozen9\", [DIVU_R2_R3_R0], {\"r3\": 0x11}, V_OVF, ...). Hmm \u2014 first fault from DIVU_R2_R3_R0 is DIV0 (divide by zero), not OVF... unless... hmm. Wait, maybe I have the operand order wrong: divu rD, rS1, rS2 \u2192 maybe DIVU_R2_R3_R0 = divu r2, r3, r0 meaning rD=r2, rS1=r3, rS2=r0 \u2192 r3/r0. Divisor r0. Hmm same thing.\n\nHmm, unless the vector field is not the first fault's vector but the EXPECTED delivery vector for DELIVER rows / expected fallback vector for HALT rows. For trapfrozen9: expected DELIVER to 0x48 (OVF=9)... wait no, trapfrozen9's frozen trap names 9 \u2192 delivers 0x48 = 9's entry. And the row's vector field V_OVF (9) \u2014 matches the EXPECTED final delivery. And trapfrozen8: vector field V_DIV0 (8); expected delivery 0x40 (8's entry) \u2014 the frozen trap names 8 \u2192 0x40. YES: the vector field = the row's expected FINAL delivery vector (for DELIVER rows) or the fallback's named vector (for HALT rows, via EXPECT_VECTOR). And the FIRST fault is whatever the program words raise: trapfrozen8's program words [ADD_R2_R3_R4] with r3=0x7fffffff, r4=1 \u2192 OVF! (\"first fault add 7fffffff+1\"). The handler words [TB1_0_R3_8] planted at handler_at=V_OVF (0x48) \u2014 the OVF entry \u2014 so the first fault (OVF) delivers to 0x48 and runs the handler there. That's why trapfrozen8 sets handler_at to OVF. For rows whose first fault is DIV0 (the default), handler_at defaults to 8 (0x40, the DIV0 entry). \n\nSo: trapfrozen9's first fault: [DIVU_R2_R3_R0] with r3=0x11 \u2192 hmm, divu r2, r3, r0 \u2192 r3/r0 = 0x11/0 \u2192 DIV0! But the row says V_OVF... wait no, I just decided the vector field is the EXPECTED FINAL vector. trapfrozen9: frozen trap names 9 \u2192 expected deliver 0x48 \u2192 V_OVF=9. \u2713. And the first fault is DIV0 (from divu by r0) \u2192 handler planted at default 0x40 (DIV0 entry) \u2192 handler TB1_0_R3_9 runs \u2192 frozen trap naming 9 \u2192 0x48. \u2713\u2713. And trapfrozen128: [DIVU_R2_R3_R0] \u2192 DIV0 first fault \u2192 handler [TB1_0_R3_128] at 0x40 \u2192 frozen trap naming 128 \u2192 exempted \u2192 0x400. \u2713. trapfrozen10: same \u2192 frozen trap naming 10 \u2192 0x50. \u2713. And r3=0x11 for trapfrozen9/10/128: irrelevant (divide by r0). Hmm, or maybe rD=r2 gets some value... the BADREGS assert wants r2=0xdeadbeef for... hmm, want dict: r2=0xdeadbeef. Hmm, that's for the frozen-shadow rows? \"want = dict(sxip=..., psr=..., epsr=epsr_exp, r2=0xdeadbeef)\" \u2014 r2 expected to be 0xdeadbeef?? That seems like a sentinel... whatever, not essential.\n\nWait, actually there's a subtlety about sfu1h again: first fault DIV0 \u2192 delivered to 0x40 (DIV0 entry, where handler [DIVU_R6_R5_R4] is planted) \u2192 handler divu r6,r5,r4 = 0x60/0x10 legal BUT SFD1 set (PSR_EXC = 0x8000000b) \u2192 SFU1 (114) \u2192 second fault. SFRZ also set \u2192 re-route check: is_trap? divu is not a trap \u2192 is_trap=0 \u2192 re-route fires! vector = ERROR, fatal printed, deliver 0x50?!?! But sfu1h expects HALT (fallback naming 114)!!! \n\nWAIT. That's a contradiction... unless the fallback fires BEFORE the re-route (premise 2's order: switch 1 \u2192 fallback \u2192 re-route). SFU1 (114): switch 1 \u2192 not implemented (no class for 114); fallback: vector < 128 && !implemented \u2192 114 < 128 \u2713, !implemented \u2713 \u2192 fallback fires \u2192 print + stop + RETURN. The re-route never reached. So sfu1h \u2192 HALT naming 0x072. \u2713. GOOD \u2014 the order matters and premise 2's order (fallback BEFORE re-route) is what makes sfu1h HALT. And this is exactly why sfu1h pins the ORDER: a mutant that swaps the order (re-route before fallback) would send sfu1h's second fault to the re-route \u2192 0x50 + fatal line \u2192 sfu1h would... the probe expects HALT naming 114; instead it gets DELIVER at 0x50 \u2192 red row. So the order IS pinned by sfu1h. Hmm interesting \u2014 was that in the denominator? The denominator list: \"the fallback -- predicate / off / stop / near bound / far bound / outside / user mode / IND / C\". Order of fallback vs re-route... M_REVERT 3/22? Not obviously. Hmm. Actually, is the order pinned? sfu1h: second fault SFU1, SFRZ set, non-trap. If re-route came first: vector = ERROR (10) \u2192 then fallback check: 10 < 128 && !implemented(ERROR is implemented) \u2192 no fallback \u2192 deliver 0x50, fatal printed, shadows frozen. sfu1h expects HALT \u2192 probe would see... the machine delivers to 0x50 (br . planted) and the breakpoint at expected entry (0x390? or wherever) never fires. Result classification: the machine stops? It sits at br . at 0x50 \u2014 actually wait, if it delivers to 0x50 and executes br . forever, the probe's continue... hmm, how does the probe classify? HALT = fallback diagnostic + \"All machines stopped\". Under the swap mutant, neither appears; the probe would time out \u2192 STOP? Red row regardless (expected HALT, got STOP/whatever). So sfu1h pins the fallback-before-re-route order for the SFU1 case. And err* rows pin it too? err* rows: second fault class X (implemented) with SFRZ set. Under order swap: re-route fires first \u2192 vector=ERROR, fatal \u2192 fallback: ERROR implemented \u2192 no fallback \u2192 deliver 0x50. Same as correct! So err* rows do NOT distinguish order. Only sfu1h does (a second fault whose class is NOT implemented, with SFRZ set). OK so order is pinned by sfu1h. Good.\n\nHmm wait, actually let me double-check the correct behavior of err* rows w.r.t. order once more. Correct order: switch 1 (class X implemented) \u2192 fallback: vector < 128 && !implemented \u2192 false (implemented) \u2192 no fallback \u2192 re-route: SFRZ && !is_trap \u2192 vector = ERROR, fatal \u2192 common path \u2192 deliver 0x50. Yes. So err* rows: 0x50 + fatal line. \u2713 REFATAL_EXPECT True. \u2713.\n\n**Now \u2014 the refatal channel and HALT rows.** REFATAL_EXPECT includes only err* (True) and trapfrozen* (False). What about sfu1h? sfu1h's second fault: fallback fires, return BEFORE the re-route \u2192 no fatal line. Under a MUTANT that re-routes before the fallback (order swap), sfu1h would see the fatal line. But REFATAL_EXPECT doesn't include sfu1h \u2192 the channel doesn't check sfu1h's absence. But sfu1h's HALT expectation itself catches the swap (no fallback diagnostic \u2192 not HALT). Fine.\n\nWhat about resid50/resid127/resid11/resid50ind/resid50c/usfu1 \u2014 their PSR: resid rows run at PSR_RESET (0x80000002, SFRZ clear) \u2192 no re-route anyway. usfu1: PSR_USERSFD1 = 0x0000000a = SFD1|IND (user, SFD1) \u2014 SFRZ clear \u2192 no re-route. OK.\n\n**Now the DEEP hunt for a clean-sweep mutant in the (d) enumeration.**\n\nLet me list the composite's guarded predicates and think about each:\n\n1. Switch 1: `switch (vector)` mapping to classes, setting `implemented`. Edited lines: class labels. Mutants: relabel a class (e.g., vector 9's arm says OVF \u2192 change label to... the label determines which TAIL arm runs and what `implemented` is? Hmm, actually what does switch 1 DO per class? \"switch 1 (class -> `implemented`)\". So switch 1 sets implemented per class. The tail switch \"labels per class\" \u2014 the tail switch does per-class work (e.g., setting EXIP for traps vs faults, setting PSR bits?). Mutants: \n   - A class arm's `implemented = 1` \u2192 `= 0`: e.g., make OVF unimplemented \u2192 ovf rows \u2192 fallback HALT instead of DELIVER \u2192 caught (M_REVERT-ish covers polarity generally: 3/22).\n   - A class arm conditioned on something: covered per class? {class} x {MODE} closure (M_OVFSUPONLY etc.), ERROR-under-SFRZ (M_ERRORFRZOFF, now caught by trapfrozen10). What about {class} x {SFD1}? E.g., `implemented = !(PSR & SFD1)` on some class arm: which row has SFD1 set AND raises a class? Rows with SFD1 set: any row after a first fault (PSR_EXC sets SFD1): err* rows (second faults, SFD1 set via PSR_EXC), sfu1h (handler divu raises SFU1 \u2014 not a class), usfu1 (SFD1 set from the start, raises SFU1 \u2014 not a class), trapfrozen rows' handlers raise traps (SFD1 set: PSR_EXC): e.g., trapfrozen (7): frozen trap naming 7 \u2014 a trap with SFD1 set! If the class-7 arm said `implemented = !(PSR & SFD1)`, the trap naming 7 would go: switch 1 \u2192 not implemented \u2192 fallback (7 < 128) \u2192 HALT instead of DELIVER 0x38 \u2192 caught by trapfrozen. Similarly all trapfrozen* rows have SFD1 set (post-exception PSR). So {class} x {SFD1} at switch 1: for classes reachable post-exception (all trapfrozen classes 4..10, 128), pinned by the trapfrozen rows' DELIVER expectations. For err* classes: SFD1 set too, pinned. What about SFD1 CLEAR + class? First-fault rows (div0, ovf, etc.) have SFD1 clear \u2014 pinned by their DELIVERs. So {class} x {SFD1} both sides pinned. Hmm OK.\n   - {class} x {C}: rows with C set: ovfc (first fault OVF with C set \u2014 PSR_CARRY = 0x10000000), resid50c (C set, fallback row). A class arm conditioned on C: e.g., OVF arm `implemented = !(PSR & C)` \u2192 ovfc (C set, OVF fault) \u2192 fallback \u2192 HALT instead of DELIVER \u2192 caught. resid50c pins the fallback's C-conditioning. What about IND: indclr (IND clear, OVF fault) + resid50ind. MODE: user rows. SFRZ: sfu1h (SFRZ set... but sfu1h's class isn't implemented \u2014 SFU1 isn't a class). Hmm: {class} x {SFRZ} at switch 1 for IMPLEMENTED classes: err* rows have SFRZ set + class \u2192 M_ERRORFRZOFF is the ERROR-arm version, caught by trapfrozen10. For OTHER class arms conditioned on SFRZ (e.g., `implemented = !(PSR & SFRZ)` on the OVF arm): which rows have SFRZ set and raise an implemented class? err* rows! errovf: SFRZ set, OVF second fault \u2192 if OVF arm says implemented = !SFRZ \u2192 not implemented \u2192 fallback (9 < 128) \u2192 HALT instead of DELIVER 0x50 \u2192 caught by errovf. \u2713 And trapfrozen rows: SFRZ set + trap naming class \u2192 caught. So per-class SFRZ-conditioning of `implemented` is pinned for classes with err*/trapfrozen rows. BOUNDS: no non-trap route; trapfrozen4/5/6 include BOUNDS (whichever of 4/5/6 is BOUNDS)... \"the same shape naming 4, 5, 6, 9\" and classes: BOUNDS and ERROR \"have no user-mode route\", \"BOUNDS and ERROR have no non-trap route\". So BOUNDS is one of 4/5/6/7. trapfrozen(7) names 7; trapfrozen4/5/6 name 4/5/6. So classes 4,5,6,7 = the remaining four of {MISALIGNED, UNIMPL, PRIVILEGE, BOUNDS} minus... wait we have 7 classes for 4..10: MISALIGNED, UNIMPL, PRIVILEGE, BOUNDS, DIV0(8), OVF(9), ERROR(10) \u2192 4=MISALIGN? 5=UNIMPL? 6=PRIVILEGE (from privsuptb: naming 6 \u2192 PRIVILEGE), 7=BOUNDS? Something like that. Anyway every class 4..10 has a trapfrozen row (4,5,6,7,8,9,10) \u2014 so trap-route SFRZ-conditioning per class: pinned. Non-trap SFRZ per class: err* covers MISALIGNED, OVF, DIV0, PRIVILEGE, UNIMPL; BOUNDS/ERROR no non-trap route \u2014 can't be pinned, disclosed as no-route (that's a hardware fact, fine).\n\n2. The fallback predicate `vector < 128 && !implemented`:\n   - Bound: resid11 (near), resid127 (far), usertb (128 outside). `<` vs `<=`: M_FALLBACKLE caught by usertb. `>= 12`: caught by resid11. What about the bound tested against a DIFFERENT variable \u2014 operand swap \"drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read\"? e.g., `vector < M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START` where the constant is 128 \u2014 a swap to another constant? The enumeration says operand swaps from fields the guarded bodies read. The bodies read: vector, implemented, PSR bits, is_trap. A mutant `vector < 128` \u2192 `vector <= 127`: equivalent (vector is integer). Fine.\n   - `!implemented` polarity: M_REVERT caught.\n   - PSR-bit conditioning: IND (resid50ind), C (resid50c), MODE (usfu1, M_FBSUPONLY), SFRZ (sfu1h \u2014 wait, does sfu1h pin SFRZ-conditioning of the fallback? sfu1h: SFRZ set, fallback fires. A mutant `&& !(PSR & SFRZ)` on the fallback \u2192 sfu1h's second fault skips the fallback \u2192 falls through to the re-route \u2192 0x50 + fatal \u2192 sfu1h red (expected HALT). \u2713 caught. And SFRZ-clear fallback rows (resid50 etc.) pin the other side. SFD1: rows with SFD1 set at the fallback: sfu1h (SFD1 set via PSR_EXC) and usfu1 (SFD1 set deliberately). Both are fallback rows \u2192 mutant `&& !(PSR & SFD1)` \u2192 both red. SFD1-clear at fallback: resid50/127/11/50ind/50c (PSR_RESET/INDCLR/CARRY \u2014 SFD1 clear) \u2713 both sides.\n   - is_trap conditioning of the fallback: `&& !is_trap`? Which fallback rows are traps? resid rows use tb1 \u2014 TB1 = trap! resid50: [TB1_0_R3_50] \u2014 tb1 \u2192 is_trap=1! So resid50/127/11/50ind/50c/usertb are TRAP rows. sfu1h/usfu1 are non-trap. A mutant `&& !is_trap` on the fallback \u2192 resid* rows skip fallback \u2192 deliver to... vector 50 has br . planted (no handler) \u2192 the machine runs br . at 0x190 forever? Result: not HALT \u2192 red. \u2713 caught. And the non-trap side: sfu1h/usfu1 \u2713. Hmm wait, but actually there's something interesting: the FALLBACK rows via tb1 are traps with vector < 128 \u2014 and premise 4: \"a user tb1 naming a vector < 128 raises PRIVILEGE\" \u2014 but these rows run SUPERVISOR (PSR_RESET has MODE set) \u2192 the tb1 delivers the trap to vector 50 \u2192 switch 1: class for 50? Not implemented \u2192 fallback. OK so a supervisor tb1 naming an unimplemented vector \u2192 the trap \"delivers\" to the fallback. Consistent with rows.\n\n3. The re-route `if (PSR & SFRZ) { if (!is_trap) {...} update_shadow_regs = false; }`:\n   - `PSR & SFRZ` polarity/conditioning: M_SFRZ_EPSR_SWAP? (23/29, agy) \u2014 source swap (EPSR vs PSR?) caught. Per-class trap/non-trap: tables 5, 7, 10. is_trap polarity: M_SFRZTRAP caught (29/35 \u2192 on v10 presumably still caught by trapfrozen rows: polarity flip \u2192 trapfrozen rows re-routed \u2192 refatal/delivery changes).\n   - What about `is_trap` for the user trap 128: M_SFRZTRAPUSER caught by trapfrozen128.\n   - vector == ERROR for traps: M_SFRZTRAP10 caught by trapfrozen10 refatal.\n   - What about the re-route conditioned on MODE? e.g., `if ((PSR & SFRZ) && (PSR & MODE))` \u2014 rows with SFRZ set in USER mode: umisalign? No \u2014 umisalign is a first fault (SFRZ clear). User + SFRZ set: errunimpl (\"the handler drops to user mode keeping SFRZ\")! errunimpl: user mode, SFRZ set, non-trap \u2192 re-route must fire \u2192 refatal True expected. A mutant adding `&& (PSR & MODE)` \u2192 errunimpl's re-route suppressed \u2192 second fault UNIMPL (implemented class) \u2192 no fallback \u2192 no re-route \u2192 delivers to UNIMPL's own vector (0x10?) instead of 0x50 \u2192 errunimpl red (expected 0x50 + line). \u2713 caught. And the supervisor side: err* others \u2713.\n   - update_shadow_regs placement: covered.\n\n4. Tail switch labels: per-class tail arms. Rows deliver per class \u2192 tail arm behavior pinned via register asserts (sxip/snip/sfip/psr/epsr). Mutants M_TAILLE etc. caught. Vectors 0..3 tail arms: disclosed (m88kexchwtraps). What about the tail switch for vector 128 (user trap)? usertb/trapfrozen128 deliver 0x400 \u2014 the tail arm for user traps (return address etc.) pinned by BADREGS asserts. \u2713.\n\nHmm. So within the stated enumeration, the measured mutants all show 40/41 \u2014 none sweep. The question asks ME to determine if a clean-sweep mutant EXISTS. Let me think harder about specific corners:\n\n**Corner A: {SFRZ set, is_trap} for vectors 0..3.** Disclosed (hardware-event tails, m88kexchwtraps). Not a breach.\n\n**Corner B: switch 1's ERROR arm and trapfrozen10's DELIVER vs HALT.** trapfrozen10 pins `implemented` for ERROR under SFRZ (M_ERRORFRZOFF \u2192 HALT 0x00a \u2192 red). But what about the ERROR arm's `implemented` under... other conditions already covered.\n\n**Corner C: the refatal channel's coverage of `error` row.** The `error` row: second fault MISALIGNED with SFRZ set \u2192 re-route \u2192 vector = ERROR (10) \u2192 common path \u2192 tail switch: class now... wait \u2014 after the re-route sets vector = ERROR, the tail switch dispatches on the (new) vector 10 \u2192 ERROR tail arm? Or on the original class? The re-route changes `vector` to ERROR before the common path and tail switch. So the tail arm is ERROR's. The want-dict asserts for error row expect sxip etc. per the ERROR class? Fine.\n\nHmm \u2014 here's a thought: **what distinguishes the re-route's `vector = ERROR` from the exempt arm for a trap ALREADY naming ERROR (trapfrozen10)?** Both deliver 0x50, both frozen shadows; only the fatal line differs \u2014 pinned by refatal (trapfrozen10 False). \u2713.\n\n**Corner D: the fallback's diagnostic format.** `unimplemented vector=0x%03x` \u2014 EXPECT_VECTOR pins the named vector (0x00b, 0x032, 0x07f, 0x072). A mutant changing the FORMAT (e.g., %02x \u2192 prints 0x0b instead of 0x00b): EXPECT_VECTOR check \u2014 how is it checked? The probe checks the transcript for the vector named. If the mutant prints \"0xb\" the probe's expected-string match would fail \u2192 red. Is that in the (d) enumeration? Format strings aren't predicates/operand swaps \u2014 out of scope probably.\n\n**Corner E: HALT's \"All machines stopped.\" vs single-cpu stop.** M_NOSTOP caught.\n\n**Corner F: joint cell {MODE} x {SFRZ} x {class}** \u2014 disclosed for MISALIGNED/OVERFLOW/DIV0 (clause e). So a mutant conditioning the re-route or class on {MODE x SFRZ} jointly for those classes... disclosed. Hmm wait \u2014 but is it? The \"still disclosed\" list includes \"the {MODE} x {SFRZ} x {class} triple for MISALIGNED/OVERFLOW/DIV0 (clause e)\". So a clean-sweep mutant MIGHT exist there and it's DISCLOSED. E.g., re-route `if ((PSR & SFRZ) && (!is_trap) && ((PSR & MODE) || class not in {MISALIGN,OVF,DIV0}))` \u2014 some joint cell no row covers: rows with SFRZ set in USER mode raising MISALIGNED/OVF/DIV0? err* rows: errpriv/errunimpl drop to user mode (PRIVILEGE/UNIMPL classes). error/errovf/errdiv0 run supervisor. So {SFRZ, user, MISALIGNED-class} has no row \u2014 a mutant exempting MISALIGNED-class re-route in user mode sweeps... but DISCLOSED (clause e). OK.\n\nSo the disclosed list seems to cover the residual holes. Now \u2014 is there a hole NOT in the disclosed list? Let me think about the five new rows and what they DON'T pin:\n\n**resid50ind/resid50c pin IND/C at the fallback. But do they pin IND/C anywhere ELSE?** The common path's PSR write: PSR_EXC = 0x8000000b (MODE|SFD1|IND|SFRZ). M_NOIND caught (22/23) \u2014 by indclr (IND must be SET by the exception path; a write dropping IND \u2192 bad=psr). C preservation: ovfc PSR_WANT 0x9000000b \u2014 \"carry PRESERVED through the exception path\" \u2014 M_CARRYCLR caught (22/23). MODE: M_NOMODE (19/23). SFD1: M_NOSFD1. SFRZ: M_NOSFRZ (2/23). EPSR: M_NOEPSR/M_EPSRAFTER. So the common-path PSR writes are pinned.\n\nHmm, what about **the PSR written at the fallback**? The fallback stops the machine \u2014 no PSR write. HALT rows assert the diagnostic + stop. Fine.\n\n**What about the second-fault rows' EPSR?** epsr_exp \u2014 pinned per row.\n\nLet me now think about **operand swaps**: \"operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read\". The guarded bodies: switch-1 arms (set implemented), fallback body (print, stop, return), re-route body (vector = ERROR; fatal; update_shadow_regs = false), tail arms (write sxip/snip/sfip/psr/epsr, vector scaling). Swaps: e.g., in the tail, snip/sfip sources \u2014 M_SNIPX, M_DSLOTSWAP, M_SFIPNOTGT caught. Vector scaling: M_VEC4 (\u00d74 instead of \u00d78) caught 1/23. M_VEC7. Return address: M_TAILRETURN.\n\nHmm what about **the re-route's `vector = ERROR` swapped to `vector = <some other constant>`** \u2014 e.g., vector = 0x50/8 = 10... same thing. vector = DIV0 (8)? err* rows expect DELIVER at 0x50 (entry breakpoint at 8*10). If re-route sent to 8 \u2192 0x40 \u2192 red. Caught.\n\nWhat about **`update_shadow_regs = false` vs the trap exemption's shadow semantics** \u2014 premise 4: exempted traps delivered \"with frozen shadows\" \u2014 pinned by trapfrozen* BADREGS asserts (frozen shadow values in want dict: \"the shadows stay FROZEN at the first fault's values\").\n\nOK here's another angle \u2014 **the (d) enumeration says \"predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary\"**. The COMPOSITE's product diff (#502 fix) edited lines in m88k_exception(). The probe v10 rows pin behaviors. A clean-sweep mutant must (i) be within the enumeration (predicate edits on the named guards, operand swaps from read fields, joint cells distinguishable by body tests), (ii) survive all 41 rows.\n\nLet me try to CONSTRUCT one:\n\nAttempt 1: **Fallback conditioned on fault_pc or epsr?** \"operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read\" \u2014 the fallback body reads `vector` (for the print). If the predicate used a field the body doesn't read, it's out of enumeration. The fallback predicate: `vector < 128 && !implemented`. Operand swaps within: `vector` \u2194 `implemented`? nonsense. Constant 128 \u2194 something? The bound mutants are measured (FB_LO, FALLBACK127, FALLBACK51, FALLBACKLE, FALLBACK_NO_BOUND). What about `vector > 0`? i.e., fallback `if (vector > 0 && ...)` vs `vector < 128` \u2014 wait, that's not a swap. Hmm, a mutant `vector > 3 && vector < 128`?? That EXEMPTS vectors 0..3 from the fallback \u2014 disclosed (vectors 0..3, m88kexchwtraps). Hmm, and `vector != 0`? Same. \n\nAttempt 2: **Re-route `is_trap` operand swap: `!is_trap` \u2192 `!is_user`?** Fields read by the body: vector, PSR (via PSR & SFRZ), is_trap, update_shadow_regs. A swap like `if (PSR & SFRZ)` \u2192 `if (PSR & SFU1_...)` \u2014 PSR-bit swaps! The re-route's guard `PSR & SFRZ` \u2014 swap SFRZ for another PSR bit, e.g., SFD1: `if (PSR & SFD1) { if (!is_trap) { vector = ERROR; fatal; } update_shadow_regs = false; }`. Which rows have SFD1 set and SFRZ clear? Hmm \u2014 SFD1 set + SFRZ clear: usfu1! (PSR_USERSFD1 = 0x0000000a = SFD1|IND, user). usfu1: legal divu with SFD1 set \u2192 SFU1 (114) \u2192 is_trap=0 \u2192 re-route (mutant) fires \u2192 vector = ERROR, fatal, deliver 0x50 instead of fallback HALT \u2192 usfu1 red (expects HALT naming 0x072). Caught. SFD1 set + SFRZ set: sfu1h \u2014 fallback fires first (before re-route) \u2192 same as correct \u2192 sfu1h green. But usfu1 catches it. What about SFD1 clear + SFRZ set: err*/trapfrozen rows (PSR_EXC sets SFD1! 0xb = SFD1|IND|SFRZ \u2014 wait, 0xb = 1011: bit0 SFRZ, bit1 IND, bit3 SFD1. So PSR_EXC = MODE|SFD1|IND|SFRZ \u2014 all set). Hmm, so post-exception PSR always has SFD1 set. Rows with SFRZ set: err*, trapfrozen*, sfu1h (handler runs with PSR_EXC: SFD1+SFRZ). Under the SFD1-swap mutant: for err* rows (SFD1 set, SFRZ set): re-route fires as before (guard now SFD1 \u2014 set) \u2192 same behavior \u2192 green. For trapfrozen rows: guard fires (SFD1 set), inner !is_trap \u2192 exempt \u2192 update_shadow_regs=false \u2192 same \u2192 green. For sfu1h: fallback fires before re-route \u2192 green. For usfu1: SFD1 set, is_trap=0 \u2192 re-route fires \u2192 0x50 \u2192 RED. So the SFD1 swap is caught by usfu1. \u2713 (Good \u2014 usfu1 was added in pass 5 precisely to pin user-mode + SFD1.)\n\nSwap SFRZ \u2192 IND: `if (PSR & IND)`. Rows with IND set: most (PSR_RESET has IND; PSR_EXC has IND). Rows with IND clear: indclr (IND clear, first fault OVF), resid50ind (IND clear, fallback). Under IND-swap: resid50ind (IND clear, trap, vector 50): switch 1 \u2192 not implemented \u2192 fallback fires \u2192 HALT \u2713 green (re-route not reached). resid50/127/11: IND set \u2192 fallback first \u2713. Hmm, first-fault rows (ovfc: IND set? PSR_CARRY = 0x10000000 \u2014 IND clear! ovfc: r5=PSR_CARRY=0x10000000 \u2192 stcr \u2192 PSR = 0x10000000: MODE clear?! Wait \u2014 PSR_CARRY = C bit 0x10000000 \u2014 stcr with MODE clear prints a WARNING and continues (premise 4). So ovfc runs... user mode?! PSR = 0x10000000: MODE clear (user), C set. Then ADD 7fffffff+1 \u2192 OVF fault from USER mode. Hmm \u2014 but the docstring says OVERFLOW has a user route: \"OVERFLOW uovf\" \u2014 and ovfc: OVF from user with C set, expecting PSR_WANT[\"ovfc\"] = 0x9000000b (MODE|C|SFD1|IND|SFRZ \u2014 carry preserved). OK so ovfc is a USER-mode OVF with C set. Interesting. So ovfc: user, C set, IND clear, SFD1 clear, SFRZ clear.\n   Under the IND-swap mutant (re-route guard = PSR & IND): ovfc: first fault OVF \u2014 re-route guard: IND clear \u2192 no re-route \u2192 normal delivery \u2713 green. Hmm \u2014 but wait, ovfc has SFRZ clear anyway, so the correct re-route also doesn't fire. Fine.\n   indclr: IND clear, OVF first fault, SFRZ clear \u2192 no re-route either way \u2713.\n   err* rows: PSR after first fault = PSR_EXC... wait, but err* handlers \u2014 do they modify PSR? errpriv/errunimpl \"drop to user mode keeping SFRZ\" \u2014 PSR = user | SFRZ | ...? IND state? \"keeping SFRZ\" \u2014 presumably stcr writes with MODE clear; IND probably still set (or written explicitly). Hmm \u2014 if errpriv's PSR at second fault = MODE-clear | SFRZ | IND | SFD1 (i.e., PSR_EXC & ~MODE), then IND set \u2192 IND-swap re-route fires \u2192 same as correct \u2192 green. If any err* row dropped IND... unclear. trapfrozen rows: PSR_EXC (IND set) \u2192 green. usfu1: IND set! (0xa = SFD1|IND) \u2192 re-route guard fires \u2192 is_trap=0 \u2192 re-route \u2192 0x50 instead of HALT \u2192 RED. Caught by usfu1 again. \u2713.\n\nSwap SFRZ \u2192 MODE: `if (PSR & MODE)`. MODE set rows: supervisor rows. MODE clear: user rows (umisalign, usfu1, ovfc?, errpriv, errunimpl). Under MODE-swap: errpriv (user, SFRZ set, non-trap): guard MODE clear \u2192 NO re-route \u2192 second fault PRIVILEGE (implemented) \u2192 no fallback \u2192 deliver to PRIVILEGE's vector 0x30 instead of 0x50 \u2192 errpriv RED (expects 0x50 + line). Caught. \u2713.\n\nSwap SFRZ \u2192 C: `if (PSR & C)`. C set rows: ovfc (C set, SFRZ clear \u2014 first fault), resid50c (C set, SFRZ clear, fallback row). C clear + SFRZ set: err*/trapfrozen (PSR_EXC C clear) \u2192 mutant guard (C) clear \u2192 NO re-route for err* rows \u2192 err* deliver to their OWN class vectors instead of 0x50 \u2192 RED. Caught. \u2713.\n\nSo PSR-bit swaps on the re-route guard are all caught. Good.\n\nAttempt 3: **is_trap swap: `!is_trap` \u2192 `is_trap`** = M_SFRZTRAP polarity \u2014 caught (trapfrozen rows re-routed: refatal present on trap rows \u2192 bad=refatal; err* rows NOT re-routed \u2192 deliver own vectors \u2192 red).\n\nAttempt 4: **switch-1 label swaps: e.g., swap the labels of vectors 4 and 5.** The trapfrozen4 row: frozen trap naming 4 \u2192 expects DELIVER at 8*4=0x20? wait \"0x20/0x28/0x30/0x48\" \u2014 trapfrozen4/5/6/9 expect entries 0x20, 0x28, 0x30, 0x48. So trapfrozen4 expects delivery at 0x20 = 8*4. A label swap (vector 4 \u2192 class 5's arm) \u2014 hmm, what does the label change? If switch 1's arm for vector 4 sets implemented and the TAIL switch's arm for vector 4 does per-class work \u2014 a \"label swap\" mutant: swap the case labels 4 and 5 in the tail switch \u2192 trap naming 4 delivers to 0x20 but runs vector-5's tail arm (wrong sxip/snip?) \u2192 BADREGS? The want dicts assert snip/sfip per row \u2192 likely red. Hmm, \"labels\" in the enumeration: \"switch 1's class labels; ... the tail switch's labels\". A label swap between two vectors both covered by rows \u2192 red on the swapped rows. Between a covered and an UNCOVERED vector (e.g., swap tail labels 12 and 13)? Vectors 11..113/116..127 have no tail arms (they're not classes \u2014 the tail switch dispatches per class... hmm, actually vectors 11..113 aren't classes at all; they hit the fallback. The tail switch's labels are the class vectors. Swapping labels of two classes \u2192 both have rows \u2192 red. Swapping a class label with... the default? That's M_NAIVE-ish. OK.\n\nAttempt 5: **Joint cells \"where the body's own tests distinguish them\"** \u2014 the closures listed: {class} x {MODE}, {SFRZ,!is_trap} x {class}, {SFRZ,is_trap} x {class}, fallback x {PSR bit}, window bounds. What joint cells might be MISSING?\n\nHmm \u2014 **{SFRZ set, is_trap} x {vector 128}**: trapfrozen128 \u2713. **{SFRZ set, is_trap, USER trap}** \u2713 trapfrozen128 (runs supervisor though! The frozen trap naming 128 \u2014 the handler runs supervisor (PSR_EXC), so the trap is a supervisor trap naming 128. Is there a distinction between a supervisor trap naming 128 and a user trap naming 128? tb1 in user mode naming 128: allowed (usertb \u2014 \"the first USER trap vector\"). usertb: user, SFRZ clear. A USER tb1 naming 128 with SFRZ set \u2014 no row! Joint cell {MODE user} x {SFRZ} x {trap 128}: a mutant `if (!is_trap || (vector >= 128 && (PSR & MODE)))`... hmm, that's getting contrived \u2014 and arguably within \"{MODE} x {SFRZ} x {class} triple\" disclosure? The disclosed triple is \"for MISALIGNED/OVERFLOW/DIV0 (clause e)\". Hmm, {user} x {SFRZ} x {trap 128} \u2014 the re-route exemption conditioned on MODE for the user trap: mutant `if (!is_trap || (is_trap && vector >= 128 && !(PSR & MODE)))` \u2192 trapfrozen128 (supervisor) exempt \u2713 green; a user frozen trap naming 128 would be re-routed \u2014 but no row. Is this in the enumeration? \"joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them\" \u2014 hmm, the body's tests: the re-route body tests PSR & SFRZ and is_trap; the common path tests... MODE? The tail arms read PSR/MODE? The guarded bodies: the re-route body reads vector (writes ERROR), prints. Hmm \u2014 \"operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read\". A joint predicate {is_trap, vector>=128, MODE} \u2014 MODE is read by bodies (the exception path reads PSR extensively; privsuptb's route: the user-mode filter reads MODE). Eh. This is getting into diminishing returns. The disclosure \"{MODE} x {SFRZ} x {class} triple for MISALIGNED/OVERFLOW/DIV0 (clause e)\" covers the analogous gap for three classes; the user-trap-under-SFRZ-in-user-mode cell is a fourth analog. Hmm. But wait \u2014 can a USER-mode trap even occur with SFRZ set... yes: handler drops to user (stcr MODE clear), then executes tb1 0,r3,128 \u2192 user trap, SFRZ set \u2192 exempt \u2192 deliver 0x400. No row. A mutant re-routing only user-mode traps naming >= 128 would sweep. But is that within \"(d)\"? The (d) enumeration: \"predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary..., operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read, and joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them\". A compound predicate adding MODE to the trap exemption \u2014 MODE is a field the guarded bodies read (the re-route... does the re-route body read MODE? The re-route body: `vector = ERROR; fatal(); update_shadow_regs = false;` \u2014 reads nothing but writes vector. The EXEMPTION (skipping the body) \u2014 the tests that distinguish: hmm.\n\nHonestly, the (d) enumeration is somewhat fuzzy at the edges. The panel wants: either name a clean-sweep mutant with the exact edit and the row that should have caught it, or CLOSE with my own denominator. The disclosed list is part of the contract \u2014 disclosed gaps don't count as breaches (they're adjudicated disclosures with cross-references). The question is whether an UNDISCLOSED clean-sweep mutant exists in the enumeration.\n\nLet me look at the disclosed list once more: \n1. {class} x {delay_slot} beyond DIV0 (m88kdslotshadow)\n2. M_DELAYALWAYS (post-delivery delay_slot write on non-delay path \u2014 needs second UNFROZEN exception \u2014 filed)\n3. vectors 0..3's switch-1 and tail arms (m88kexchwtraps)\n4. DMT contents; SSBR; fcr2/4/5\n5. first-exception SFU1 (m88kexcsfu1; SFU1_IMPRECISE 115 likewise)\n6. exact INFO\n7. `implemented = is_trap` at BOUNDS/ERROR (equivalent today, m88ktbnd)\n8. {MODE} x {SFRZ} x {class} triple for MISALIGNED/OVERFLOW/DIV0 (clause e)\n9. an exemption of a single residual vector other than 11/50/127 from the fallback (a value the body never tests as a case)\n10. MXM/BO not settable by stcr (fallback PSR-bit closure limit)\n\nNow, hunt for undisclosed holes in v10's row set:\n\n**Hole H1: {SFRZ set, is_trap, USER mode} x {trap vector}.** As analyzed: trapfrozen rows run supervisor (PSR_EXC has MODE). errpriv/errunimpl drop to user but are non-trap. So the cell {user, SFRZ, trap} is unrowed. A mutant `if (!is_trap || (PSR & MODE))` on the re-route: trapfrozen* (supervisor) exempt \u2713; errpriv/errunimpl (user, non-trap) re-routed \u2713; a user frozen trap would be re-routed \u2014 no row \u2192 clean sweep? Wait \u2014 check all rows: any row with a TRAP in user mode? usertb: user tb1 naming 128, SFRZ CLEAR \u2192 re-route guard (PSR & SFRZ) false \u2192 no re-route either way \u2713 green. privtb: user tb0 \u2192 PRIVILEGE raised with is_trap=0 \u2014 hmm, the PRIVILEGE exception has SFRZ clear (first fault) \u2713. umisalign, uovf, udiv0, unimpl(user?): user-mode first faults, SFRZ clear \u2713. So M_SFRZTRAPUSERMODE: re-route `if (!is_trap) \u2192 if (!is_trap || (PSR & MODE))`: for all 41 rows, no row has {SFRZ set AND trap AND user} \u2192 clean sweep 41/41!\n\nHmm wait \u2014 is that right? Let me double check errpriv: \"the handler drops to user mode keeping SFRZ\" \u2014 errpriv: second fault PRIVILEGE from user mode, SFRZ set, is_trap=0 \u2192 mutant: !is_trap true \u2192 re-route \u2713 same. errunimpl same. trapfrozen128: supervisor trap \u2192 exempt via is_trap \u2713. Yes \u2014 the mutant `if (!is_trap || (PSR & MODE))` sweeps v10 cleanly.\n\nBut WAIT \u2014 is it in the (d) enumeration? The enumeration: \"predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary (switch 1's class labels; the fallback's `vector < 128 && !implemented`; the re-route's `PSR & SFRZ` and `is_trap`; the tail switch's labels), operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read, and joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them.\"\n\nThe re-route's `is_trap` \u2014 a mutant conditioning on `(PSR & MODE)` conjunctively: `if (!is_trap || (PSR & MODE))`. Is MODE \"a field the guarded bodies read\"? The guarded body of the re-route: writes vector=ERROR, prints, sets update_shadow_regs=false. Does the body READ MODE? The print doesn't. Hmm. \"operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read\" \u2014 this constrains OPERAND SWAPS, not new conjuncts. The first category: \"predicates dominating the diff's edited lines\" \u2014 a predicate edit on the re-route line. Adding `|| (PSR & MODE)` to the `!is_trap` test \u2014 that's a predicate edit on an edited line (the re-route's is_trap). Is it \"dominating\"? Hmm, \"predicates dominating the diff's edited lines\" \u2014 I read this as: mutants that alter the predicates guarding the edited lines. The composite's diff (the #502 fix) edited m88k_exception() \u2014 the fallback, re-route, tail. A mutant on the re-route's condition adding a MODE disjunct is a predicate edit within the function boundary. \n\nHmm, but hold on \u2014 is {MODE} x {SFRZ} x {is_trap} actually distinguishable by \"the body's own tests\"? The third enumeration category: \"joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them\" \u2014 meaning mutant classes that create joint cells which the body's tests CAN distinguish \u2014 i.e., cells the probe COULD see. The user-frozen-trap cell: the body (re-route) would print + deliver 0x50 instead of 0x400 \u2014 distinguishable IF a row existed. So it's a joint cell in-scope-ish. Is it DISCLOSED? The disclosed item 8: \"the {MODE} x {SFRZ} x {class} triple for MISALIGNED/OVERFLOW/DIV0 (clause e)\" \u2014 that's for classes MISALIGNED/OVERFLOW/DIV0 (the classes whose err* rows run supervisor). Hmm \u2014 but PRIVILEGE and UNIMPLEMENTED err* rows DO drop to user, so {MODE} x {SFRZ} for PRIVILEGE/UNIMPL non-trap is pinned (errpriv/errunimpl run user). The TRAP side: {MODE} x {SFRZ} x {trap} \u2014 all trapfrozen rows supervisor. Is that covered by clause e's disclosure? Clause e is about MISALIGNED/OVERFLOW/DIV0 \u2014 not traps. Hmm. So the cell {user, SFRZ, trap-naming-any-vector} seems UNDISCLOSED.\n\nHmm wait, wait. Let me reconsider: can a USER-mode trap with SFRZ set even reach the re-route question... The exemption: `if (!is_trap) { re-route }`. The mutant `!is_trap || (PSR & MODE)`: for a USER-mode frozen TRAP: re-route fires (prints, vector=ERROR). Distinguishable from correct (exempt, deliver own vector)? Yes \u2014 0x50 vs own vector, plus the fatal line. No row covers user+trap+SFRZ. So the mutant sweeps.\n\nBUT \u2014 is this mutant within \"(d)\"? Let me re-read the (d) enumeration once more: \"predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary (switch 1's class labels; the fallback's `vector < 128 && !implemented`; the re-route's `PSR & SFRZ` and `is_trap`; the tail switch's labels), operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read, and joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them.\"\n\nHmm \u2014 \"the re-route's `PSR & SFRZ` and `is_trap`\" \u2014 the re-route's predicate components. A mutant conjoining/disjoining a MODE test into the re-route: MODE is not among \"the re-route's PSR & SFRZ and is_trap\" \u2014 it's introducing a new operand. The \"operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read\" \u2014 the re-route's guarded body reads... hmm, does it read any fields? `vector = ERROR` writes; fatal() reads nothing; update_shadow_regs=false writes. So \"fields the guarded bodies read\" for the re-route: arguably NONE \u2192 no operand swaps allowed for the re-route \u2192 introducing MODE (a field the body doesn't read) is OUT of the enumeration!\n\nHmm. That reading makes the enumeration quite tight: for the re-route, only edits to `PSR & SFRZ` (e.g., polarity, which PSR bit \u2014 wait, swapping SFRZ for another bit would be an operand swap with a field the body doesn't read \u2192 out?) and `is_trap` (polarity, and... the measured mutants M_SFRZTRAP10/USER add vector tests \u2014 vector IS read by the... hmm, the re-route body reads vector? `vector = ERROR` \u2014 writes. Hmm, but M_SFRZTRAPUSER (`!is_trap || vector >= 128`) was measured and caught by trapfrozen128. And the fallback body READS vector (prints it). \n\nOK here's the thing: the enumeration is the reviewer's scope definition, and the panel asks: within THAT enumeration, does a clean-sweep mutant exist? The measured table shows six mutants all 40/41. The question is whether the enumeration admits another mutant that sweeps. Given the enumeration's constraint \"operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read\" and \"joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them\", let me look for the sharpest candidate:\n\n**Candidate X: the re-route's `is_trap` conjoined with vector tests beyond 10/128.** M_SFRZTRAP10 (`|| vector == ERROR`) and M_SFRZTRAPUSER (`|| vector >= 128`) measured. What about `|| vector == 7`?? A mutant re-routing only frozen traps naming 7: trapfrozen (7) \u2192 re-routed \u2192 0x50 instead of 0x38 \u2192 RED (trapfrozen expects 0x38). Every trapfrozen* row names a distinct vector (4,5,6,7,8,9,10,128) \u2192 any single-vector exemption is caught by that row. \u2713 closed.\n\n**Candidate Y: the fallback predicate's `!implemented` operand.** `!implemented` \u2014 implemented is computed by switch 1. Mutants on switch-1 arms measured per class. What about a mutant on switch 1 for vector 114/115 (SFU1)? E.g., switch 1 gains an arm `case 114: implemented = 1;` \u2192 SFU1 no longer hits the fallback \u2192 delivers to 0x390 (br . planted) \u2192 sfu1h/usfu1 expect HALT \u2192 RED. Caught. And 115: disclosed (SFU1_IMPRECISE).\n\n**Candidate Z: joint cell {SFRZ} x {PSR bit} at the RE-ROUTE (not the fallback).** The fallback closure now has IND/C both sides. The re-route's `PSR & SFRZ` \u2014 a mutant `PSR & SFRZ && (PSR & IND)`?? Rows with SFRZ set and IND clear: hmm \u2014 do any exist? err* handlers: what PSR do they run with? PSR_EXC = 0x8000000b (IND set) possibly modified (errpriv drops MODE: 0x0000000b \u2014 IND still set). sfu1h: PSR_EXC (IND set). trapfrozen*: PSR_EXC. So EVERY SFRZ-set row has IND set \u2192 a mutant `if ((PSR & SFRZ) && (PSR & IND))` sweeps!! Wait \u2014 check: is there any row with SFRZ set and IND clear? Hmm. Let me scan: rows with SFRZ set: error, errovf, errdiv0, errpriv, errunimpl, trapfrozen*, sfu1h. What PSR do the err* rows' second faults run with? The first fault sets PSR to PSR_EXC = 0x8000000b (from the docstring's PSR_WANT/PSR_EXC discussion: \"indclr wants the default PSR_EXC (0x8000000b): IND was CLEAR before the fault and must be SET by the exception path\" \u2014 so the exception path WRITES PSR = 0x8000000b = MODE|SFD1|IND|SFRZ). So after the first fault, PSR always = 0x8000000b (or 0x9000000b with carry preserved for ovfc \u2014 wait, PSR_WANT[\"ovfc\"] = 0x9000000b: MODE|C|SFD1|IND|SFRZ). So all second faults run with IND SET (unless the handler clears it via stcr \u2014 errpriv \"drops to user mode keeping SFRZ\": stcr with r5 = user|SFRZ|...? presumably 0x0000000b-ish minus MODE = 0x0000000b? that has IND set). trapfrozen handlers: tb1 directly, PSR_EXC \u2192 IND set. sfu1h handler: divu directly, PSR_EXC.\n\nSo: **M_SFRZIND: re-route `if ((PSR & SFRZ) && (PSR & IND))` \u2014 every SFRZ-set row has IND set \u2192 re-route behavior identical on all rows \u2192 clean sweep 41/41?**\n\nHold on \u2014 also need: rows with IND clear and SFRZ CLEAR \u2014 re-route guard false either way (SFRZ clear) \u2713. Rows with SFRZ set: IND set \u2713 (as analyzed). So the mutant is behaviorally identical ON EVERY ROW \u2192 sweeps.\n\nIs it in the enumeration? \"the re-route's `PSR & SFRZ`\" \u2014 conjoining another PSR bit: hmm, this is exactly the same CLASS of mutant as the fallback's PSR-bit conditioning (M_FBIND/M_FBNOC \u2014 kimi's pass-6 class \"{fallback predicate} x {PSR bit}\"). The re-route's guard is `PSR & SFRZ` \u2014 the enumeration names it. A conjunct `(PSR & IND)` \u2014 is IND \"a field the guarded bodies read\"? The re-route body reads... nothing. Hmm. But wait \u2014 kimi's pass-6 finding was about the FALLBACK predicate x PSR bits; the repair swept the fallback side (resid50ind/resid50c/usfu1/sfu1h give every settable bit both sides AT THE FALLBACK). Did anyone check the RE-ROUTE predicate x PSR bits?? The re-route fires only when SFRZ is set \u2014 and the rows with SFRZ set all have IND set (because the exception path itself sets IND, and no err* handler clears it). What about C at the re-route? Rows with SFRZ set and C set: hmm \u2014 ovfc is a FIRST fault (SFRZ clear at fault time... wait, ovfc: PSR = 0x10000000 (C set, user) \u2192 ADD \u2192 OVF first fault. At the first fault, SFRZ clear \u2192 no re-route. But after the first fault, PSR = 0x9000000b (C preserved!). ovfc is a single-fault row though (expects DELIVER to OVF's entry with PSR_WANT = 0x9000000b). No second fault. So no row has {SFRZ set, C set}. A mutant `if ((PSR & SFRZ) && !(PSR & C))` on the re-route: all SFRZ-set rows have C clear \u2192 identical \u2192 sweeps!\n\nAnd MODE at the re-route: errpriv/errunimpl run USER with SFRZ set \u2192 `if ((PSR & SFRZ) && (PSR & MODE))` would skip their re-route \u2192 RED (caught). SFD1: all SFRZ-set rows have SFD1 set (PSR_EXC) \u2192 `if ((PSR & SFRZ) && !(PSR & SFD1))` sweeps!! \n\nHmm wait \u2014 so the RE-ROUTE's PSR-bit closure is NOT complete: IND, C, SFD1 are all SET on every SFRZ-set row (because PSR_EXC sets SFD1|IND|SFRZ and C is only set via ovfc which doesn't re-fault). The fallback's PSR-bit closure was completed (kimi's class), but the same CLASS applied to the RE-ROUTE's guard is open: {re-route predicate} x {PSR bit}!\n\nLet me verify carefully. The re-route: `if (PSR & SFRZ) { if (!is_trap) { vector = ERROR; fatal(...); } update_shadow_regs = false; }`. Mutant M_SFRZAND: `if ((PSR & SFRZ) && (PSR & IND)) { ... }`.\n\nRows where the re-route MATTERS (SFRZ set):\n- err* (error, errovf, errdiv0, errpriv, errunimpl): PSR at second fault = PSR_EXC modified: error/errovf/errdiv0 supervisor 0x8000000b; errpriv/errunimpl user ~0x0000000b. All have IND (0x2) set. Mutant: guard true (SFRZ \u2713 IND \u2713) \u2192 same as correct \u2192 DELIVER 0x50 + fatal line \u2713 green.\n- trapfrozen* (4,5,6,7,8,9,10,128): PSR = PSR_EXC (0x8000000b, IND set) \u2192 guard true \u2192 inner !is_trap \u2192 exempt \u2192 same \u2713 green.\n- sfu1h: second fault SFU1 114 \u2192 fallback fires BEFORE the re-route \u2192 returns \u2192 re-route never evaluated \u2713 green (guard value irrelevant).\nSo M_SFRZAND sweeps 41/41. Similarly `&& (PSR & SFD1)`, `&& !(PSR & C)`, `&& (PSR & MXM)` (MXM clear everywhere \u2014 `&& !(PSR & MXM)` also sweeps, but MXM/BO are disclosed as unsettable... the disclosure is about the FALLBACK's bit closure: \"MXM and BO cannot be set by stcr without stopping the cpu -- disclosed\". Hmm \u2014 the MXM/BO disclosure was scoped to the fallback predicate closure. But the same limitation applies to the re-route: an IND/C/SFD1 conjunct sweeps; MXM/BO conjuncts would too but those bits were disclosed globally?).\n\nNow \u2014 is this within the (d) enumeration? The enumeration explicitly lists \"the re-route's `PSR & SFRZ` and `is_trap`\" among the predicates dominating the edited lines. A mutant that conjoins `(PSR & IND)` to the re-route's guard is a predicate edit on the re-route \u2014 the guard `PSR & SFRZ` becomes `(PSR & SFRZ) && (PSR & IND)`. Both operands are PSR bits \u2014 an \"operand swap drawn from fields the guarded bodies read\"? Hmm \u2014 does the re-route's guarded body read the PSR? The body: `vector = ERROR; fatal(); update_shadow_regs = false`. No PSR read. Hmm. But the GUARD reads PSR. \"Fields the guarded bodies read\" \u2014 I think this phrase means: for operand swaps, you may only swap in fields that the guarded body actually reads (so the mutant remains observable). For the re-route... the body reads nothing, so pure operand swaps are vacuous there. But the guard EDIT itself (conjoining a PSR bit) \u2014 the first category, \"predicates dominating the diff's edited lines\" \u2014 the re-route's `PSR & SFRZ` is named. Editing that predicate by adding a conjunct is a predicate mutation on a named line.\n\nHmm, but here's the rub: is the mutant DISTINGUISHABLE by any test the body could ever see? The panel's category 3: \"joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them\". For the re-route x IND conjunct: the joint cell {SFRZ set, IND clear} would be distinguished (re-route suppressed \u2192 err* deliver own vectors). The body's tests distinguish them IF a row existed. The question is whether a row CAN exist: can the guest ever have SFRZ set and IND clear at an exception? IND clear + SFRZ set: after a first fault, PSR = 0x8000000b (IND set) \u2014 but the HANDLER can stcr IND clear! Like indclr/resid50ind do (stcr r5,cr1 with IND-clear values) \u2014 but keeping SFRZ: r5 = 0x80000001 (MODE|SFRZ, IND clear)? stcr stores verbatim \u2192 PSR = MODE|SFRZ, IND clear \u2014 wait, does stcr print a WARNING for... MODE is set here, fine. So a row COULD exist: first fault (sets SFRZ), handler: stcr r5=0x80000001 (clear SFD1? 0x80000001 = MODE|SFRZ \u2014 SFD1 clear, IND clear), then a second fault (non-trap, e.g., misaligned load) \u2192 SFRZ set, IND clear, non-trap \u2192 re-route must fire \u2192 mutant suppresses \u2192 red. So the cell is reachable and row-able \u2014 it's a genuine floor hole, same CLASS as kimi's pass-6 finding (predicate x PSR bit), just on the re-route's guard instead of the fallback's.\n\nHmm wait, but hold on. Let me double-check whether the pass-6 repair's rows incidentally cover it. resid50ind: stcr 0x80000000 (MODE only \u2014 wait PSR_INDCLR = 0x80000000 = MODE, IND clear!) then tb1 50 \u2192 trap, SFRZ CLEAR (no first fault \u2014 it's a single instruction sequence: stcr then tb1; SFRZ was never set \u2014 PSR_RESET has SFRZ clear, stcr wrote 0x80000000, SFRZ clear) \u2192 fallback row. So resid50ind has SFRZ clear. Not a re-route row. \u2713 my analysis stands: no row has SFRZ set + IND clear.\n\nHmm, and what about sfu1h \u2014 its second fault runs with PSR_EXC (IND set) \u2014 but the fallback fires first anyway.\n\nWhat about usfu1: PSR = 0x0000000a (user|SFD1|IND) \u2014 SFRZ clear. \u2713 no.\n\nSo: **M_REIND: re-route guard `if ((PSR & SFRZ) && (PSR & IND))` sweeps v10 41/41.** Likewise `&& (PSR & SFD1)` and `&& !(PSR & C)` and (vacuously) anything conjunctive that's true on PSR_EXC-pattern rows. Actually even `&& (PSR & MODE) || !is_trap`... no wait, simpler: the pure conjunct sweeps.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 but actually, let me reconsider whether `&& (PSR & MODE)` is caught: errpriv/errunimpl (user, SFRZ, non-trap) \u2192 guard false \u2192 no re-route \u2192 they deliver to PRIV/UNIMPL vectors \u2192 RED. Caught. So MODE is pinned both ways at the re-route (by errpriv/errunimpl user + err* supervisor). SFD1: all SFRZ-set rows have SFD1 set \u2192 `&& !(PSR & SFD1)` sweeps; `&& (PSR & SFD1)` also sweeps (all have it set). C: all SFRZ-set rows have C clear \u2192 `&& !(PSR & C)` sweeps; `&& (PSR & C)` suppresses ALL re-routes \u2192 err* red \u2192 caught. IND: all have IND set \u2192 `&& (PSR & IND)` sweeps; `&& !(PSR & IND)` suppresses all \u2192 err* red \u2192 caught. SFRZ-bit vs others... So the open cells: {re-route} x {SFD1 set-side, IND set-side, C clear-side}. Each sweeps.\n\nHmm \u2014 but WAIT. Is this really \"a clean-sweep mutant in the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration\"? Let me re-read the (d) enumeration once more: \"predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary (switch 1's class labels; the fallback's `vector < 128 && !implemented`; the re-route's `PSR & SFRZ` and `is_trap`; the tail switch's labels), operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read, and joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them.\"\n\n\"the re-route's `PSR & SFRZ`\" \u2014 yes, the re-route's SFRZ test is an enumerated predicate. A mutant of that predicate \u2014 conjoining IND \u2014 is a mutant of an enumerated predicate. The pass-6 repair explicitly swept the analogous class on the FALLBACK (\"the {fallback predicate} x {PSR bit} CLASS... every settable PSR bit now has both sides at the fallback\"). The repair-chain rule: \"when consecutive passes find breaches of one CLASS the next repair closes the class, not the cell.\" Kimi's class was \"{fallback predicate} x {PSR bit}\" \u2014 arguably the CLASS is \"{guard predicate} x {PSR bit}\" over the exception path's guards, and the repair closed only the fallback instance, leaving the re-route instance open. That's a STRONG finding: the same class, one guard over.\n\nHmm, but hold on \u2014 let me double-check the C side more carefully. Is there any row with SFRZ set AND C set at the moment the re-route is evaluated? ovfc: first fault from PSR=0x10000000 (C set) \u2014 at the fault, SFRZ clear. The re-route is evaluated DURING the first fault's delivery: PSR at that point = 0x10000000 (the pre-fault PSR \u2014 the re-route happens before the common path writes PSR_EXC). So ovfc's first fault: PSR = C-set, SFRZ-clear \u2192 guard false either way \u2713 green. No row faults with {C set, SFRZ set}. \u2713. And is {C set, SFRZ set} even reachable? Handler: first fault sets PSR=0x9000000b? No wait \u2014 ovfc's WANT is 0x9000000b because C is preserved through the exception path. So a second fault after ovfc-style first fault would run with PSR = 0x9000000b (C set, SFRZ set!). Is there such a row? No \u2014 ovfc is single-fault. But it's reachable: handler could stcr or just fault again (ADD again) \u2192 OVF second fault with C set, SFRZ set \u2192 re-route must fire. Row-able. So M_REC (`re-route && !(PSR & C)`) is a genuine hole too \u2014 same class.\n\nAnd SFD1: {SFRZ set, SFD1 clear}: reachable? After first fault PSR = 0x8000000b (SFD1 set); handler stcr 0x80000003 (MODE|SFRZ|IND, SFD1 clear) \u2192 second fault \u2192 SFD1 clear, SFRZ set. Reachable, row-able. Hole.\n\nHmm OK wait, but now I should double check the claim \"every SFRZ-set row has IND set\" \u2014 errpriv: \"the handler drops to user mode keeping SFRZ\" \u2014 the handler stcr's some value. If the handler wrote 0x00000001 (user, SFRZ, IND clear, SFD1 clear)?? Then errpriv would be an SFRZ-set IND-CLEAR row and M_REIND would be caught by errpriv! Hmm. What does errpriv's handler write? Not inlined. \"PRIVILEGE errpriv and UNIMPLEMENTED errunimpl -- the handler drops to user mode keeping SFRZ\". Dropping to user mode = clearing MODE via stcr. The minimal edit to PSR_EXC (0x8000000b) clearing MODE = 0x0000000b (user|SFD1|IND|SFRZ). Would the handler instead write 0x00000009 or 0x00000003? Can't tell from the packet. If the handler clears ONLY MODE (keeping the rest of PSR_EXC), IND stays set \u2192 M_REIND sweeps. If the handler writes a fresh value without IND... unknowable from the packet. Hmm. But note: the diff's own repair reasoning says for the FALLBACK: \"every fallback-reaching row had IND set and C clear\" \u2014 and the repair added resid50ind/resid50c to give IND/C both sides AT THE FALLBACK. For the re-route, the packet says NOTHING about IND/C/SFD1 both sides. And the pass-6 adjudication of kimi's class was scoped to the fallback (\"the {fallback predicate} x {PSR bit} CLASS\"). So the re-route's PSR-bit closure is at best unadjudicated, and from the packet's own premises (PSR_EXC = 0x8000000b written by the exception path; all second faults run PSR_EXC or user-dropped PSR_EXC), IND and SFD1 are set on all re-route rows \u2192 the conjunct sweeps.\n\nWait, one more check on the \"user-dropped PSR_EXC\" assumption for errpriv/errunimpl: if the handler writes 0x0000000b (PSR_EXC & ~MODE), then IND set, SFD1 set, C clear \u2192 M_REIND (`&& IND`) sweeps, M_RESFD1 (`&& SFD1`) sweeps, M_REC (`&& !(C)`) sweeps. If the handler writes something else \u2014 say 0x00000003 (user|SFRZ|IND, SFD1 clear) \u2014 then M_RESFD1 would be caught by errpriv (SFD1 clear \u2192 guard false \u2192 no re-route \u2192 errpriv delivers 0x30 \u2192 red). But M_REIND still sweeps (IND set either way, probably). The packet doesn't inline the handlers' stcr values \u2192 I should present M_REIND as the primary (robust under any plausible handler PSR: \"keeping SFRZ\" implies minimal edit keeping other bits; and even a fresh-write handler would likely keep IND since dropping IND isn't implied) \u2014 hmm, honestly I can't be 100% sure errpriv keeps IND. But there's a cleaner variant:\n\n**The cleanest statement**: the re-route's guard `PSR & SFRZ` has only ever been tested with PSR values from ONE family (post-exception PSR_EXC patterns: SFD1|IND|SFRZ set, C clear, MODE either). Every measured re-route mutant varies `is_trap`/vector, never the PSR-bit conjunct. A mutant conjoining any bit-pattern constant across that family sweeps. Whether errpriv's exact handler PSR keeps IND is NOT INLINED \u2014 that's the one fact that would settle whether `&& (PSR & IND)` specifically sweeps; but `&& !(PSR & C)` requires only \"no row has C set at the re-route\", which follows from the packet (only ovfc sets C, single fault, SFRZ clear at its fault; PSR_WANT shows C preserved but no row re-faults with C set). Hmm wait \u2014 actually let me re-check: does anything else set C? resid50c: stcr 0x90000002 (MODE|C|IND... 0x90000002 = MODE|C|IND? 0x90000002: bit31 MODE, bit28 C, bit1 IND) then tb1 50 \u2192 trap, SFRZ CLEAR (no first fault; stcr wrote SFRZ clear) \u2192 fallback. SFRZ clear \u2713. So no row evaluates the re-route with C set. M_REC: `if ((PSR & SFRZ) && !(PSR & C))` \u2014 on all 41 rows, behavior identical to correct \u2192 41/41 sweep. GUARANTEED from the packet's premises (given premise 2's structure and the row list). Well \u2014 \"guaranteed\" modulo rows not inlined: the 41 rows include ~36 v8 rows whose full definitions aren't in the diff. Do any of THEM have SFRZ set + C set? The err* rows' shapes are described: \"DIV0 errdiv0 -- the handler clears SFD1 first\" (interesting \u2014 errdiv0's handler clears SFD1! So errdiv0's second fault runs with SFD1 CLEAR, SFRZ set! That means M_RESFD1 (`&& (PSR & SFD1)`) is CAUGHT by errdiv0: SFD1 clear \u2192 guard false \u2192 no re-route \u2192 errdiv0 delivers DIV0's vector 0x40 instead of 0x50 \u2192 RED!). Hmm!! Let me re-read: \"DIV0 errdiv0 -- the handler clears SFD1 first, with it set the divu raises SFU1 instead (sfu1h) --\". So errdiv0's handler clears SFD1 (so the second divu raises DIV0, not SFU1). So errdiv0: second fault DIV0 with SFD1 CLEAR, SFRZ set. So {SFRZ set, SFD1 clear} IS rowed (errdiv0) \u2192 `&& (PSR & SFD1)` caught by errdiv0; `&& !(PSR & SFD1)` caught by the other err* rows (SFD1 set). SFD1 both sides \u2713 at the re-route. Good \u2014 so SFD1 is closed.\n\nWhat about IND on err* rows? errdiv0's handler: \"clears SFD1 first\" \u2014 clearing SFD1 via stcr: writes PSR with SFD1 clear \u2014 keeping IND presumably (0x80000003? = MODE|SFRZ|IND with SFD1 clear \u2014 wait 0x8000000b & ~0x8 = 0x80000003). IND stays. The other err* handlers: error/errovf presumably don't touch PSR (PSR_EXC); errpriv/errunimpl drop MODE. So IND set on all err* second faults (as far as the packet shows). C: none set C. So M_REIND and M_REC both sweep, UNLESS some non-inlined v8 row has SFRZ+IND-clear or SFRZ+C-set. From the packet: the SFRZ-set rows are exactly {error, errovf, errdiv0, errpriv, errunimpl, trapfrozen, trapfrozen4,5,6,8,9, (new:10,128), sfu1h}. That's the want-dict frozen list + sfu1h. Their handler PSRs: PSR_EXC family. The packet's own PSR_WANT/PSR_EXC discussion (0x8000000b) supports IND set, C clear on all. I'm fairly confident.\n\nSo the answer to THE ONE QUESTION: **YES \u2014 a clean-sweep mutant exists: conjoin the re-route's `PSR & SFRZ` guard with a PSR-bit test that is constant across every SFRZ-set row.** Concretely: `if ((PSR & SFRZ) && !(PSR & C))` \u2014 no row reaches the re-route with C set (only ovfc/resid50c set C, and both have SFRZ clear when their exceptions evaluate) \u2192 41/41 sweep. The row that should have caught it: an err*-shaped row whose handler sets C (stcr 0x9000000b-ish... wait, careful: to have C set at the second fault, handler stcr's PSR with C and SFRZ \u2014 e.g., first fault, handler: stcr r5=0x9000000b? Hmm, stcr writes verbatim: 0x9000000b = MODE|C|SFD1|IND|SFRZ \u2192 then second fault misaligned-load \u2192 re-route must fire with C set \u2192 mutant suppresses \u2192 red). Or `&& (PSR & IND)` with a handler clearing IND keeping SFRZ (stcr 0x80000001-ish... wait SFD1: 0x80000001 = MODE|SFRZ \u2014 SFD1 clear too; fine, the row would also pin SFD1's set side... no wait \u2014 clearing SFD1 makes it an SFD1-clear re-route row, duplicating errdiv0's pin but with IND clear \u2014 fine, a single row \"errC\": first fault, handler stcr 0x9000000b (C set, SFRZ kept), second fault non-trap class X \u2192 expect 0x50 + refatal line; mutant M_REC \u2192 delivers X's vector, no line \u2192 red.)\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, but is C settable by stcr without stopping? Yes \u2014 ovfc/resid50c do exactly that (stcr with C set: PSR_CARRY). And MODE must be set to avoid the WARNING... ovfc used 0x10000000 (MODE clear \u2192 WARNING printed and continues \u2014 so user mode with C is fine). For the new row: 0x9000000b keeps MODE set. Fine.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 the re-route's guard is `PSR & SFRZ` per premise 2. Is the guard evaluated against the CURRENT PSR (pre-common-path, i.e., the PSR as left by the handler) \u2014 yes. So the row: first fault (any class, sets SFRZ via PSR_EXC write), handler: stcr r5=0x9000000b (re-set C|SFRZ|SFD1|IND|MODE), then a non-trap second fault (e.g., misaligned load or add-overflow) \u2192 SFRZ set, C set \u2192 re-route fires \u2192 0x50 + line. Mutant `&& !(PSR & C)` \u2192 suppressed \u2192 delivers the class's own vector \u2192 red row.\n\nSo: ONE MORE REPAIR (not CLOSE, not DO NOT SHIP). The repair: add the {re-route predicate} x {PSR bit} closure \u2014 rows giving C (and IND) their off-pattern side at the re-route; SFD1 is already both-sided (errdiv0 clears it \u2014 wait, does errdiv0 pin `&& !(PSR & SFD1)`? errdiv0: SFD1 clear \u2192 mutant `&& !(PSR & SFD1)`: guard = SFRZ && !SFD1 = true for errdiv0 \u2192 re-route fires \u2713 green for errdiv0; but error/errovf/errpriv/errunimpl (SFD1 set) \u2192 guard false \u2192 no re-route \u2192 red. So `&& !(PSR & SFD1)` caught. And `&& (PSR & SFD1)`: errdiv0 SFD1 clear \u2192 guard false \u2192 no re-route \u2192 errdiv0 red. \u2713 both sides. So SFD1 closed at the re-route \u2014 by accident of errdiv0's design (handler clears SFD1 to make the divu raise DIV0). Nice.\n\nSo the OPEN bits at the re-route: IND (set on all SFRZ rows \u2192 `&& (PSR & IND)` sweeps) and C (clear on all \u2192 `&& !(PSR & C)` sweeps). Also MXM/BO trivially (disclosed-ish as unsettable, but the disclosure was framed for the fallback).\n\nHmm, hold on \u2014 let me reconsider IND once more. Is there really no SFRZ-set IND-clear row? The fallback rows resid50ind clears IND but SFRZ clear. sfu1h: PSR_EXC. usfu1: 0xa. trapfrozen*: PSR_EXC. err*: PSR_EXC family (errdiv0: SFD1-cleared PSR_EXC = 0x80000003, IND set; errpriv/errunimpl: MODE-cleared, IND set presumably). So yes \u2014 IND set on all. M_REIND sweeps. But the C variant is the one I can assert most cleanly from the packet, since errdiv0's \"clears SFD1 first\" explicitly rewrites PSR and could in principle also clear IND (0x80000001? unknown \u2014 the packet doesn't say). Hmm: \"the handler clears SFD1 first\" \u2014 if errdiv0's handler stcr'd 0x80000001 (clearing SFD1 AND IND), then errdiv0 would be an IND-clear SFRZ-set row \u2192 M_REIND caught by errdiv0, M_REC still sweeping. Either way, M_REC (`&& !(PSR & C)`) sweeps: NO row sets C at any SFRZ-set exception evaluation \u2014 C-set rows are ovfc (SFRZ clear at fault... wait wait. Hold on. ovfc: the FIRST fault's re-route evaluation: PSR = 0x10000000 \u2014 SFRZ clear \u2192 guard false, mutant guard also false (SFRZ clear) \u2192 same \u2713. But hmm \u2014 what about PSR_WANT ovfc = 0x9000000b \u2014 that's the POST-delivery PSR. No second fault. \u2713.) and resid50c (stcr 0x90000002, SFRZ clear, tb1 \u2192 fallback; re-route never reached \u2014 fallback returns first... wait, resid50c: vector 50 \u2192 switch 1: not implemented \u2192 fallback fires \u2192 return. Re-route never evaluated \u2713; and even if it were, SFRZ clear \u2713 same). So M_REC is a clean 41/41 sweep, provable from the packet's inlined premises + row list.\n\nHmm, although \u2014 one more: are there OTHER v8 rows