C (and MXM/BO) on one side only.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 but I should double-check the second question's list: \"of the pass-7 dispositions (five row closures, one channel, one disclosure withdrawn (class 10), one gate repair, one interval wording, the deepseek 0..3 class kept disclosed), WHICH IS WRONG?\" \u2014 the dispositions are pass-7's adjudications of pass-6's findings. Hmm: five row closures = trapfrozen10, resid11, resid50ind, resid50c, trapfrozen128. One channel = refatal. One disclosure withdrawn = class 10 \"observationally identical\" withdrawn (now told by refatal + trapfrozen10). One gate repair = the literal backslash-n check lines. One interval wording = 115..127. deepseek 0..3 kept disclosed. WHICH IS WRONG?\n\nIf my M_REC/M_REIND finding holds, the WRONG disposition is... hmm, none of those seven directly \u2014 my finding is a NEW hole (the re-route's PSR-bit closure), which indicts the pass-6 REPAIR's scope (and hence pass 7's closure of it). Hmm. But wait \u2014 maybe one of the seven dispositions is independently wrong. Let me check each:\n\n1. **trapfrozen10 closure**: sound per premises (switch-1 ERROR arm under SFRZ; M_ERRORFRZOFF caught \u2192 HALT naming 0x00a \u2014 consistent: fallback fires (vector 10 < 128, implemented=false) \u2192 diagnostic names 0x00a \u2713). Also the refatal expectation for trapfrozen10 = False (exempt arm doesn't print) \u2713 consistent with premise 2.\n\nHmm wait \u2014 one check on trapfrozen10's expected DELIVER and the want-dict: trapfrozen10 is in the frozen-shadow list (added: \"trapfrozen10\", \"trapfrozen128\" appended to the frozen set) \u2192 shadows frozen at first fault's values \u2713 (SFRZ set \u2192 update_shadow_regs=false even exempted \u2713 premise 2/4).\n\nAnd trapfrozen128 in the frozen set \u2713 (exempted trap, frozen shadows).\n\n2. **resid11 closure**: `vector >= 12 &&` mutant \u2192 vector 11 skips fallback \u2192 \"delivered to 0x58, the tail default\" \u2192 TAILHALT. Wait \u2014 premise 2: \"the tail switch (labels per class; its `default:` prints `internal error` and stops the cpu -- reachable by no vector below 128 at HEAD-with-fix)\". With the mutant, vector 11 reaches the tail switch \u2192 default \u2192 internal error + stop \u2192 TAILHALT \u2713. And resid11's correct result: HALT naming 0x00b \u2713 (fallback prints 0x00b = 11). EXPECT_VECTOR[\"resid11\"] = 0x00b \u2713. Sound.\n\n3. **resid50ind closure**: `&& (PSR & IND)` on fallback \u2192 resid50ind (IND clear) skips fallback \u2192 vector 50 delivered to 0x190 \u2192 tail default \u2192 TAILHALT \u2713. Sound. Hmm \u2014 wait: skips fallback \u2192 falls through to the RE-ROUTE (PSR & SFRZ \u2014 SFRZ clear \u2713 no) \u2192 common path \u2192 tail switch \u2192 vector 50 \u2192 default \u2192 internal error \u2192 TAILHALT \u2713.\n\n4. **resid50c closure**: same shape \u2713.\n\n5. **trapfrozen128 closure**: M_SFRZTRAPUSER (`!is_trap || vector >= 128`) \u2192 frozen trap naming 128 re-routed \u2192 0x50, refatal line printed \u2014 wait, the table says \"trapfrozen128 STOP (re-routed to 0x50 instead of 0x400)\". Why STOP not BADREGS bad=refatal? Hmm! The mutant's inner structure: `if (!is_trap) { vector = ERROR; fatal(...); }` \u2014 the mutant as stated is \"re-route `if (!is_trap || vector >= 128)`\" \u2014 if this replaces the INNER if's condition, then for trapfrozen128: inner fires \u2192 vector = ERROR, fatal printed \u2192 0x50. REFATAL_EXPECT[trapfrozen128] = False \u2192 line present \u2192 bad=refatal \u2192 but that requires the ENTRY BREAKPOINT to fire (BADREGS = \"the entry breakpoint fired but ... assert failed\"). Expected entry 0x400; delivery went to 0x50 \u2192 breakpoint at 0x400 never fires \u2192 the probe can't do register asserts \u2192 classified STOP (machine stopped for another reason \u2014 actually hmm, would the machine stop? Delivery to 0x50 \u2192 br . planted \u2192 infinite loop \u2192 probe timeout \u2192 STOP). So STOP \u2713 consistent \u2014 bad=refatal only applies when the breakpoint fired (i.e., same-vector confusions like trapfrozen10). \u2713 Sound.\n\n6. **refatal channel**: sound per premise 3 (fatal is a plain print landing in the transcript). One caveat: the channel asserts on err* rows the line's PRESENCE \u2014 pinned. And on trapfrozen* its ABSENCE. Sound.\n\n7. **class-10 disclosure withdrawn**: sound \u2014 the re-route arm prints, the exempt arm doesn't; the composite now tells them apart (trapfrozen10 refatal + HALT/DELIVER for the switch-1 arm). \u2713.\n\n8. **gate repair**: the three check lines with literal backslash-n between arguments \u2014 \"check() would have compared the wrong pair and failed on correct output. Repaired before any gate ran.\" Hmm \u2014 a gate line with a LITERAL `\\n` between arguments \u2014 in bash, `check \"a\"\\n\"b\"`? The claim: repaired before any gate ran \u2192 no false-green risk. Can't verify from packet; plausible. But note the gate narration's \"four frozen-handler rows at 0x50\" vs five err* rows \u2014 possible miscount in the narration (cosmetic if M88KEXC_RESULT=41/41 is the real pin). Hmm \u2014 actually wait, let me recount: \"the four frozen-handler rows at 0x50\" \u2014 err* rows: error, errovf, errdiv0, errpriv, errunimpl = FIVE, all re-routed to 0x50, all with planted handlers, all frozen-shadow. Four vs five \u2014 DISCREPANCY. Unless one of the five isn't \"frozen-handler\"... they all are (all in the frozen want-dict set). So the gate's narration says four where the composite has five. IF the gate script literally checks four rows (say it greps for DELIVER on error/errovf/errdiv0/errpriv but not errunimpl), then a mutant breaking ONLY errunimpl's delivery... would still fail M88KEXC_RESULT=41/41 (errunimpl red \u2192 40/41). So no coverage loss. Cosmetic. Not the \"WRONG disposition\".\n\n9. **interval wording**: \"the shrink VALUES are 115..127, 128 being the bound itself\" \u2014 deepseek's correction of glm's \"[115,128]\". Hmm: the far-bound shrink mutants: `< 127` (M_FALLBACK127: fallback `vector < 127`) \u2192 vector 127 delivered... wait no: `vector < 127` means 127 is NOT < 127 \u2192 127 skips fallback \u2192 delivered \u2192 red on resid127. The shrink VALUES that escape detection when the bound is shrunk to N: vectors in [N, 127] escape... the wording: \"the shrink VALUES are 115..127\" \u2014 i.e., a mutant `vector < N` for N in 115..127 escapes v8/v9 (since only 114=SFU1... wait, hmm: sfu1h pins 114 at the fallback (114 must HALT). `vector < 115` \u2192 114 < 115 \u2192 114 still falls back \u2713 sfu1h green; 115..127 unrowed \u2192 escape. `vector < 127`: 127 not < 127 \u2192 escapes... but resid127 exists in v8! M_FALLBACK127 29/30 \u2014 caught. Hmm, the sentence \"the shrink VALUES are 115..127, 128 being the bound itself\" refers to glm's pass-5 interval [115,128] of shrink values that would escape \u2014 with v8 (which had resid127), shrinks to 127 are caught; the interval [115,128] was about the v5-era instrument? \"the accept-row-at-the-LAST-slot rule (deepseek and glm, pass 5...)... glm's [115,128] named it--the shrink VALUES are 115..127, 128 being the bound itself: deepseek, pass 6\". OK: the set of N such that `vector < N` sweeps: N in 115..128? If N=128, `vector < 128` = correct \u2192 sweeps trivially (not a mutant). So shrink VALUES (N values that change behavior and escape) = 115..127; 128 is the original bound. The corrected wording is right: [115,128] inclusive of 128 would name N=128 = no-op. \u2713 Sound. Hmm \u2014 wait, but with resid127 now in the instrument (v8 had resid127? yes \u2014 \"M_FALLBACK127 29/30\" and resid127 is a v8 row), N=127 is CAUGHT. So \"shrink values 115..127\" escape only instruments WITHOUT resid127. The wording in the diff is about the pass-5 packet (v5: 29/29 rows). Eh \u2014 the disposition is just wording; fine.\n\n10. **deepseek 0..3 kept disclosed**: vectors 0..3's switch-1 arms unrowed \u2014 hardware events (reset, interrupt, etc.?) \u2014 kept disclosed with cross-fixture m88kexchwtraps. Reasonable; can't row them without another fixture. Sound.\n\nSo the seven dispositions are individually sound-ish, BUT the pass as a whole fails to close because the pass-6 REPAIR's class closure was incomplete \u2014 {guard} x {PSR bit} was closed only on the fallback guard, not the re-route guard. Hmm \u2014 but the question says \"WHICH IS WRONG?\" expecting perhaps one specific disposition. Let me re-examine... Maybe one of the five row closures IS wrong on its own terms. Let me scrutinize trapfrozen10 vs the refatal channel once more:\n\ntrapfrozen10 under CORRECT code: frozen trap naming 10 \u2192 re-route outer (PSR & SFRZ) \u2713 \u2192 inner !is_trap false \u2192 EXEMPT \u2192 vector stays 10 \u2192 common path \u2192 tail: vector 10 \u2192 ERROR arm \u2192 deliver 0x50, no print. \u2713 expected DELIVER 0x50, refatal absent \u2713.\n\nUnder M_ERRORFRZOFF: switch 1's ERROR arm: `implemented = !(PSR & SFRZ)` \u2192 SFRZ set \u2192 implemented=false \u2192 fallback: 10 < 128 \u2713 && !implemented \u2713 \u2192 fallback fires \u2192 HALT naming 0x00a \u2713 table says \"trapfrozen10 HALT (naming 0x00a)\" \u2713.\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, but under M_ERRORFRZOFF, what about the err* rows (they END at vector 10 after the re-route \u2014 but the fallback runs BEFORE the re-route, at which point their vector is still the ORIGINAL class (e.g., 9 OVF) \u2014 implemented=true \u2192 no fallback \u2713; then re-route \u2192 vector=10 \u2192 tail ERROR arm \u2014 the mutant's `implemented` isn't consulted again \u2713 err* green \u2713). And the `error` row: second fault MISALIGNED (class 1?) \u2014 implemented \u2713 \u2192 re-route \u2192 0x50 \u2713 green. And any first-fault ERROR-class row? Is there a row that raises ERROR directly as a first fault? \"BOUNDS and ERROR have no non-trap route\" \u2014 ERROR reachable only via trap naming 10 or re-route. First-fault rows: none raise ERROR. \u2713 So M_ERRORFRZOFF's only red row is trapfrozen10 \u2713 40/41 \u2713.\n\nNow scrutinize **resid11's addition to VECTORS and the `br .` planting**: V_RESID0 = 11 added to VECTORS \u2713 (\"now incl. 11 and 127\" \u2014 premise 5 \u2713). resid11: tb1 0,r3,11 \u2192 supervisor trap naming 11 \u2192 switch 1: class for 11? None (classes 4..10, 0..3 hw) \u2192 not implemented \u2192 fallback: 11 < 128 \u2713 \u2192 HALT naming 0x00b \u2713. Under M_FB_LO (`vector >= 12 &&`): 11 fails the added test... wait the mutant: fallback `if (vector >= 12 && vector < 128 && !implemented)` \u2014 11 \u2192 no fallback \u2192 re-route: SFRZ clear \u2192 common path \u2192 tail switch: vector 11 \u2192 default \u2192 internal error + stop \u2192 TAILHALT \u2713 table \u2713.\n\n**resid50ind/resid50c fault_pc 0x10008** (stcr then tb1: fault_pc 0x10008 = second instruction \u2014 hmm, fault_pc is the expected... the field is \"fault_pc\" = the sxip expected? For trap rows: \"want.update(snip=((fault_pc + 4)...)\" hmm \u2014 fault_pc 0x10008 with the trap at the second word. Fine, mirrors v8's ovfc/indclr shape (0x10008) \u2713.\n\nHmm wait, actually \u2014 resid50ind/resid50c: stcr r5,cr1 with r5=PSR_INDCLR=0x80000000 (MODE set, everything else clear). Then tb1 0,r3,50. The trap: is_trap=1, supervisor \u2713. Fallback: HALT naming 0x032 \u2713. But \u2014 EXPECT_VECTOR[\"resid50ind\"] = 0x032 \u2713 set in the dict \u2713. And EXPECT_RESULT includes both \u2713. And the gate's \"seven HALT rows\" \u2713.\n\nOK so the five closures are sound. The channel is sound. The withdrawn disclosure is sound. The gate repair: hmm \u2014 \"Repaired before any gate ran\" \u2014 but then \"The gate pins M88KEXC_RESULT=41/41...\" \u2014 the gate RAN after repair \u2713. The wording disposition sound. 0..3 sound.\n\nSo \"WHICH IS WRONG?\" \u2014 the best answer: **the fifth row closure's CLASS framing / the pass-6 repair's class closure** \u2014 i.e., what's wrong is not any single disposition but the REPAIR's claim to have closed kimi's class: it closed {fallback} x {PSR bit} and left {re-route} x {PSR bit} open (C and IND one-sided at the re-route; SFD1 accidentally both-sided via errdiv0). Actually \u2014 hmm, the question lists \"five row closures\" as dispositions. If I must pick WHICH disposition is wrong: I'd say NONE of the seven is individually wrong; the wrongness is in the closure claim \u2014 the pass closes only if zero floor breaches survive adjudication, and {re-route} x {C} (and {re-route} x {IND}) is an unadjudicated breach of the very class kimi opened. If the panel wants one named disposition: the \"five row closures\" disposition is wrong AS A CLASS CLOSURE \u2014 resid50ind/resid50c closed the fallback instance; the class ({guard predicate} x {settable PSR bit}) also lives on the re-route's `PSR & SFRZ`, where C (and IND, modulo errdiv0's handler PSR) is single-sided.\n\nWait, wait. Hmm, let me reconsider whether the (d) enumeration really covers the re-route guard's PSR-bit conjunction... \"the re-route's `PSR & SFRZ`\" is explicitly listed as an enumerated predicate. YES. And the measured mutant table varies is_trap and vector on the re-route (M_SFRZTRAP10/USER) but never the SFRZ conjunct's bit family. And kimi's pass-6 class statement: \"the {fallback predicate} x {PSR bit} CLASS: pass 5 closed only the MODE cell; `&& (PSR & IND)` and `&& !(PSR & C)` swept\" \u2014 the pass-6 finding was explicitly about the fallback, and the repair swept the fallback. The re-route's `PSR & SFRZ` guard is a DIFFERENT guard with the SAME class of exposure. The repair-chain rule says a repair closes the CLASS \u2014 the class here, stated generally, is \"{exception-path guard} x {settable PSR bit}\". The pass-6 repair closed the fallback instance only. So pass 7 should find this and order ONE MORE REPAIR.\n\nLet me also double-check the SFRZ bit itself at the fallback vs re-route... the fallback's SFRZ side: sfu1h (SFRZ set, fallback fires) \u2713 and resid rows (SFRZ clear) \u2713 both sides at the FALLBACK. At the re-route: SFRZ is the guard's own bit \u2014 trivially both sides? The guard IS SFRZ; a mutant on the guard's bit operand (swap SFRZ\u2192SFD1) \u2014 analyzed above: caught by usfu1 (SFD1 set, SFRZ clear \u2192 mutant fires \u2192 usfu1 red). \u2713. Swap SFRZ\u2192C: caught by err* (C clear \u2192 mutant guard false \u2192 no re-route \u2192 err* red). \u2713. Swap SFRZ\u2192IND: usfu1 has IND set + SFRZ clear \u2192 mutant fires \u2192 usfu1 red \u2713 caught. Swap SFRZ\u2192MODE: errpriv/errunimpl (user, SFRZ set... wait swap MODE: guard = PSR & MODE: errpriv user \u2192 MODE clear \u2192 guard false \u2192 no re-route \u2192 errpriv red \u2713 caught). So single-bit OPERAND swaps on the re-route guard are all caught \u2014 it's the CONJUNCT variants that sweep: `SFRZ && C-clear` (sweeps: no row has C set at re-route), `SFRZ && IND-set` (sweeps if err* handlers keep IND \u2014 near-certain but not inlined for errdiv0... hmm, actually \u2014 wait, errdiv0's handler \"clears SFD1 first\" \u2014 to clear SFD1 the handler stcr's a PSR value; the natural value keeps IND (0x80000003). But even if it cleared IND too, M_REIND would be caught by errdiv0 \u2014 I'll present M_REC as the certain one and M_REIND as probable).\n\nAlso the DISJUNCT variants on the re-route: `SFRZ || (PSR & C)`?? \u2014 C-set rows with SFRZ clear at the re-route: ovfc's first fault (C set, SFRZ clear) \u2192 mutant guard fires \u2192 ovfc re-routed to 0x50 + line \u2192 ovfc expects DELIVER to OVF's entry 0x48 \u2192 RED, caught \u2713. `SFRZ || (PSR & IND)`: IND-set SFRZ-clear rows at re-route: first-fault rows (div0, ovf, misalign...) \u2014 PSR_RESET has IND \u2192 mutant re-routes ALL first faults \u2192 massive red \u2713. `SFRZ || !(PSR & SFD1)`: SFD1-clear rows at re-route: first-fault rows (PSR_RESET SFD1 clear) \u2192 re-routed \u2192 red \u2713 caught. So only the conjunct-true-on-pattern variants sweep: `&& !(C)` for sure; `&& IND`, `&& SFD1` \u2014 SFD1 caught by errdiv0 (SFD1 clear \u2192 guard false \u2192 errdiv0 red... wait: `&& (PSR & SFD1)`: errdiv0 runs SFD1-CLEAR \u2192 guard false \u2192 no re-route \u2192 errdiv0 delivers 0x40 \u2192 RED \u2713 caught). So: `&& (PSR & IND)` and `&& !(PSR & C)` are the two sweeping conjuncts (IND: set on all re-route rows per packet; C: clear on all re-route rows per packet).\n\nHmm \u2014 also `&& !(PSR & MXM)` and `&& !(PSR & BO)` sweep trivially (no row sets MXM/BO \u2014 disclosed as unsettable, and that disclosure (\"MXM and BO cannot be set by stcr without stopping the cpu -- disclosed\") was made in the FALLBACK context but reads globally \u2014 I'd count MXM/BO as covered by the existing disclosure). The C and IND conjuncts are NOT covered by any disclosure: the disclosed list has no \"{re-route} x {PSR bit}\" item. Clause e covers {MODE} x {SFRZ} x {class} triples for MISALIGNED/OVERFLOW/DIV0 \u2014 not C/IND at the re-route.\n\nHmm, one more candidate sweep to consider: **the fallback's `vector < 128` operand: `128` \u2192 `M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START`** \u2014 same value. **The tail switch's labels for vectors 116..127?** They don't exist (tail switch is per-class; residual vectors hit default). \u2713.\n\n**What about `update_shadow_regs = false` conditioned on something?** `if (PSR & SFRZ) { if (!is_trap) {...} update_shadow_regs = false; }` \u2014 mutant: move `update_shadow_regs = false` inside the !is_trap arm \u2192 trapfrozen* rows get UPDATED shadows \u2192 BADREGS red \u2713 caught (M_SFRZ_TRAP_UPDATE 28/29 \u2014 that's this, caught by trapfrozen rows).\n\n**What about the re-route's `vector = ERROR` \u2014 operand swap to `vector = 10`?** Same. To the trap's own vector? No-op. To BOUNDS (4)? err* red \u2713.\n\n**Switch 1's class labels x {vector 115}?** SFU1_IMPRECISE disclosed.\n\n**What about a switch-1 arm for vector 128 (user trap)?** `case 128: implemented = 1` \u2014 wait, is vector 128 handled by switch 1? Premise 2: fallback `vector < 128 && !implemented` \u2014 usertb (128): if switch 1 doesn't implement 128, the fallback's `vector < 128` excludes it \u2192 delivers to 0x400 via the tail switch's... hmm, the tail switch must have an arm for user traps (or a range). A mutant `case 128: implemented = 0` \u2014 usertb: fallback: 128 < 128 false \u2192 no fallback \u2192 re-route: SFRZ clear \u2192 common \u2192 tail: user-trap arm \u2192 0x400 \u2713 same. Green \u2014 no wait, `implemented` for 128 doesn't matter (the `vector < 128` guard already excludes 128 from the fallback). Equivalent mutant \u2014 fine, not a breach (behaviorally identical).\n\nOK here's another angle \u2014 **the {fallback} x {MXM/BO} disclosure vs the re-route**: already covered.\n\n**What about the HALT classifier's dependence on \"All machines stopped.\"?** M_NOSTOP caught (21/23). The fallback's stop: \"every cpu stopped\" \u2014 a mutant stopping only the CURRENT cpu... caught per M_NOSTOP. \u2713.\n\n**What about the fallback printing BEFORE stopping vs AFTER?** Equivalent in transcript? If a mutant printed after the stop \u2014 the transcript would still have both lines (print then stop vs stop then print \u2014 the print still executes? If the stop is `cpu->running = 0` style, the print after still lands). Equivalent-ish. Not a breach.\n\n**The gate's \"bad=refatal absent\" pin** \u2014 \u2713.\n\nAlright \u2014 let me also sanity-check the claimed v10 red-row classifications once more against premise 5's classifiers (the table):\n- M_ERRORFRZOFF \u2192 trapfrozen10 HALT naming 0x00a \u2713 (fallback path: print + all stopped \u2713 HALT).\n- M_SFRZTRAP10 \u2192 trapfrozen10 BADREGS bad=refatal \u2713 (delivered 0x50 = expected entry \u2192 breakpoint fires \u2192 asserts pass (shadows frozen either way \u2014 hmm wait: under M_SFRZTRAP10, trapfrozen10's trap is re-routed: vector=ERROR (same 10), update_shadow_regs=false (outside inner if) \u2192 shadows frozen \u2713 asserts pass \u2192 only refatal present-but-expected-absent \u2192 bad=refatal \u2713 BADREGS) \u2713 consistent.\n- M_FB_LO \u2192 resid11 TAILHALT \u2713.\n- M_FBIND \u2192 resid50ind TAILHALT \u2713 (50 delivered to 0x190... wait \u2014 \"resid50ind TAILHALT (50 delivered to 0x190)\" \u2014 delivered to 0x190 = vector 50's entry = 8*50 \u2014 then the TAIL switch \u2192 default \u2192 internal error + stop \u2713 TAILHALT \u2713. Hmm \u2014 but 0x190 has br . planted (VECTORS includes 50)! Wait \u2014 the tail default fires BEFORE reaching the entry: the delivery writes sxip etc. and the tail switch's default stops the cpu BEFORE the guest ever executes at 0x190 \u2713 \u2014 the `internal error` + stop happens inside the exception tail \u2713 TAILHALT \u2713 consistent with trapfrozen10/M_SFRZTRAPUSER analysis.)\n- M_FBNOC \u2192 resid50c TAILHALT \u2713.\n- M_SFRZTRAPUSER \u2192 trapfrozen128 STOP \u2713 (delivered 0x50, expected bp at 0x400 never fires; probe sees... hmm, \"STOP = the machine stopped for another reason\" \u2014 the mutant's fatal is a plain print; delivery to 0x50 \u2192 guest runs br . at 0x50 \u2014 machine running... how does it STOP? Hmm \u2014 unless the probe's continue hits the br . self-loop and the debugger... Actually maybe the probe single-steps or has a watchdog. OR: hmm \u2014 wait, maybe under M_SFRZTRAPUSER the INNER if for trapfrozen128: `if (!is_trap || vector >= 128)` \u2014 if this replaces the INNER condition, then vector=ERROR; fatal... but fatal is a plain print, no stop... The machine then delivers to 0x50 (br .) and loops. The probe waits at its breakpoint... The classification \"STOP\" is their measurement; I can't re-derive it fully (the probe's timeout behavior isn't inlined). It's a measured row \u2014 I don't need to re-derive; the row IS red either way (not HALT/DELIVER-0x400). Fine.\n\nAnd HEAD 7/41, fix 41/41 \u2014 measured, fine.\n\nNow, let me also double check the \"**bad=level**\" and \"**exception <CLASS> line**\" bit \u2014 unchanged from v8 \u2713.\n\nAnd the \"**REFATAL_EXPECT**\" dict: it includes exactly 13 rows: 5 err* (True) + 8 trapfrozen* (False). Note the comment line glued to the closing brace: `...trapfrozen128\": False}   # the vector the fallback diagnostic must NAME for an expected-HALT row` \u2014 that comment is a copy-paste leftover from the EXPECT_VECTOR line (it describes EXPECT_VECTOR, not REFATAL_EXPECT). Cosmetic defect in the diff: a misplaced/misdescribed comment. Worth a one-line mention (cosmetic, no behavioral effect).\n\nAlso the diff's line: \"resid11 ... EXPECTED RESULT = HALT naming 0x00b        (0x58)\" \u2014 0x58 = 8*11 \u2713 entry.\n\nAlso: \"trapfrozen10 ... Expect 0x50, shadows frozen 0x50\" \u2713.\n\nAlso check the docstring's changed closure text: \"trapfrozen (7), trapfrozen4/5/", "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 361185689554}
