regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass7_panel_20260906_113427\kimi.txt:156:  Hmm, but is this a genuine floor breach or overreach? Let me compare with the disclosed residuals. The disclosures include: "`implemented = is_trap` at BOUNDS/ERROR (equivalent today, m88ktbnd)" — interesting! That's a disclosed equivalent mutant on switch 1's BOUNDS/ERROR arms: conditioning `implemented` on is_trap. So the authors KNOW the ERROR arm's `implemented` is under-pinned and disclose specific equivalents. "equivalent today, m88ktbnd" — so `implemented = is_trap` at BOUNDS/ERROR is disclosed as equivalent. By the same token, `implemented = !(PSR & C)` at ERROR is equivalent-for-the-probe... but is it "equivalent" for the PRODUCT? For the product, a supervisor trap naming 10 with C set WOULD behave differently (HALT vs deliver). That's a real behavioral difference the probe can't see. The disclosure "equivalent today" for is_trap presumably means: today, only traps reach the BOUNDS/ERROR arms (no non-trap route), so `implemented = is_trap` is observationally identical TO THE PROBE and to any guest (since only traps ever enter those arms... a trap naming 7 or 10 — is_trap is always 1 there → implemented = 1 always → genuinely equivalent in the product!). Yes: BOUNDS/ERROR arms are reachable ONLY by traps (non-trap routes don't exist — premise: "BOUNDS and ERROR have no non-trap route"), so `implemented = is_trap` is a true semantic equivalent in the product. But `implemented = !(PSR & C)` is NOT a product-equivalent: a supervisor trap naming 10 with C set (settable via stcr — ovfc proves C survives stcr + exception) would HALT instead of delivering to 0x50. So it's a genuine mutant that the probe does not kill. Is it in the enumeration? The enumeration: "predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary (switch 1's class labels; ...)". The phrase "switch 1's class labels" — if the enumeration is only about the LABELS (case values), then arm-body conditionings are out of enumeration... but M_ERRORFRZOFF (an arm-body conditioning) WAS enumerated, measured, found by codex in pass 6, and closed in this repair. So arm-body predicates are in the enumeration de facto. The closure list in the packet claims: "switch 1's eligibility per class incl. ERROR under SFRZ (M_ERRORFRZOFF 40/41)". It says "incl. ERROR under SFRZ" — it pins the ERROR arm's eligibility under SFRZ only. The {class} x {PSR bit} closure for switch 1 arms: for other classes, is `implemented` pinned against PSR-bit conditioning both ways? Consider class 4 (MISALIGNED) arm: `implemented = !(PSR & SFRZ)` — rows entering class 4 at switch 1: misalign (SFRZ clear, first fault), umisalign (user, SFRZ clear), error (second fault, SFRZ set? no wait — "error" row: the SECOND fault is a misaligned access with SFRZ set → re-routed to ERROR. Does it enter switch 1 as MISALIGNED (4)? The second fault's vector is 4 at switch-1 time (re-route happens AFTER the fallback, which is after switch 1). So yes: the "error" row enters switch 1 as MISALIGNED with SFRZ set. So `implemented = !(PSR & SFRZ)` on the MISALIGNED arm → error row's second fault → implemented = 0 → fallback → HALT naming 0x004 → "error" row expects DELIVER at 0x50 → RED. Caught! So for class 4, SFRZ conditioning IS pinned (by error). And that's exactly what the {SFRZ set, non-trap} x {class} rows (err*) do — they ALSO pin switch 1's per-class eligibility under SFRZ, in addition to the re-route. And trapfrozen* pin it for the trap route per class. So for classes 4..9, `implemented = !(PSR & SFRZ)` is caught twice. For class 10, only trapfrozen10 catches it — and it does (SFRZ set at entry). 
