regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass7_panel_20260906_113427\kimi.txt:215:  But wait — is `implemented = !(PSR & C)` on the ERROR arm within the (d) enumeration? The enumeration as defined: "predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary". The #502 fix's edited lines in cpu_m88k.c — did the fix touch switch 1's ERROR arm? The composite is "#502 fix + probe v10". The (d) enumeration is of the composite. Hmm, I don't have the #502 fix diff. The enumeration parenthetical lists: "switch 1's class labels; the fallback's `vector < 128 && !implemented`; the re-route's `PSR & SFRZ` and `is_trap`; the tail switch's labels". If switch 1's contribution to the enumeration is only its LABELS, then arm-body conditioning is outside the (d) enumeration — and M_ERRORFRZOFF would have been outside too... but codex found it in pass 6 and it was treated as a legitimate cell ("switch 1's ERROR arm is its own predicate"). So arm bodies ARE in the enumeration as predicates. Then `implemented = !(PSR & C)` on the ERROR arm is a predicate mutant on an edited line → in enumeration → and it sweeps clean → FLOOR BREACH?
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass7_panel_20260906_113427\kimi.txt:223:  Hmm OK, but now — how does this compare to the pass-6 codex cell? Codex's cell: `implemented = !(PSR & SFRZ)` on the ERROR arm — a frozen trap naming 10 (SFRZ set) → fallback → HALT. Closed by trapfrozen10 (SFRZ set at entry). The repair closed the SFRZ cell but NOT the C cell. Is the C cell "in the (d) enumeration"? If the enumeration is "predicates dominating the edited lines" — the line `case M88K_EXCEPTION_ERROR: implemented = 1;` — mutating its body to condition on any PSR bit is the same mutant family as M_ERRORFRZOFF. The pass-6 closure rationale ("switch 1's ERROR arm is its own predicate") implies the family is in-enumeration. The repair pinned SFRZ only. C, MXM, BO unpinned. MXM/BO: not settable → the mutant `!(PSR & MXM)` is untestable-equivalent... in the PRODUCT, MXM can't be set by stcr without stopping the cpu — but can MXM be set another way? It's a PSR bit; the exception path... whatever — disclosed as not settable. But C is settable and unpinned.
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass7_panel_20260906_113427\kimi.txt:235:  Whoa. So `implemented = !(PSR & C)` on ANY switch-1 arm except OVF sweeps clean on v10. That's a whole FAMILY of clean-sweep mutants. Hmm. But wait — is this within the (d) enumeration as the pass defines it? This is the crux. The enumeration: "predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary (switch 1's class labels; ...)". 
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass7_panel_20260906_113427\kimi.txt:239:  Hmm, hmm, hmm. But is this over-enumeration? Every pass has the (d) rule. The question is what "the body's own tests distinguish" — that's for JOINT cells. Single-predicate conditionings: the enumeration says "predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary". A conditioning `implemented = !(PSR & C)` introduces a NEW predicate on an edited line. M_ERRORFRZOFF set the precedent that this family is in-enumeration. So I'd have to say: YES, clean-sweep mutants exist: M_ERRORARM_NOC (and its siblings on MISALIGNED/PRIVILEGE/BOUNDS/UNIMPL/DIV0 arms; OVF is pinned by ovfc).
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass7_panel_20260906_113427\kimi.txt:274:  Let me think about what the authors would consider in-enumeration, because that decides it. The (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration: "predicates dominating the diff's edited lines up to the function boundary (switch 1's class labels; the fallback's `vector < 128 && !implemented`; the re-route's `PSR & SFRZ` and `is_trap`; the tail switch's labels), operand swaps drawn only from fields the guarded bodies read, and joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them."
