regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass7_panel_20260906_113427\kimi.txt:338:  So by precedent (M_FBIND, M_FBNOC enumerated), PSR-bit conjuncts on DOMINATING PREDICATES are in-enumeration. Is switch 1's arm body a "predicate dominating the edited lines"? The arm body doesn't dominate anything (it's a leaf: `implemented = 1; break;`). But M_ERRORFRZOFF converted it into a predicate, and it was counted. Precedent says yes.
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass7_panel_20260906_113427\kimi.txt:357:  - Re-route `is_trap`: polarity + per-class + user-trap — caught via trapfrozen*/refatal. The `|| vector >= 129` far-bound variant: hmm — the enumerated cell was the is_trap guard; conditioning it on the vector bound with an UNROWED range (129+)... is 129+ distinguishable by the body's tests? No. Joint cell {is_trap} x {vector≥N}: tests distinguish N=128 (trapfrozen128) but not N>128. So under "joint cells where the body's own tests distinguish them", only N=128 is enumerated. The N>128 variants fall outside the enumeration → properly a DISCLOSURE candidate, and the packet discloses... hmm, it doesn't disclose user-trap 129+ frozen traps explicitly. But — product-wise, is `!is_trap || vector >= 129` even wrong? The correct code exempts ALL traps from the re-route. A frozen trap naming 200 SHOULD be exempt. The mutant re-routes it. Guest-visible? A guest taking a frozen user trap naming 200 → delivered to ERROR instead of 0x640. Yes guest-visible. Undisclosed. Hmm. But is it in-enumeration? Under the joint-cell clause, no (tests don't distinguish). Under "predicates dominating the edited lines", the `is_trap` guard is one predicate; adding `|| vector >= 129` is a new conjunct — the M_SFRZTRAPUSER precedent (adding `|| vector >= 128`) was enumerated... and caught. If `>= 128` is enumerated, why not `>= 129`? The difference: tests distinguish one, not the other. The joint-cell clause seems designed exactly for this: enumeration includes the cells tests can distinguish; the rest must be disclosed or filed. Not disclosed → gap in the contract, but not strictly a "(d) enumeration" breach if the clause excludes it. Meh. This is the kind of thing adjudication would decide.
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass7_panel_20260906_113427\kimi.txt:369:  Hmm, hmm. But OK, wait — now I should ask: does the (d) enumeration REALLY include `implemented = (PSR & SFRZ)`? It's a predicate on the arm body. The arm body is an edited line (if the fix wrote it). The mutant space "predicates dominating the edited lines" — the arm body doesn't dominate; it IS an edited line. The enumeration says "predicates dominating the diff's edited lines UP TO THE FUNCTION BOUNDARY" — i.e., start from each edited line, walk UP the dominator tree to the function boundary, collecting predicates: switch 1's switch(vector)/case labels, the fallback if, the re-route ifs, the tail switch. Under THAT reading, the enumerated predicates are the DOMINATORS of the edited lines — the case labels dominate the arm bodies! So for an edited arm body, the dominating predicates are: the switch-1 case label, and the function entry. The arm body itself is not a predicate. Mutants = mutations of those dominating predicates + operand swaps in the bodies + joint cells. Under this reading, M_ERRORFRZOFF (conditioning the arm BODY on SFRZ) is... a body mutation, not a dominating-predicate mutation. Was it in-enumeration? Pass 6 treated it as a valid breach ("switch 1's ERROR arm is its own predicate"). Hmm — but maybe pass 6's codex finding was accepted as a VALID breach even though it's technically a body-mutation, because the body's mutation is observationally equivalent to a dominating-predicate mutation (dropping the ERROR arm for frozen entries). I.e., `implemented = !(PSR & SFRZ)` on the arm ≈ making the case label conditional. Fine — either way it was counted.
