regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass2_20260908\seats\ollama_glm.txt:85:2. (type-0, bus==0, write): any row? The ten rows: bus ctl, bus ctlsig, bus kill, bus attrib, t1 alive, t1 anchor, t1 alias, t0 alive, t0 real, t0 accept. `bus kill` = "an accepted stq 1 then an ordinary ldq" — stq 1 is to IOC_CONF (the lca_ioc handler, not this window). The ldq is in the config window at ioc_conf=1 → that's a TYPE-1 read. Hmm. So which row exercises a TYPE-0 WRITE to the config window? Let me scan: t1 alive = "a type-1 write to a NON-zero bus must not end the host" — type-1 write, bus≠0 → forwarded → empty slot → fatal() diagnostic (no exit). t1 anchor = type-1 write landing at bus 1 dev 3 fn 2 (the diagnostic). t1 alias = type-1 write with bus 0 → refused (write discarded). t0 rows: "t0 alive: the type-0 path still reaches a registered device", t0 real, t0 accept — reads presumably (t0 accept checks the ldq value 0x304848086 → a read). So type-0 WRITE to the config window: NO ROW directly. Does it matter? Pre-fix, a type-0 write to a registered device would go to bus_pci_data_access with dev from the IDSEL scan → the i82378zb's write handler. Post-fix identical code path (the else branch calls the same two lines). The type-0 forward path is shared between read and write; a write to dev 7 would hit the device's own write handling. Not rowed. Is it owed? The diff didn't change the type-0 path's forwarding semantics; it changed the variable name (bus==0 → !type1) and the shift UB (#511 already). Hmm, but the refusal arm: type-0 never enters it. So type-0 write behavior is unchanged by construction. But "unchanged by construction" is an argument, not a measurement — the vacuity floor wants rows. Is (type-0, write) a cell in the induced partition? The body's own tests induce {type-0/type-1} × {bus==0/!=0} × {read/write}. The author's Q1 says the unrowed cells are (type-1, bus!=0, read) and (type-1, bus==0, read). Let me check (type-1, bus!=0, read): rows exercising type-1 bus≠0: t1 alive (write), t1 anchor (write). Both writes. A type-1 READ to bus 1 → forwarded → empty slot → read: reg==0 → all-ones, else 0. That's the same answer the refusal arm gives for (type-1, bus==0, read) — and the same as `bus ctlsig` (type-0 empty slot read: all-ones at reg 0). So indeed, a read oracle can't distinguish: forwarded-empty vs refused-empty vs type-0-empty all give all-ones at reg 0. The author asks "Am I right that they are indistinguishable, or is there a channel I have missed?"
regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass2_20260908\seats\ollama_glm.txt:125:The de-escalation clause: the diff deletes exit(1) — every predicate that gated death owes an accept row. Rows shipped: t0 accept (type-0 registered device, exact value), t1 anchor (type-1 to bus 1 forwards, right tuple). Missing: type-1 to a REGISTERED device — impossible on this machine (P3: only 0:7:0 registered, and type-1 with bus≠0 → bus 1 has no devices; type-1 with bus 0 → refused, not forwarded). So the forwarding-to-registered-device state is UNCONSTRUCTIBLE without adding a device — which the ladder forbids (adding a device to reach a site = laundering). Is "no such state constructible" acceptable? My analysis: the exit(1) that was deleted was reached for ALL type-1 (pre-fix `bus != 0` = any ioc_conf bit 0 set). The accept rows needed: prove that accesses that USED TO DIE now complete, and prove legal traffic still lands. t1 alive + t1 anchor cover type-1-to-nonzero-bus completing and forwarding. The "type-1 to a registered device" cell: pre-fix it died (exit), post-fix it would forward — but the state can't exist. Actually wait — can it? Type-1 with bus≠0, dev matching a registered device on bus≠0 — no devices on bus≠0. Type-1 with bus==0 is refused (never reaches a device). So the "type-1 → registered device" behavior is unreachable BY CONSTRUCTION of the refusal. The forwarding path (setaddr + data_access with type-1-derived bus/dev/func) IS exercised by t1 anchor (to an empty tuple — data_access's dev==NULL arm). The dev!=NULL arm of data_access for a type-1-derived tuple: unexercised. But the dev!=NULL arm is exercised by t0 real/t0 accept (type-0-derived tuple 0:7:0). The factors: (setaddr with bus=1/dev=3/func=2) proven by anchor's printed tuple; (data_access dev!=NULL) proven by t0 accept; the composition (type-1-derived tuple hitting a registered device) is not constructible. So the forwarding path is tested factor-wise, not compositionally. Verdict: the disclosure is acceptable, PROVIDED it's disclosed as exactly that: "type-1→registered is unconstructible on this machine; the path is held factor-wise (anchor: tuple decode + setaddr; t0 accept: data_access dev!=NULL arm)." And note: adding a device to construct the state WOULD be laundering — the author is right. Alternative that ISN'T laundering: a mutant test (mutate the refusal predicate to also refuse bus≠0 — t1 anchor goes red ✓ that's the mutant coverage). Actually the right instrument for "forwarding works where it matters" is not an accept row but a MUTANT: kill the else branch (make all type-1 refused) → t1 anchor must go red. The packet doesn't list mutants for THIS round (mutant 1 mentioned in §F — "cmp-clean before mutant 1" — but no mutant results table!). Hmm — §F mentions "before mutant 1" implying mutants were run, but NO mutant results are inlined. That's a gap worth naming: the packet shows pre/post but no mutant table; for vacuity-floor purposes, the mutant set (e.g., `type1 && bus == 0` → `type1`, `bus == 0` → `0`, else-branch removal) would prove the rows' sensitivity. The author asked Q1/Q4 about fragility — mutants are the standard answer for oracle sensitivity when cells are indistinguishable. I'll name: the mutant table is not inlined; the single fact that would settle row sensitivity is the mutant run results (e.g., mutant on the refusal predicate → alias red; mutant on the else-branch → anchor red). Actually — wait, the brief says "Building and running a single detector or mutant in your own temp directory is fine and encouraged" — but I can't build anything (no repo). I just name it.
regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass2_20260908\seats\ollama_glm.txt:184:- (type-0, bus 0, write): NOT HELD by any row. Behavior unchanged by the diff (same setaddr+data_access lines), but unrowed. Is it owed? The vacuity floor asks which cells the ten rows hold — the author's Q1 asks specifically about the two type-1 read cells. But (type-0, write) is ALSO unrowed! Let me double check: any row doing a type-0 write to the config window? bus kill's stq is to IOC_CONF (different register, lca_ioc handler, not this window). t1 alive/anchor/alias are type-1 writes. So no type-0 config-window write in the row set. Hmm — but wait: does the fix touch the type-0 write path? The else branch (forward) is shared; pre-fix type-0 write to a registered device → data_access write to i82378zb; post-fix identical. Unchanged. But the vacuity floor (clause (d)) as stated by the author: "which cells the ten rows hold and which they do not" — so the honest enumeration: (type-0, write) is unrowed too, and the author's Q1 list ("the unrowed cells are (type-1, bus!=0, read) and (type-1, bus==0, read)") MISSES (type-0, bus 0, write). Three unrowed cells, not two! Actually hold on — also (type-1, bus==0, WRITE) — held by t1 alias (the alias IS a type-1 bus-0 write) ✓ held. (type-1, bus!=0, write) held by t1 alive + anchor ✓. (type-1, bus==0, read): unrowed ✓ author right. (type-1, bus!=0, read): unrowed ✓ author right (and genuinely indistinguishable given no devices on bus≠0). (type-0, bus 0, read): held ✓. (type-0, bus 0, write): UNROWED — author's enumeration misses it. Is that a real gap? The type-0 write path pre-fix vs post-fix: pre-fix `if (bus == 0)` → scan → setaddr → data_access(write). Post-fix `if (!type1)` → same scan → same setaddr → same data_access. Identical modulo the variable rename and the (already-shipped #511) shift fix. A regression here would need the rename to break something — e.g., if `bus` were accidentally clobbered... In the post-fix code, `bus = 0;` before the if; type-0 leaves it 0 ✓. So type-0 write behavior is preserved by inspection. But the floor wants a row. The author asked "Am I right that they [the two type-1 read cells] are indistinguishable" — I should answer: you're right about those two (with the dev-7 refinement), but your enumeration is INCOMPLETE: (type-0, write) is a third unrowed cell, and it's rowable (write to dev 7 config and read back / check no death + no misrouting... actually a type-0 write to the registered device MUTATES the device config — the probe could write a config register (e.g., command reg — careful, could break the machine) or write to an empty type-0 tuple (dev 5, say) and observe the diagnostic-with-bus-0. Simplest row: type-0 write to an EMPTY tuple → expect the bus_pci diagnostic with `bus 0` (proves forwarding of type-0 writes with the right bus), plus alive. That's constructible without mutating registered state. So the third unrowed cell IS rowable and the author's Q1 enumeration missed it.
regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass2_20260908\seats\ollama_glm.txt:306:Let me now also think about whether there are any OTHER rows the fix's behavior space needs per the vacuity floor "clause (d)" — the author defined the closure as the two predicates' partitions. I'll answer within their frame and add the third cell. I think that's the complete realizable cross-product: type-0 (bus forced 0) × {r,w} and type-1 × {bus0, bus≠0} × {r,w} = 2 + 4 = 6 realizable cells. Held strongly: (t0, r) ✓ [4 rows], (t1, b≠0, w) ✓ [alive+anchor], (t1, b0, w) weakly ✓ [alias, never-ran hole]. Unrowed: (t1, b≠0, r) [unrowable on this machine], (t1, b0, r) [ROWABLE at dev 7 — my recommendation], (t0, w) [rowable via empty-tuple write].
regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass2_20260908\seats\ollama_glm.txt:682:- (type-0, bus=0, write): Hmm. Is there a row that does a type-0 WRITE? The rows: bus ctl (read at ioc_conf=0), bus ctlsig (read), bus kill (stq 1 to IOC_CONF then ldq — the stq is to IOC_CONF, not to the config window; the ldq is the config-window access, a read), bus attrib, t1 alive (type-1 write to non-zero bus), t1 anchor (type-1 write), t1 alias (type-1 write? "the alias write" — the author says "the alias write NEVER EXECUTED", so yes, a write), t0 alive (read), t0 real (read), t0 accept (read). So type-0 WRITE to the config window: NOT covered by any row! Unless bus kill's ldq... no, that's a read. Hmm — does any row write to the config window on the type-0 path? "bus kill" writes 1 to IOC_CONF (a different register, the lca_ioc CONF register, not the config window). So the cell (type-0, bus=0, write) is UNROWED. Wait — but is it *at risk* from this diff? The diff changed the condition `bus == 0` → `!type1`, where for type-0, bus==0 and !type1 both true — same branch. And the forwarding call for type-0 is unchanged. But the diff DID touch the enclosing lines; a mutation in the `if (!type1)` predicate, or in the refusal predicate `type1 && bus == 0`, could affect type-0 writes only if it misroutes them into the refusal arm (then writes would be silently discarded — a type-0 write to dev 7 would be dropped, host alive, no diagnostic — would any row catch that? t0 rows are reads. The `t1 alive`/`t1 anchor` rows are type-1 writes. So a mutant that, say, inverted the refusal predicate to `!type1 && bus == 0` (i.e., refuses type-0 instead of type-1-bus-0) would: type-0 reads at dev 7 → refused → all-ones at reg 0. Would `t0 real` catch it? "t0 real: ACCEPT: dev 7 is neither empty-slot nor unmapped" — reads dev 7 reg 0 and checks the value is the device's (0x04848086), not all-ones. So yes, caught by READ. A mutant refusing type-0 WRITES but not reads? The refusal arm sets odata based on reg; for writes, odata irrelevant, the write is just not forwarded. To catch a dropped type-0 write you need a WRITE oracle: write to dev 7, then read back and check the write landed (e.g., write a command-register bit then read it back). No row does that. Hmm, but wait — is that within the diff's blast radius? The diff's refusal arm is new code; a mutant `if (type1 || bus == 0)`? For type-0, bus==0 always → ALL type-0 accesses refused → reads caught by t0 real/accept. A mutant `if (!type1 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE)`? That refuses type-0 writes specifically → NOT caught by any row (no type-0 write oracle). Is such a mutant plausible under the ladder? The vacuity floor question asks: which cells do the ten rows hold. So (type-0, write) is an unrowed reachable cell — the author didn't list it! The author claims the unrowed cells are (type-1, bus≠0, read) and (type-1, bus==0, read). But (type-0, write) is also unrowed, and it IS reachable and arguably in the blast radius (the refusal arm's predicate reads type1, bus, and the else-branch forwards writeflag — a predicate mutation can turn on writeflag). Hmm, but hold on — is the type-0 write cell within the DEPENDS-ON closure of the diff? The diff's changed lines: the declaration, the type1/bus assignment, the if(!type1) predicate, the else arm (bus/dev decode), the refusal predicate + refusal body, and the moved forwarding call. The forwarding call itself is unchanged but now sits inside an else. A mutant of the refusal predicate is in the closure. So yes, (type-0, bus=0, write) is in the closure and unrowed. The author's enumeration MISSED it. That's a real finding.
regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass2_20260908\seats\ollama_glm.txt:691:So my Q1 answer: the author's enumeration MISSES the (type-0, bus=0, write) cell — reachable, in the closure (the refusal predicate guards the forwarding of writes; a predicate mutant keyed on writeflag or an inverted type1 test drops type-0 writes silently), and NO row holds it. The ten rows include NO type-0 config-window write at all. That's the real vacuity-floor gap. And the author's two "indistinguishable" read cells: correct as to value, given P3; but note bus kill already liveness-covers (type-1, read) at whatever address it uses — the packet doesn't say which, so I can't confirm which bus. Name that.
regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass2_20260908\seats\ollama_glm.txt:697:But hold on — could there be a concern that printing on every type-0 write to an empty slot is expected pre-fix too? Yes — pre-fix, type-0 writes forwarded identically. So this row would be ok/ok pre and post — a pure ACCEPT/regression row, which is exactly what the de-escalation clause (Q3) is about! Q3 asks "are the accept rows complete?" — the author shipped t0 accept and t1 anchor. The missing type-0 WRITE accept row is precisely a de-escalation gap: the diff DELETES exit(1) — wait, actually the diff deletes `fatal("TODO. Non-zero bus.\n"); exit(1);`. Pre-fix, a type-0 write... pre-fix, with ioc_conf=0, type-0 writes forwarded (same as post). The exit(1) only fired for ioc_conf=1. So type-0 writes were never killed pre-fix. Hmm, so is a type-0 write accept row owed by the de-escalation clause? The clause: "every predicate that used to gate death owes an accept row proving legal traffic still lands." The predicate gating death was `bus == 0` (i.e., ioc_conf&1 == 0) — inverted: death when bus≠0. The diff REPLACED that predicate with !type1 and redefined the death case into refusal/forwarding. Legal traffic on the previously-safe side (type-0) includes writes. The diff wraps the forwarding call in a NEW predicate (`type1 && bus == 0`) — so writes now pass through a new gate. A vacuity-floor audit of the new gate needs both read and write on each side. So yes, owed — by the vacuity floor (Q1) rather than strictly the de-escalation clause, but owed.
regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass2_20260908\seats\ollama_glm.txt:703:Hmm, but wait — one more consideration on Q3: "every predicate that used to gate death owes an accept row proving legal traffic still lands." The pre-fix death gate: `else { fatal("TODO..."); exit(1); }` — reached when ioc_conf&1 == 1. Post-fix, that case splits into refusal (bus==0) and forwarding (bus≠0). "Legal traffic that used to die" — was ANY of it legal? Pre-fix, ioc_conf=1 + any window access = death. Post-fix: type-1 bus≠0 accesses forward (previously died) — rowed by t1 alive + t1 anchor (write). Type-1 bus=0 → refused (previously died) — rowed by t1 alias (write) + bus kill (read, liveness). Type-1 reads at bus≠0: previously died, now forward — rowed? NO ROW is a type-1 bus≠0 read! Pre-fix, ANY ioc_conf=1 access died. Post-fix, a type-1 bus≠0 read forwards and returns empty-slot data. Is there a row? t1 alive is a write; t1 anchor is a write. Bus kill is a read but at (probably) bus 0 — unverified address. So the "legal traffic still lands" for type-1 bus≠0 READS is unrowed. But indistinguishable by value anyway (as established). The de-escalation clause strictly read: the death gate was `ioc_conf & 1 != 0`; every input class that used to die needs a row showing the new disposition. Classes: type-1 × {bus 0, bus≠0} × {read, write}. Rowed: bus0-write (alias), bus0-read (kill, liveness only), bus≠0-write (alive+anchor), bus≠0-read: UNROWED. So yes — a fourth cell of the de-escalation is missing too, same as the Q1 gap on the read side, plus my new type-0-write gap on the vacuity side. Hmm, but is bus≠0-read at risk of death post-fix? The forwarding path on read: bus_pci_setaddr + bus_pci_data_access dev==NULL → returns. No kill. So the risk is a REGRESSION mutant reintroducing a kill on that path — e.g., a mutant that restores exit(1) in the else arm (the type-1 decode arm): `bus = ...; dev = ...; ` — a mutant that puts exit back somewhere. The vacuity floor wants each cell rowed so mutants can't survive. A type-1 bus≠0 READ row is constructible: set ioc_conf=1, read at the anchor address (0x234000) — a READ there → forwarded → empty slot → all-ones at reg 0. But that's value-identical to refusal... yet the LIVENESS is the point: a mutant that kills on type-1 (e.g., restores the fatal/exit in the else arm of the decode) would kill on the WRITE rows too (t1 alive/anchor catch it). Hmm — a mutant that kills only on READ? Contrived. The author's position (read cells indistinguishable, covered by write-channel equivalents) is defensible for bus≠0, since the decode is shared between read and write (the decode happens before the refusal predicate; writeflag only matters at the forwarding call). A kill-mutant in the decode arm doesn't care about writeflag. So the write rows do cover the decode arm for kills. The only read-specific risk is the refusal arm's VALUE (odata = reg==0 ? -1 : 0) — a mutant `odata = 0` unconditionally, or `odata = (uint64_t)-1` unconditionally — caught by? A type-1 bus-0 READ at reg≠0 vs reg 0... bus kill reads at some address (unknown reg). ctlsig is type-0. So refusal-arm value mutants: UNCAUGHT. Is the refusal value load-bearing? It emulates the empty-slot answer so the guest sees "nothing there". A mutant returning 0 at reg 0 would look like "unmapped" rather than "empty slot" — the ctlsig row distinguishes empty-slot from unmapped for the type-0 path, but the same distinction for the type-1 refusal path is unrowed. Hmm — is that within the closure? The refusal body `odata = reg == 0? (uint64_t) -1 : 0;` is a new line in the diff — squarely in the closure. Mutant: `odata = 0;` (drop the reg==0 test). Rows: bus kill (liveness — passes), t1 alias (write — odata unused on write — passes). ALL TEN PASS. So that mutant survives — the refusal arm's read VALUE is unverified by any row. The author half-sees this ("a read oracle cannot see them") but frames it as indistinguishability between forwarding and refusal — the sharper point is that the refusal arm's own value is mutant-vacuous. Can a read oracle see it AT ALL, given forwarding-to-empty returns the same? The only way: forward the type-1 bus-0 read to the primary bus where a REGISTERED device might answer differently — i.e., compare refusal (-1) vs misrouting-to-dev-7 (0x04848086 at reg 0!). THAT's the channel! The alias row uses bus 0 dev 0 func 0 (empty). If the alias address were crafted to decode (under the MISROUTING hypothesis) to dev 7 — i.e., a type-1 address whose dev field = 7, func = 0, reg = 0, bus = 0 — then: correct fix → refused → all-ones; misrouting mutant → forwarded to 0:7:0 → returns 0x04848086 ≠ all-ones. A READ oracle CAN see the alias misrouting if the aliased target is the REGISTERED device! The author's alias at dev 0 func 0 can only distinguish via the write-diagnostic channel. But an alias READ at (type-1, bus=0, dev=7, func=0, reg=0) — tag = (7<<11)|(0<<8) = 0x3800, relative_addr = 0x3800<<5 = 0x70000 — would return all-ones under the fix and 0x04848086 under a misrouting mutant (and 0x04848086 pre-fix... no wait, pre-fix it dies). So (type-1, bus=0, read) IS oracle-able — contrary to the author's claim that "a read oracle cannot see them"! The author's claim "both of which return the same all-ones value as the control" assumes the comparison is against an EMPTY slot; but the discriminating comparison for the bus-0 refusal is against the REGISTERED device (0:7:0), because THAT's what a misrouting would hit — the author's own comment says exactly this: "Forwarding this case would set cur_bus = 0 and hand the access to whichever primary-bus device matched — i82378zb is registered at 0:7:0". So the author's own misrouting hypothesis names the target 0:7:0, and a read at the alias-to-0:7:0 address separates refusal (-1) from misrouting (0x04848086). The author MISSED this channel. That's a strong finding for Q1: the cells are NOT indistinguishable; the right probe address makes the read channel discriminating. 
regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass2_20260908\seats\ollama_glm.txt:811:**Now — mutant demonstration for (t0,w):** `if (type1 && bus == 0)` → mutant `if (!type1 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` (refuse type-0 writes). Ten rows: all reads unaffected; t1 writes unaffected (type1 → not !type1). t0 rows are reads. → 10/10 PASS on the mutant. The mutant drops every type-0 config write on this machine — including writes a NetBSD guest would make to the i82378zb during configuration. REAL regression class, uncaught. That's the vacuity floor breach. I'm confident in this. Alternatively even simpler mutant: `bus_pci_data_access(... writeflag == MEM_READ? &odata : &idata ...)` — hmm, unchanged. Or the refusal arm swallowing `else` incorrectly... The cleanest is the predicate mutant. Also the refusal-value mutant `odata = 0` (drop the reg==0 ternary): all rows pass (no row reads a type-1 value). Both mutants survive → two unrowed cells confirmed mutant-vacant.
regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass2_20260908\seats\ollama_glm.txt:831:Verdict: the fix's logic is sound from the packet's premises (the decode arithmetic checks out end-to-end; the refusal matches the cited contract), but the row set has a real vacuity-floor hole — (type-0, write) is unrowed and mutant-vacant — and the Q1 indistinguishability claim is half wrong: (type-1, bus=0, read) IS oracle-able via an alias onto the registered device 0:7:0, which the author's own misrouting comment points at but the alias row doesn't probe. Also the acceptance/disposition questions: disclose the hardware master-abort divergence; latched announce for the refused write is the parity-restoring choice.
regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass2_20260908\seats\ollama_glm.txt:897:VERDICT LINE: something like — "VERDICT: FIX LOGIC CONFIRMED FROM THE PACKET (decode arithmetic re-enumerated, holds), but the row set does NOT yet meet its own vacuity floor: (type-0, write) is reachable, in the closure, and unrowed — two named mutants survive all ten rows — and the Q1 'read oracle cannot see them' claim is half wrong: (type-1, bus=0, read) is value-discriminable at the registered-device alias (tag 0x3800) that the author's own misrouting comment points at. Q2: disclose the §6.2.18 divergence; latched announce on the refused write restores sibling parity. Q3: disclosure acceptable given P3; constructible (type-0, write) accept row still owed. Q4: coupling is fail-safe (false-red only); no better channel exists given P2/P3 — but the alias row can be freed of it via the value channel."
