2 caught \u2713. Both \u2192 E \u2713 measured control \u2713. Good \u2014 the flood channel catches one-sided translations \u2713.\n\n- `d->warned |= (1u << bit)` \u2192 `d->warned |= (1u << bit)` \u2014 the parens \u2192 n/a.\n\nSo helper coverage is good; the five new rows (rb2 etc.) don't target the helper \u2014 the helper edits are caught by latch rows \u2713. The brief says \"part 4 measures the five mutants\" \u2014 pass-4's five rows \u2713 (latch aba, latch same2, latch all d, acc pscale ign, acc ipl ro) \u2713.\n\nHmm wait \u2014 the brief's pass-4 section: \"This diff adds five rows\" \u2014 pass-4 delta 96\u2192101 = 5 \u2713: hrd * nod \u00d76 + wrd sccerr nod + rb t2ctl/t2icr/scctx+dcl \u00d76 + hwr sccerr dcl + rb genctl lo + latch all/lv + op rows... no wait \u2014 96 was AFTER pass-2's 82\u219296? Hmm: 60 \u2192 78 (repair) \u2192 82 (repair) \u2192 96 (pass-2 rows? The \"96-row instrument, md5 72759b4f\" is \"the shipped probe\" for part 3 \u2014 96 rows at pass 3). 96 \u2192 101 (pass-4 rows: latch aba, latch same2, latch all d, acc pscale ign, acc ipl ro = 5 \u2713). 101 \u2192 118 (pass-5 rows: 9 rb2 + pscale noig2 + ipl noig2 + 4 hrd rb + hrd ipl noro + latch all fail = 17 \u2713). \u2713 \"Detector 101 -> 118\" \u2713 header \u2713. And the header says \"product diff is comments plus the typedef bound\" \u2713 matches the inlined product diff \u2713 (comments + typedef only \u2014 no behavior change \u2713 GOOD: the product diff is inert, so no row should move \u2713 and BASE 118/118 \u2713 consistent).\n\n**Now the PRETABLE honesty question (Q4):** \"PRETABLE.out was restored to Sections 0-4 and its amendments moved to a second file because appending had destroyed its mtime discriminator: is the split honest as written?\" \u2014 From the packet: PRETABLE_amendments.out holds 5-7; PRETABLE_pass4.out holds 8; PRETABLE_pass5.out holds 9-10. The stated reason: mtime as order witness \u2014 appending destroys it (an appended file has one mtime; separate files have distinct mtimes). The split: amendments 5-7 in one file (which has ONE mtime for three sections written at different times! \u2014 Section 5 written after part 1/before part 2; Section 6 after pass 3/before part 3; Section 7 after part 3). So PRETABLE_amendments.out ITSELF destroys the intra-file order witness for 5 vs 6 vs 7 \u2014 the very defect the split was made to avoid, recreated one level down (the packet ADMITS this for the pass-4 append: \"appending Section 7 there had re-created the very mtime defect Section 6 was split out to avoid \u2014 a pass-4 seat caught it\" \u2014 wait, re-read: \"PRE-TABLE AMENDMENT, PASS 4 -- its own file so its mtime is its own order witness for part 4. (PRETABLE_amendments.out holds Sections 5-7; appending Section 8 there had re-created the very mtime defect Section 6 was split out to avoid -- a pass-4 seat caught it.)\" \u2014 so Section 8 got its own file because appending it to amendments would re-create the defect. But amendments 5-7 sit in ONE file \u2014 was THAT file written once (all three sections at once)? If it was split out of PRETABLE.out on pass 3 (as its header says: \"Split out of PRETABLE.out on pass 3\"), then Sections 5, 6, 7 were written at DIFFERENT times (5 before part 2; 6 before part 3; 7 after part 3) but now share ONE file with ONE mtime \u2192 the order witness between 5, 6, 7 is destroyed by the split itself, UNLESS the split happened once after 7 was written (i.e., the file was created at split time containing already-written text) \u2014 the mtimes of PRETABLE.out (restored 0-4) and amendments would both be the split time \u2014 but the INTERNAL order 5<6<7 rests on the text's own numbering + the content's tense (\"written before part 3\"), not on mtime. Is the split honest? The claimed purpose: \"its mtime is its own order witness for part 4\" \u2014 pass4 file's mtime witnesses it was written before part 4 ran. OK. But the 5-7 file: sections written before parts 2, 3, and after part 3 \u2014 one mtime \u2014 the discriminator is lost BETWEEN 5/6/7 \u2014 but does anything turn on that? Section 7 records part-3 results; Section 6 predicts them \u2014 the tension (6 written before part 3, 7 after) is textually evident. Honesty: the header of amendments says \"Split out of PRETABLE.out on pass 3\" \u2014 but Section 7 records part 3's RESULTS (which ran after pass 3's predictions)... if the split happened ON pass 3 (before part 3 ran), Section 7 couldn't be in it yet \u2014 so the file must have been appended to (Section 7 added later) \u2014 which re-creates the defect \u2014 OR the split happened after part 3. The header \"Split out of PRETABLE.out on pass 3\" + containing Section 7 (post-part-3) is in tension: either the split postdates part 3b (then \"on pass 3\" is loose but fine) or the file was appended (defect). The pass-4 note says appending Section 8 there would re-create the defect \u2014 implying the file 5-7 was NOT to be appended to \u2014 but Section 7's own presence suggests it WAS appended (after the split \"on pass 3\"). So there's a small internal wrinkle: the amendments file's own history is either mislabeled (\"on pass 3\" vs containing post-part-3 Section 7) or it was appended to once (destroying 5-6-7's mutual mtime order). Honest? Mostly \u2014 with a wrinkle worth naming: the split preserves ORDER WITNESSES only between files, not within; the 5-7 file's three sections cannot be order-witnessed by mtime, and its header's \"split out on pass 3\" conflicts with its containing Section 7 (written after part 3) unless it was appended to. That's a Q4 finding.\n\nAlso Q4: \"The brief's 'this diff adds five rows' named the pass-4 delta over a diff that is cumulative from 60 (minimax) -- a brief defect, noted\" \u2713 already self-corrected \u2713 honest.\n\nAlso the header of THIS brief: \"PASS 6: pass 5 measured the guard qualified by its own latch bit sweeping 101 rows (no row watched the device with its latch set); the class is closed at every block.\" \u2014 \"pass 5 measured... sweeping 101 rows\" \u2014 the Opus reader measured on the 101-row instrument \u2713 consistent. \"Detector 101 -> 118\" \u2713.\n\n**Check the M_QUAL_GENCTL prediction discrepancy (0xbb vs 0xb8)** \u2014 found above \u2713 flag.\n\n**Check `latch aba` row's session** \u2014 `program([ST_H(6, GENCTL)], at=CODE + 0x100)` \u2014 the second A: st.h GENCTL again. Under the FIX: A(announce), B(announce), A(bit set \u2192 no announce) = 2 \u2713. Under `=`: 3 \u2713. Under `==` TEST: 3 \u2713. Under QUAL at all twelve: A announce, B announce, A lands (no announce) = 2 \u2713 GREEN (consistent with M_QUAL_ALL's red list not including latch aba \u2713).\n\nHmm \u2014 but under QUAL at GENCTL ONLY (M_QUAL_GENCTL): latch aba = A(announce), B(VECBASE \u2014 fix's normal guard \u2192 announce), A(bit set \u2192 lands) = 2 \u2713 GREEN \u2713 (measured: only rb2 genctl red \u2713 117/118 \u2713).\n\n**Check `latch all d` value**: `put d 0x0000000000000003` \u2192 idata = 3 \u2192 (uint8_t) = 3 \u2014 wait, put d len 8: memory_rw len 8 \u2192 device access len 8 \u2192 guard len != 1 \u2713 decline \u2713 announce \u2713 count 12 \u2713. And the latch all d comment \"an 8-byte exemption anywhere drops it\" \u2713.\n\n**Check `acc all`**: twelve guest st.b of 0x10 each block: GENCTL = 0x10 (bit 0x04 clear \u2713 pcc2c040kill safe); reassert at GENCTL/MASK/SCCICR/SCCRX/SCSIICR/TxCTL/TxICR bodies \u2192 harmless \u2713. DECL count 0 \u2713. Under GROW at ANY arm \u2192 1 \u2192 RED \u2713 (measured 95/96 acc all \u2713 part 3c).\n\nWait \u2014 GROW at VECBASE: st.b 0x10 at VECBASE \u2192 guard len != 0 TRUE (len 1) \u2192 decline+announce \u2192 DECL=1 \u2713 red \u2713. And `rb vecbase own`'s ST_B(5, VECBASE) (r5=0x50): under GROW at VECBASE \u2192 declined \u2192 VECBASE stays 0x00 \u2192 readback 0x00 \u2260 0x50 \u2192 also red \u2713. Fine.\n\n**Check `latch all fail` under the FIX**: twelve declined put h \u2192 break