regress\artifacts\lunaabsentcpu\pass3_panel_20260906_102412\ollama_glm.txt:1:{"model": "glm-5.3", "created_at": "2026-09-06T15:30:15.135372679Z", "response": "", "thinking": "Let me work through this carefully. I'm a \"packet-fed seat\" \u2014 I must reason only from the inlined text, no file access, no measurements. My verdict line first, claims labeled READ or REASONED, never MEASURED. The brief asks:\n\n1. THE ONE QUESTION: Does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration of the shipped composite (fix v2 + probe v3)? The enumeration: the dominating predicate `cpu < d->ncpus` (its constant's source, its bound both ways, its slot set 0..3); the body-read fields `interrupt_status` (per device line), `interrupt_enable[cpu]`, `software_interrupt_status[cpu]`, `irqActive[cpu]`, `cpu_irq[cpu]`; joint cells where the body tests them ({slot} \u00d7 {pending: sw, device line, none} \u00d7 {irqActive}).\n\n2. Second question: of the nine pass-3 dispositions (the capture move, three new sessions, two disclosed equivalents/debts, three filings/annotations \u2014 lunadefaultstop, lunaabsentmaskdecl, es438), WHICH IS WRONG?\n\nWait \u2014 count the nine dispositions: (1) the capture move, (2-4) three new sessions (B2, C2, E), (5-6) two disclosed equivalents/debts (M_NULLAB equivalent, M_NOREASSERT debt), (7-9) three filings/annotations (lunadefaultstop, lunaabsentmaskdecl, es438). That's nine. The question says \"WHICH IS WRONG?\" \u2014 implying at least one is wrong. I need to audit them.\n\nLet me now audit the packet internally.\n\nFirst, the settled premises:\n\nPremise 1: the walk. Per slot: status = d->interrupt_status (machine-wide, six device lines are 1 << (line+25), lines 1..6 \u2192 bits 26..31); enable = d->interrupt_enable[cpu]; if software_interrupt_status[cpu] then status |= (1 << 26); if (status & enable) \u2192 assert if !irqActive, set latch; else \u2192 deassert if irqActive, clear latch.\n\nWait \u2014 interesting: software interrupt sets bit 26, which is line 1's device bit. So SOFT_INT sets the same bit as device line 1. Bit 26 is shared between device line 1 and software interrupt. Hmm, that's from the packet: lines 1..6 \u2192 bits 26..31, and `if (d->software_interrupt_status[cpu]) status |= (1 << 26)`. So the software interrupt ORs in bit 26, same bit as device line 1. That's a potential aliasing in the model but presumably upstream behavior. Actually wait \u2014 is that right? Let me re-read: \"the six device lines are `1 << (line + 25)`, lines 1..6 -> bits 26..31\". And \"if (d->software_interrupt_status[cpu]) status |= (1 << 26)\". So yes, SOFT_INT uses bit 26 which is the same bit as device line 1. Hmm, that means a software interrupt and device line 1 share a bit. This is stated as settled premise. OK. Actually this might be a real quirk of luna88k hardware (soft interrupts are bit 26?). Whatever \u2014 it's READ from the packet as a premise.\n\nPremise 2: INTERRUPT_ASSERT(istruct) is (istruct).interrupt_assert(&(istruct)) \u2014 no NULL check. DEVINIT connects slot 0 always and slots 1..3 under ncpus >= 2/3/4; later in DEVINIT ncpus > 4 prints and exit(1)s. emul.c maps ncpus 0 \u2192 1; -n is 1..256.\n\nHmm \u2014 wait: \"-n is 1..256\" and \"ncpus > 4 exits at init\". So -n 5..256 all exit at DEVINIT. So reachable configs are ncpus 1..4. MAX_CPUS is presumably 4 (slot set 0..3 mentioned in the enumeration: \"its slot set 0..3\"). Actually the enumeration says \"its slot set 0..3\" \u2014 so MAX_CPUS = 4? The diff shows `struct interrupt cpu_irq[MAX_CPUS]`. If MAX_CPUS were larger than 4, the walk would go beyond 4 and hit never-connected slots even with ncpus=4. The premise says DEVINIT connects slots 1..3 under ncpus >= 2/3/4 and exits if ncpus > 4. So on the largest reachable machine (4 cpus), slots 0..3 are all connected. So the bound d->ncpus \u2208 {1,2,3,4} and slot set 0..3. OK.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 there's a subtlety: if -n can be 1..256 and DEVINIT exits when ncpus > 4, then d->ncpus = machine->ncpus could be up to 256 if the capture were ABOVE the exit. The capture is placed BELOW the exit, so d->ncpus \u2264 4 always. The comment in fix v2 says exactly this: \"Captured BELOW the `ncpus > 4' exit so the bound is <= MAX_CPUS by construction, not by that exit.\" Good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 actually, is d->ncpus \u2264 4 guaranteed by capture below the exit? The exit happens at DEVINIT; the capture is after it in DEVINIT. machine->ncpus \u2264 4 past the exit. Yes. But also: is MAX_CPUS == 4? The packet never states MAX_CPUS's value explicitly. Hmm. It says \"its slot set 0..3\" in the question, implying the connected slots are 0..3 max, i.e., MAX_CPUS \u2265 4 and reachable connects are 0..3. If MAX_CPUS were 8, the bound d->ncpus \u2264 4 still keeps the walk within 0..3, all connected on a 4-cpu machine. So the fix is safe regardless of MAX_CPUS value as long as d->ncpus \u2264 4 and connects cover 0..ncpus-1. Fine. But there's a premise: \"DEVINIT connects slot 0 always and slots 1..3 under ncpus >= 2/3/4\" \u2014 so connects cover exactly 0..ncpus-1 for ncpus \u2208 {1,2,3,4}. So bound == connect predicate. Good \u2014 \"The walk bound and the connect predicate are the same number on purpose.\"\n\nNow audit the diff itself.\n\nThe diff adds `int ncpus;` to struct luna88k_data, changes the walk bound to `cpu < d->ncpus`, and adds `d->ncpus = devinit->machine->ncpus;` below the `ncpus > 4` exit.\n\nOne thing to check: the diff hunk at line 1038 shows:\n\n```\n \t\texit(1);\n \t}\n \n+\t/* comment */\n+\td->ncpus = devinit->machine->ncpus;\n```\n\nThe context above shows `exit(1); }` \u2014 presumably the `ncpus > 4` check. But wait \u2014 is there any other `exit(1)` before this point in DEVINIT? The context line just above is `exit(1); }`. The comment says the capture is below the ncpus > 4 exit. The packet asserts this in multiple places (premise 2, the minimax finding, the fix comment). I can't verify the exact position beyond the assertion, but the diff context at @@ -1038,6 +1043,19 @@ shows only a fragment. The hunk's context is `exit(1); }` then the added comment, then `add_cmmu_for_cpu(devinit, 0, ...)`. Hmm \u2014 the `ncpus > 4` exit at 1038: is it plausible that the check is `if (machine->ncpus > 4) { fprintf(...); exit(1); }` and then the capture? The context shows three lines before the addition: `exit(1);`, `}`, and blank. That's consistent. But note \u2014 is there possibly an earlier `exit(1)` (e.g., \"no bootrom found\") that could be the context instead? The packet says the capture is below the ncpus>4 exit and the minimax finding drove the move. I'll take it as READ (from packet) that the capture site is below the exit; the diff context is consistent with it but doesn't uniquely prove WHICH exit \u2014 actually, hmm. The diff context shows just `exit(1); }` \u2014 the packet premise says \"later in DEVINIT `ncpus > 4` prints and `exit(1)`s\". The diff's context doesn't show the condition. So strictly, from the diff alone, I can't confirm the capture is below THAT exit; but the packet asserts it repeatedly and the table shows M_HARD1 etc. I'll note it: the packet asserts the capture site; the diff context alone is consistent but not conclusive. Actually \u2014 wait, does it matter? If the capture were above some earlier exit(1) \u2014 there could be an earlier exit for missing ROM etc. If capture is before the ncpus>4 exit, then on -n 256, d->ncpus = 256 and the walk would go to cpu=255 \u2192 crash on unconnected slots. But that's exactly the minimax finding (capture above the exit makes the exit load-bearing) \u2014 addressed by moving it below. The premise says it moved below. OK.\n\nAnother check on the fix: `d->ncpus = devinit->machine->ncpus;` \u2014 is the field `machine->ncpus` the right one? Premise 2: \"emul.c maps ncpus 0 -> 1; -n is 1..256\". And DEVINIT connects based on ncpus thresholds \u2014 presumably reading devinit->machine->ncpus. The fix reads the same source. The enumeration says \"its constant's source\" is part of the audit. If DEVINIT's connect predicate uses a different source than the walk bound... e.g., if DEVINIT used `devinit->machine->ncpus` and the fix captured from somewhere else, mismatch. The fix uses `devinit->machine->ncpus`. The premise says \"DEVINIT connects slot 0 always and slots 1..3 under `ncpus >= 2/3/4`\" \u2014 presumably the same machine->ncpus. Not fully verifiable from packet whether DEVINIT's predicate reads machine->ncpus, but it's strongly implied (\"The walk bound and the connect predicate are the same number on purpose\"). READ-ish.\n\nNow \u2014 the probe diff. Session B2: 1 cpu, MASK0 \u2190 0x40000000 (bit 30 = SIO line 5), then stores SIOCMD0 0x01 then 0x02 (WR1 select then TX_IE). Then park, breakpoint VBR+8, continue.\n\nCheck bit math: line 5 \u2192 bit (5+25) = bit 30. 1 << 30 = 0x40000000. \u2713. And bit 30 is within bits 26..31. \u2713.\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, one wrinkle: SOFT_INT also ORs bit 26 into status. In B2, only bit 30 is enabled and SIO line 5 raised. Fine.\n\nSession C2: -n 2, both slots pending: MASK0+MASK1 \u2190 0x04000000 (bit 26), SOFT0+SOFT1 \u2190 1. Bit 26 = 1 << 26 = 0x04000000. \u2713. Both slots pending with software interrupt \u2192 both cpus should enter VBR+8.\n\nSession E: -n 4, cpus 1..3 parked with VBR/PSR set, MASK3 (MASK0+12) \u2190 0x04000000, SOFT3 (SOFT0+12) \u2190 1. cpu 0 must observe cpu 3 at VBR+8 and cpus 0..2 not.\n\nCheck addressing: INT_ST_MASK0 base, MASK0+4 = MASK1, +8 = MASK2, +12 = MASK3. Consistent with premise 3 (`cpunr = (addr - INT_ST_MASK0)/4`). \u2713.\n\nNow the table. Scores:\n\n- M_MASK30: 26/27 FAIL, sio5 deliver STOP.\n- M_SWONLY: 26/27 FAIL, sio5 deliver STOP.\n- M_IRQACTIVE0: 26/27 FAIL, both deliver STOP.\n- M_AND1: 25/27 FAIL, cpu3 deliver STOP + others idle BADREGS. That's two red rows \u2192 25/27. \u2713 arithmetic: 27 - 2 = 25. \u2713.\n- M_MIN2: 26/27 FAIL, cpu3 deliver STOP.\n- M_REVERT: 20/22 PRE_FIX_SYMPTOM, soft1, siofirst HALT.\n- M_OFFBY1 (<=): 20/22.\n- M_NOASSIGN: 18/23 FAIL \u2014 red rows: slot0 deliver, sio5 deliver, both deliver, cpu1 deliver, cpu3 deliver \u2014 that's 5 red rows. 23 - 5 = 18. \u2713.\n- M_HARD1: 21/24 FAIL \u2014 red: both deliver, cpu1 deliver, cpu3 deliver \u2014 3 red. 24 - 3 = 21. \u2713.\n- M_SKIP1: 23/25 FAIL \u2014 red: both deliver, cpu1 deliver \u2014 2 red. 25 - 2 = 23. \u2713.\n- M_NULLA: 21/22 \u2014 soft1 HALT. 1 red. \u2713.\n- M_NULLAB: 27/27 PASS.\n- M_LATCH: 20/22 \u2014 soft1, siofirst HALT \u2014 2 red. \u2713.\n- M_GUARDASSERT: 21/22 \u2014 soft1 HALT \u2014 1 red. \u2713.\n- M_CALLGUARD: 21/23 \u2014 sio5, siofirst HALT \u2014 2 red. \u2713.\n- M_NOREASSERT: 27/27 PASS.\n- HEAD: 20/22 PRE_FIX_SYMPTOM with final instrument; soft1, siofirst HALT signal 11; every new session (B2, C2, E) passes on HEAD.\n\nDenominators: the note says \"The RESULT denominators shrink when the host exits mid-session (the remaining rows of that session cannot run)\". Base is 27/27.\n\nLet me now cross-check the denominators per mutant for consistency. The probe rows (v3): Let me enumerate rows from the diff and context.\n\nFrom the probe docstring and code, rows visible: session A2 (rows?), session B (rows: slot0 deliver? siofirst? soft1? \u2014 from HEAD result: soft1, siofirst HALT in session B), session B2 (sio5 deliver), session C (cpu1 deliver, cpu1 shadow? \u2014 v2 docstring: \"cpu1 deliver / cpu1 shadow / cpu0 parked\" \u2014 hmm that's three labels but the row is \"cpu1 deliver\"? Actually the docstring line lists \"two cpus / cpu1 setup / mask1 land / survive / cpu1 deliver / cpu1 shadow / cpu0 parked\" \u2014 these look like row names for session C: maybe rows \"two cpus\", \"cpu1 setup\", \"mask1 land\", \"survive\", \"cpu1 deliver\", \"cpu1 shadow\", \"cpu0 parked\"? That would be 7 rows for session C. Hmm wait, session C in v2 was described: \"session C (-n 2, FREE-RUNNING) cpu 1: VBR..., cpu 0 steps MASK1 <- 0x04000000 and SOFT_INT1 <- 1, parks; breakpoint VBR+8; continue\" with rows \"two cpus / cpu1 setup / mask1 land / survive / cpu1 deliver / cpu1 shadow / cpu0 parked\". So session C has up to 7 rows? But some might be description words not rows. The v2 description column format seems to be a list of row names separated by \" / \". So session C emits rows: two cpus, cpu1 setup, mask1 land, survive, cpu1 deliver, cpu1 shadow, cpu0 parked. That's 7 rows.\n\nSession D (control): \"nodeliver ctl\" \u2014 1 row.\n\nSession A2 and B: unknown row lists, but HEAD's result is 20/22 PRE_FIX_SYMPTOM with soft1 and siofirst HALT. Session B appears to include rows soft1, siofirst (and maybe others). Base probe v2 was 20 rows (\"measured 20/20\" appears twice for v2: \"measured 20/20\" for the both-sided NULL guard equivalence, and \"TOTAL ESCAPE of every 1-cpu row, measured 20/20\" hmm \u2014 actually the v2\u2192v3 diff of session C's description changes \"measured 20/20\" to \"measured 19/20, cpu1 deliver alone red\". Wait, re-read:\n\nSession C description in v2: \"M_HARD1 (`d->ncpus = 1' hardcoded -- the clause-(d) null repair: a TOTAL ESCAPE of every 1-cpu row, measured 20/20): cpu 1 enters its vector...\" \u2014 hmm, this is confusing. In v2, M_HARD1 escaped totally (20/20 with d->ncpus=1 hardcoded?). Wait, if d->ncpus=1 hardcoded, on session C (-n 2), the walk only does slot 0, so cpu 1's IPI would never be delivered \u2192 cpu1 deliver should be STOP (red). So \"TOTAL ESCAPE\" with 20/20 seems wrong for v2 \u2014 and indeed v3's diff changes it: \"measured 19/20, cpu1 deliver alone red\". So the v2 docstring claimed M_HARD1 was a total escape measured 20/20, and the v3 diff CORRECTS that to 19/20 with cpu1 deliver red. Wait, but the diff direction: `-` lines are v2, `+` lines are v3. The `-` line: \"a TOTAL ESCAPE of every 1-cpu row, measured 20/20): cpu 1 enters\" and `+` line: \"a TOTAL ESCAPE of every 1-cpu row; measured 19/20, cpu1 deliver alone red): cpu 1 enters\". So v3 fixes a stale/incorrect claim in the v2 docstring. OK \u2014 that's a correction of a prediction. Note \"a predicted sweep is a measurement owed\" \u2014 hmm.\n\nAlso in v3 CONTRACT changes: \"measured 20/20 + 8/8\" \u2192 \"measured 22/22 in killtable/\". So the equivalence claim for M_NULLAB is now 22/22 (the v2-era claim was 20/20 + 8/8 from a different table). And M_NULLAB in table 2 is 27/27 PASS. Hmm wait \u2014 the CONTRACT says \"measured 22/22 in killtable/\" \u2014 killtable (table 1?) vs killtable2 (table 2)? Table 2 header says \"regress/artifacts/lunaabsentcpu/killtable2/\". The CONTRACT v3 says 22/22 in killtable/ \u2014 maybe killtable was the pass-2 table (22 rows after pass-2 additions?) and killtable2 is the new one. Slight inconsistency but not necessarily a defect: v2's probe had 20 rows; pass-2 added sio5 rows? Hmm, actually the pass-2 findings led to fix v2 + probe v3, and killtable/ (22 rows) was presumably the pass-2-era table where M_NULLAB scored 22/22. Fine.\n\nNow, row count: base 27/27 for v3. Sessions: A2 (?), B (?), B2 (1 row: sio5 deliver), C (7 rows), C2 (1 row: both deliver), D (1 row: nodeliver ctl), E (3 rows: four cpus, cpu3 deliver, others idle). 1+7+1+3 = 12 plus sessions A2 and B. For total 27, A2+B must contribute 15 rows. Plausible if B has many rows (soft1, siofirst, slot0 deliver, mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks, etc.). Hmm, wait \u2014 M_NOASSIGN red rows: slot0 deliver, sio5 deliver, both deliver, cpu1 deliver, cpu3 deliver (5 red), 18/23. Denominator 23 \u2014 smaller than 27. Why? Because if the bound is never assigned (d->ncpus stays 0 from memset), the walk does NOTHING \u2014 no crash, no delivery anywhere. Then which sessions die? Hmm, actually with d->ncpus = 0, no delivery at all \u2192 slot0 deliver STOP, siofirst STOP?, soft1 STOP?... But the table says red rows are slot0 deliver, sio5 deliver, both deliver, cpu1 deliver, cpu3 deliver \u2014 five rows. Denominator 23 means 4 rows didn't run. Which session died mid-way? With bound 0, the walk never touches cpu_irq at all \u2014 nothing crashes. Hmm, so why would the denominator shrink to 23? Unless... hmm.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. Denominators per mutant:\n- M_MASK30, M_SWONLY, M_IRQACTIVE0, M_MIN2: 26/27 \u2192 one row missing from 27? No wait \u2014 26/27 with ONE red row (sio5 deliver STOP) means 26 OK out of 27 total, i.e., 1 red, full 27 ran. \u2713 (27 - 1 red = 26 OK).\n- M_AND1: 25/27, two red \u2192 27 ran. \u2713.\n- M_REVERT, M_OFFBY1, M_LATCH: 20/22 \u2192 22 ran, 2 red (soft1, siofirst HALT). So 5 rows didn't run (27 \u2192 22): the host died mid-session, killing subsequent rows of that session. Which session? soft1 and siofirst are presumably early rows of session B. If session B dies at soft1... wait but both soft1 AND siofirst are red, so the host died at/after siofirst. Rows after siofirst in session B (and maybe session B is last?) \u2014 hmm. Actually if session B comes after session A2 (in main: A2, B, B2, C, C2, D, E), a death in B would kill B's remaining rows AND subsequent sessions? No \u2014 wait, does the probe start a NEW emulator process per session (`boot(n)`)? Yes, each session boots its own emulator. So a HALT in session B kills session B's remaining rows only; sessions B2, C, C2, D, E boot fresh hosts. Hmm, but then M_REVERT at 20/22 means only 22 rows total ran \u2014 so 5 rows lost, all within one session? If session B has, say, 12 rows and dies at row 2 (soft1 is row 1? siofirst row 2?), then 10 rows of B are lost \u2192 total 27 - 10 = 17, not 22. Hmm.\n\nWait, let me recount. Maybe session order and rows: Let me think about the \"20/22 PRE_FIX_SYMPTOM\" for HEAD. HEAD (unfixed) crashes on absent-slot walks: session B (1 cpu) with mask1/soft1 \u2192 soft1 HALT. siofirst HALT \u2014 \"siofirst\" might be \"SIO first\" \u2014 a row where the SIO line is raised BEFORE the soft int, so the first walk that touches the absent slot is via the device caller \u2192 HALT. So in session B, rows soft1 and siofirst both HALT (two separate crashes \u2014 each row restarts? or the session continues with a new boot per row?). If each row has its own boot, a HALT kills just that row (HALT result) and subsequent rows still run. Then why does the denominator shrink to 22 for M_REVERT/HEAD?\n\nThe note: \"The RESULT denominators shrink when the host exits mid-session (the remaining rows of that session cannot run)\". So a session = one host boot, multiple rows within it. If the host dies at row k, rows k+1.. of that session can't run. For HEAD: 20/22 \u2192 22 ran, 5 lost. soft1 and siofirst both HALT \u2014 so the host died twice? That means after a HALT, the probe reboots? Hmm, or soft1 and siofirst are in DIFFERENT sessions. Maybe \"siofirst\" is a row in session B2's predecessor... Actually maybe there are sessions: A2 (1 cpu, several rows incl. soft1?), B (siofirst?), etc.\n\nHonestly, the exact session layout isn't fully inlined \u2014 v2's full row list isn't in the packet; only the diff fragments. The denominators 22, 23, 24, 25 for different mutants are explained by mid-session deaths at different points. Let me sanity check a few:\n\nM_NOASSIGN (bound never assigned \u2192 d->ncpus = 0, walk never delivers): red rows listed: slot0 deliver, sio5 deliver, both deliver, cpu1 deliver, cpu3 deliver. 5 red, denominator 23 \u2192 18 OK. 27 - 23 = 4 rows lost. Which? With bound 0, no crash ever (walk does nothing). But wait \u2014 would ANY row HALT? No. So why do 4 rows not run? Hmm. Unless some session's row sequencing depends on delivery: e.g., session C parks cpu 1 at `br .` and expects... if delivery never happens, the probe `cont_until_breakpoint` times out (STOP), session continues. Hmm, so why 23 not 27?\n\nHold on \u2014 maybe with the bound never assigned, d->ncpus is 0 \u2014 but the `boot()` itself: DEVINIT... the fix's capture line is `d->ncpus = devinit->machine->ncpus;` \u2014 M_NOASSIGN removes that line, so d->ncpus = 0 (memset). Walk: `for (cpu = 0; cpu < 0; cpu++)` \u2014 zero iterations. Nothing delivers. All delivery rows STOP. But also mask readback rows should still be OK (stores still work \u2014 the walk isn't involved in stores... wait, is it? The store arms CALL reassert_interrupts (four callers). With the walk doing nothing, stores still write storage; readbacks OK). So red rows = every delivery-expecting row: slot0 deliver (session A2 or B), sio5 deliver (B2), both deliver (C2), cpu1 deliver (C), cpu3 deliver (E), maybe \"mask1 land\" (session C row \u2014 does it expect vector entry? \"mask1 land\" might be a readback row \u2192 OK). 5 red listed. And 4 rows lost (27\u219223). Why 4 lost? Possibly session C's rows after cpu1 deliver: session C has 7 rows (two cpus, cpu1 setup, mask1 land, survive, cpu1 deliver, cpu1 shadow, cpu0 parked). Hmm, if cpu1 deliver STOPs (timeout, 15 s each), the probe continues... unless the session code `return`s early on STOP. Actually look at session C2's code: on STOP it just rows and returns. In session_C2, if len(entered) < 2 after attempts, row \"both deliver\" STOP and s.close(). No other rows in C2. Fine.\n\nFor M_NOASSIGN, maybe session E: \"four cpus\" row OK (machine listing), then MASK3 store OK, SOFT3 store OK, park, continue \u2192 timeout \u2192 cpu3 deliver STOP, others idle: pcs \u2014 cpu 3 is parked at PARK, not VBR+8; cpus 0..2 also not at VBR+8 \u2192 idle = True \u2192 \"others idle\" OK? Wait, that row says OK if no other cpu entered the vector. With no delivery, no cpu entered \u2192 OK. Hmm, but that row would pass vacuously \u2014 it's a control that only means something when cpu3 deliver is OK. Fine, that's a design quirk: \"others idle\" can pass vacuously on a total no-deliver mutant. Not a defect per se since cpu3 deliver is red anyway.\n\nSo why denominator 23? 4 rows lost. Maybe: in session C, the code parks cpu1, cpu0 steps stores, then `cont_until_breakpoint` \u2014 if no delivery, the FIRST breakpoint hit never comes; maybe session C does TWO continues (v2 description: \"breakpoint VBR+8; `continue'\"). Each timeout is 15 s. Timeouts don't kill rows though.\n\nAlternatively \u2014 maybe with bound 0, some row expects a HALT and instead gets... no.\n\nHmm, wait. Maybe I'm misreading which rows exist. Maybe some rows are in a session that comes AFTER a session that hangs/dies. The \"remaining rows of that session cannot run\" \u2014 for M_NOASSIGN, maybe the host doesn't die but the probe's cont_until_breakpoint returns with s.dead False, and then subsequent rows still run. 4 lost rows is unexplained by the mutant's behavior... unless the probe has rows that DEPEND on a previous row's OK (e.g., session C: cpu1 shadow expects... no).\n\nAlternatively the denominator differences might be because some mutants crash the host mid-session: M_REVERT/M_OFFBY1/M_LATCH die at soft1 (HALT) \u2014 wait but both soft1 AND siofirst are red. If the host died at soft1, siofirst couldn't run \u2192 but siofirst shows HALT, so it DID run. So soft1 and siofirst must each have their own boot (or the session reboots per row). If each row reboots, denominators wouldn't shrink... but they do (22 vs 27). Unless: the 22 vs 27 loss is from session E or C2 etc. not running because... hmm, no \u2014 wait. For HEAD/M_REVERT, the table says red rows: soft1, siofirst HALT. Denominator 22. 27 - 22 = 5 rows lost. Maybe rows lost are in the SAME session after the second HALT? If soft1 and siofirst are rows 1 and 2 of a 7-row session, losing 5 rows after \u2192 22. That fits! Session with 7 rows where soft1 is row 1, siofirst row 2, and 5 rows after die because the host exited at siofirst... but then how did siofirst itself run if the host died at soft1? Unless each row boots fresh and the \"remaining rows\" note refers to rows after a NOSTART...\n\nHmm wait, actually re-read the note: \"The RESULT denominators shrink when the host exits mid-session (the remaining rows of that session cannot run)\". For M_REVERT: soft1 HALT and siofirst HALT. If soft1 is in session A2 (or B) and siofirst is in session B (or B2-precursor), and the death at soft1 killed the remaining rows of ITS session, and the death at siofirst killed the remaining rows of ITS session...\n\nLet me try: session A2 rows: ... soft1 ... ; session B rows: siofirst, ... Let me not over-infer. The packet doesn't inline the full row list of v2 sessions; I can't fully verify denominator arithmetic. But I CAN check the arithmetic that IS inlined:\n\n- 27 total base. M_MASK30: 1 red, 26/27. \u2713.\n- M_AND1: 2 red, 25/27. \u2713.\n- M_NOASSIGN: 5 red listed, 18/23 \u2192 23 - 18 = 5 red. \u2713 consistent (5 red, 23 ran).\n- M_HARD1: 3 red listed, 21/24 \u2192 24 - 21 = 3. \u2713.\n- M_SKIP1: 2 red, 23/25 \u2192 25-23=2. \u2713.\n- M_NULLA: 1 red (soft1), 21/22 \u2192 22-21=1. \u2713.\n- M_LATCH: 2 red, 20/22 \u2192 22-20=2. \u2713.\n- M_GUARDASSERT: 1 red, 21/22. \u2713.\n- M_CALLGUARD: 2 red (sio5, siofirst), 21/23 \u2192 23-21=2. \u2713.\n- M_REVERT/M_OFFBY1: 2 red, 20/22 \u2192 \u2713.\n- HEAD: 20/22, soft1+siofirst red. \u2713.\n\nAll internally consistent. Good.\n\nNow the KEY question: does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) enumeration?\n\nThe (d) enumeration: dominating predicate `cpu < d->ncpus` (constant's source, bound both ways \u2014 i.e., < vs <=, and the bound variable, slot set 0..3); body-read fields: interrupt_status (per device line), interrupt_enable[cpu], software_interrupt_status[cpu], irqActive[cpu], cpu_irq[cpu]; joint cells {slot} \u00d7 {pending: sw, device, none} \u00d7 {irqActive}.\n\nA \"clean sweep\" mutant = a mutant that passes all 27 rows (escapes the probe) while being non-equivalent (a real defect). The contract discloses: M_NULLAB (both-sided NULL guard) is equivalent on every reachable configuration \u2014 so it's NOT a defect (warrant-based choice). M_NOREASSERT (SOFT arm reassert removed) passes 27/27 \u2014 disclosed debt: the clock's reassert carries delivery. Is that in the (d) enumeration? The SOFT arm's call is a caller, not the walk body... the enumeration is about the walk's dominating predicate and body reads. M_NOREASSERT is about a caller. Hmm \u2014 the question says \"in the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration of the SHIPPED COMPOSITE (fix v2 + probe v3)\" \u2014 the enumeration given: dominating predicate, body-read fields, joint cells.\n\nLet me hunt for a clean-sweep mutant in that enumeration.\n\nWalk body per slot:\n```\nstatus = d->interrupt_status;  // machine-wide\nenable = d->interrupt_enable[cpu];\nif (d->software_interrupt_status[cpu]) status |= (1 << 26);\nif (status & enable) {\n    if (!d->irqActive[cpu]) INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->cpu_irq[cpu]);\n    d->irqActive[cpu] = 1;\n} else {\n    if (d->irqActive[cpu]) INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->cpu_irq[cpu]);\n    d->irqActive[cpu] = 0;\n}\n```\n\nCandidate mutants:\n\n1. `enable = d->interrupt_enable[0]` (collapse per-cpu enable onto slot 0). Rows: session B2 uses MASK0 (slot 0's enable) with SIO line 5 \u2192 status bit 30 & enable bit 30 \u2192 delivers to cpu 0. Collapsing enable[cpu] \u2192 enable[0]: on 1-cpu machine identical. Session C (-n 2): MASK1 \u2190 bit 26, SOFT_INT1 \u2192 slot 1: enable would read interrupt_enable[0] instead of [1]. MASK0 was NOT written in session C? v2 session C description: \"cpu 0 steps MASK1 <- 0x04000000 and SOFT_INT1 <- 1\". MASK0 (slot 0's enable) \u2014 what's its reset value? If interrupt_enable[] starts 0 (memset) and session C only writes MASK1, then enable[0] = 0 \u2192 status & 0 = 0 \u2192 NO delivery \u2192 cpu1 deliver STOP \u2192 caught (red). Unless something else writes MASK0. Hmm, wait \u2014 but does the walk see slot 0? Yes. With enable[cpu] \u2192 enable[0]: slot 0: status (bit 26 from SOFT0? no \u2014 SOFT_INT0 not written in session C) \u2014 software_interrupt_status[0] = 0, status = interrupt_status (machine-wide device lines; none pending in session C? the clock might raise... hmm). Slot 0: enable[0] = 0 \u2192 no delivery to cpu 0 (correct anyway \u2014 cpu 0 parked). Slot 1: enable collapses to enable[0] = 0 \u2192 no delivery to cpu 1 \u2192 cpu1 deliver STOP. RED. Caught. Good \u2014 so `interrupt_enable[cpu]` \u2192 `[0]` is caught by session C (cpu1 deliver), PROVIDED MASK0 isn't set in session C and reset value is 0. Hmm \u2014 wait, actually is MASK0's reset value 0? The struct arrays: interrupt_enable[MAX_CPUS] \u2014 presumably memset 0 at DEVINIT. If interrupt_enable[0] resets to 0, then yes caught. But hold on \u2014 what if the mutant `enable = d->interrupt_enable[cpu & 1]`? Session E (4 cpus): MASK3 written, SOFT3 written. cpu3's enable would read enable[1] = 0 \u2192 cpu3 deliver STOP \u2192 caught. And cpu 1's slot would read enable[1]... wait with cpu&1: slot 1 \u2192 enable[1] = 0 (MASK1 not written in E) \u2192 fine. cpu 3 \u2192 enable[1] = 0 \u2192 no delivery \u2192 STOP. Caught. OK.\n\nBut wait \u2014 is there a mutant on interrupt_enable that sweeps? What about reading `interrupt_enable[cpu]` but the probe never tests a slot whose enable is DISABLED... The probe tests: mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks (declined store rows?) \u2014 \"a declined store to an absent slot (mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks)\". Hmm.\n\n2. `software_interrupt_status[cpu]` \u2192 `software_interrupt_status[0]`: collapse. Session C: SOFT_INT1 written; slot 1's software_interrupt_status[1] = 1, but mutant reads [0] = 0 \u2192 status for slot 1 = interrupt_status only (bit 26 not set) & enable[1] (bit 26 set via MASK1) \u2192 interrupt_status machine-wide: is bit 26 set by anything? Device line 1 pending? In session C, no device lines pending (parked cpus, no SIO armed). The clock? \"the clock's own reassert carries the delivery\" \u2014 the clock calls luna88k_interrupt_assert with some line. If the clock raises a device line whose bit is in interrupt_status and MASK1 only enables bit 26 \u2192 status & enable = 0 \u2192 no delivery \u2192 cpu1 deliver STOP. Caught. But hmm \u2014 wait: does SOFT_INT store itself set software_interrupt_status[1]? Yes (premise: SOFT_INTn store arm is a caller of the walk). So the mutant reads software_interrupt_status[0] = 0 for slot 1 \u2192 red. Caught by C. What about session C2 (both): SOFT0 and SOFT1 both written \u2192 [0] = 1 \u2192 both slots see bit 26 \u2192 both deliver \u2192 OK. And session E: SOFT3 written, reads [0] = 0 \u2192 cpu3 deliver STOP \u2192 caught anyway. OK.\n\n3. `irqActive[cpu]` \u2192 `irqActive[0]`: that's M_IRQACTIVE0 \u2014 caught by C2 (both deliver STOP). 26/27. \u2713 per table.\n\n4. `cpu_irq[cpu]` \u2192 `cpu_irq[cpu & 1]`: M_AND1 \u2014 caught by E (cpu3 deliver STOP, others idle BADREGS). \u2713. What about `cpu_irq[0]` (collapse)? Session C: cpu1 deliver \u2014 slot 1 asserts cpu_irq[0] \u2192 cpu 0 enters vector, cpu 1 doesn't \u2192 cpu1 deliver STOP (red), and cpu0 parked row \u2192 BADREGS? cpu 0 was parked at `br .` \u2014 it would take the interrupt and enter VBR+8 \u2192 \"cpu0 parked\" row would fail too (it's a row: \"cpu0 parked\"). So caught. \u2713.\n\n5. Dominating predicate: `cpu < d->ncpus` \u2192 `cpu <= d->ncpus` (M_OFFBY1 \u2014 caught), `cpu < MAX_CPUS` (M_REVERT \u2014 caught), `cpu < 4` hardcoded \u2014 hmm! `cpu < 4` hardcoded: on 1-cpu machine (session B, A2, B2), walk goes 0..3 \u2192 slots 1..3 unconnected \u2192 NULL assert \u2192 HALT at soft1 (same as M_REVERT). Caught. `cpu < 2` (M_MIN2 \u2014 caught by E). `cpu < 1` (M_HARD1 \u2014 caught). Bound never assigned (M_NOASSIGN \u2014 caught). So predicate mutants are covered by the table.\n\n6. `interrupt_status` per device line: M_MASK30 masks bit 30 \u2014 caught by B2. What about masking OTHER bits, e.g., bit 26 (`status = d->interrupt_status & ~(1 << 26)`)? Hmm \u2014 bit 26 is the SOFT bit. If a mutant zeroed bit 26 of interrupt_status: session C (SOFT1, MASK1 bit 26): status = interrupt_status (bit 26 clear, no device line 1) & ~(1<<26) \u2192 still 0... wait but then `if (software_interrupt_status[cpu]) status |= (1 << 26)` re-adds bit 26 AFTER. Order: status = interrupt_status (mutated: & ~(1<<26)); then SOFT adds bit 26 back. So masking bit 26 in the device read is INVISIBLE to sw-pending slots (bit re-added), and affects only device-line-1 delivery. Is there a row where device line 1 delivers? B2 uses line 5 (bit 30). siofirst/sio5 rows from session B (v2 era) \u2014 \"sio5\" red in M_CALLGUARD \u2014 so session B has a sio5 row (1-cpu, device line 5). Hmm, what device lines do the v2 rows exercise? soft1 = software interrupt slot 1; siofirst = SIO first?; sio5 = SIO line 5 (session B?). If no row delivers device line 1 (bit 26 via the device path), then a mutant `status &= ~(1 << 26)` on the device read would sweep... but wait \u2014 would it be non-equivalent? Device line 1 raised by a real device (which device uses line 1?) with slot's MASK enabling bit 26 \u2192 delivery lost. That IS a real behavior change not covered IF no row covers device-line-1 delivery. Hmm. But the enumeration says \"interrupt_status (per device line)\" \u2014 the question explicitly asks per device line! So: does probe v3 have a row for EACH device line (1..6)? B2 covers line 5. sio5/siofirst (session B rows) \u2014 which lines? Not specified in the packet. The packet doesn't inline session B's device lines. So I CANNOT settle from the packet whether device line 1 (bit 26) delivery is covered. Hmm \u2014 but wait: bit 26 is shared with SOFT. A mutant masking bit 26 in the device status read: for sw-pending slots, the SOFT arm re-adds bit 26 \u2192 delivery works. For device line 1: lost. Which rows raise device line 1? Unknown from packet. If none, this mutant sweeps (all 27 OK). Is it equivalent? No \u2014 device line 1 is a real line (lines 1..6 exist). So THIS could be the clean-sweep mutant... but the packet doesn't give me session B's line coverage, so I can't settle it. Hmm, but hold on \u2014 actually, let me reconsider: is the mutant `status = d->interrupt_status & ~(1 << 26)` even sensible in the enumeration? \"body-read fields interrupt_status (per device line)\" \u2014 yes, the enumeration asks: for each device line, is the body's read of interrupt_status covered? The probe covers line 5 (B2: bit 30) \u2014 and per the docstring \"MASK0 <- 0x40000000 (bit 30 = SIO line 5)\". M_MASK30 kills via sio5 deliver. But what about lines 1,2,3,4,6 (bits 26,27,28,29,31)? A mutant masking ANY of those bits out of the status read would kill delivery of that line. If no row exercises that line's delivery, the mutant sweeps. From the packet, only line 5 (bit 30) is explicitly covered by a device-line delivery row, plus whatever siofirst/sio5 do in session B (v2 rows \u2014 possibly line 5 as well, since they're named \"sio5\"). \"siofirst\" \u2014 SIO first? Maybe \"SIO reaches the walk through the third AND fourth callers\" \u2014 the SIO assert/deassert callers. Hmm.\n\nActually wait \u2014 the contract says: \"a device line masked out of the walk\" is CAUGHT \u2014 via session B2's sio5 deliver row (bit 30 only). The contract's claim \"a device line masked out of the walk\" is demonstrated ONLY for bit 30 (M_MASK30). For bits 26,27,28,29,31 \u2014 a masking mutant on those bits is caught only if a row delivers that line. The probe raises SIO line 5 (WR1 TX_IE \u2192 sio_tx_pending \u2192 line 5). The clock \u2014 the clock calls luna88k_interrupt_assert with some line (the clock/timer route is disclosed as NOT steppable/host-timer-driven). So device lines other than 5 might never be raised in any row. A mutant `status = d->interrupt_status & 0xc0000000` (keep only bits 30,31)? No wait, that would break line 5? No \u2014 bit 30 kept. Lines 1..4 (bits 26..29) dropped. All rows: sw-pending rows re-add bit 26 via the SOFT arm \u2192 still deliver. Device rows: only line 5 (bit 30) \u2192 still deliver. So ALL 27 rows pass \u2192 CLEAN SWEEP. And it's non-equivalent: a real guest with, say, line 2 pending and MASK enabling bit 27 loses delivery.\n\nHmm wait, but is that in the (d) enumeration? \"the body-read fields interrupt_status (per device line)\" \u2014 yes, explicitly. So the enumeration includes per-device-line coverage of interrupt_status. And the answer: the probe covers line 5 only (plus the clock's line, whatever it is \u2014 the clock route is disclosed as not caught/not steppable, and the clock's line is raised in the background \u2014 hmm, actually wait: the clock DOES call luna88k_interrupt_assert periodically? \"the clock's own reassert carries the delivery\" \u2014 in M_NOREASSERT, the SOFT arm's reassert call is removed, but delivery still happens because the clock's assert \u2192 luna88k_interrupt_assert \u2192 calls the walk (4th caller? \"the SIO reaches the walk through the third AND fourth callers\" \u2014 the third/fourth callers are luna88k_interrupt_assert/deassert). So the clock periodically calls the walk via interrupt_assert. But the clock ASSERTS some line \u2014 if the clock's line bit is in interrupt_status machine-wide and no MASK enables it, status & enable = 0 \u2192 no delivery. The clock's assert call happens but delivers nothing unless a mask enables its bit.\n\nSo: which device lines have delivery rows? From the packet: line 5 (bit 30) \u2014 B2's sio5 deliver, and session B's sio5/siofirst rows (v2-era, likely also line 5 given the names). NO evidence of rows for lines 1,2,3,4,6. So a mutant that masks bits 26,27,28,29,31 (i.e., keeps only bit 30 from interrupt_status) sweeps all 27 rows. Actually even simpler: `status = (d->interrupt_status & (1 << 30)); if (d->software_interrupt_status[cpu]) status |= (1 << 26);` \u2014 a \"keep only line 5\" read. All rows pass. Non-equivalent (lines 1-4,6 lost).\n\nHmm wait \u2014 but hold on. Is this really in the shipped composite's DEPENDS-ON enumeration? The question: \"Does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration of the SHIPPED COMPOSITE (fix v2 + probe v3)?\" The enumeration includes \"the body-read fields interrupt_status (per device line)\". So yes \u2014 per device line is exactly this cell. The fix's correctness depends on interrupt_status being read intact for ALL six lines; the probe only witnesses line 5 (bit 30) and the sw bit (26, re-added). So lines 1 (device path), 2, 3, 4, 6 are unwitnessed. A mutant killing any of those sweeps.\n\nBut wait \u2014 is that a defect in the FIX or in the PROBE? The clean-sweep question is about the composite: a mutant that the probe can't catch. The probe's contract claims \"a device line masked out of the walk\" is caught \u2014 but it's only caught for line 5. For the OTHER five lines, masking them out of the walk is NOT caught. So the CONTRACT's claim is overbroad: it says \"a device line masked out of the walk\" as if any line; only line 5 is witnessed. Hmm, but is that a \"floor breach\"? The repair-chain rule: \"the chain closes on the first VALID pass that finds zero floor breaches surviving adjudication\". A clean-sweep mutant in the (d) enumeration = presumably a floor breach (the probe fails to kill a non-equivalent mutant in the depended-on surface). If I find one, the answer is ONE MORE REPAIR (add a row) \u2014 or is it? The question says: \"If you find one, give the exact edit and the row that should have caught it (a predicted sweep is a measurement owed -- say so)\".\n\nSo my primary answer: the per-device-line cell for lines other than 5 is unwitnessed. Candidate exact edit: `status = d->interrupt_status & (1u << 30);` (keep only the SIO line-5 bit from the machine-wide status; the SOFT arm still ORs bit 26). Predicted: 27/27 PASS (sweep). The row that should have caught it: a 1-cpu row arming MASK0 with another line's bit and raising that device line \u2014 but can the probe even raise lines 1-4/6? The probe drives the SIO (line 5) via WR1 TX_IE. Raising other lines requires other devices (the clock's line \u2014 host-timer-driven, not steppable, disclosed). Hmm \u2014 so maybe the probe CANNOT raise lines 1..4,6 deterministically. In that case the honest disposition is: the cell is unwitnessable by this probe (like the clock route \u2014 disclosed debt), and the contract should DISCLOSE it rather than claim \"a device line masked out of the walk\" is caught. Either way: the contract's \"Catches: ... a device line masked out of the walk\" is true only for line 5; the enumeration's per-device-line cells for lines 1,2,3,4,6 are unswept and (from the packet) untestable by this probe \u2192 the contract overclaims. That's a real defect in the packet AS STATED: the CONTRACT claims a kill the table doesn't support for 5 of 6 lines.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 but let me double-check: is bit 26 (line 1) covered via the DEVICE path anywhere? The sw rows re-add bit 26 AFTER the status read, so a mutant masking bit 26 in the device read is invisible to sw rows. Unless a row has a device line 1 pending AND MASK enabling bit 26 \u2014 then the device bit 26 would deliver... but the probe raises only SIO line 5 (and the clock's line in the background). So no. And actually, subtle: in session C (sw row), status read = interrupt_status (masked bit 26 \u2192 0) then SOFT re-adds bit 26 \u2192 delivery. So sw rows can't witness the device-bit-26 read. Right.\n\nHmm, but also: what about `interrupt_status` being machine-wide vs per-slot? It IS machine-wide (premise 1). A mutant making it per-slot doesn't exist (no per-slot array). OK.\n\nNow \u2014 before I commit to that as THE clean-sweep, let me check the OTHER enumerated cells more carefully, because the question says \"WHICH IS WRONG?\" about the nine dispositions \u2014 implying the intended answer to Q2 is one specific disposition. And Q1's answer per the packet's framing (\"SHORT BY DESIGN\", \"closes if zero floor breaches\") might be \"none \u2014 close\". But my audit should be honest. Let me look harder for internal contradictions.\n\nThe nine dispositions:\n1. The capture move (below the ncpus>4 exit) \u2014 minimax's finding. Is it right? Yes: capture above the exit makes d->ncpus potentially >4 (up to 256) while only 0..3 connected \u2192 walk would hit NULLs. Wait, no: if capture were ABOVE the exit... the exit still runs (ncpus>4 \u2192 exit(1)), so the process dies before any guest code. Hmm \u2014 minimax's finding: \"with the capture ABOVE the DEVINIT ncpus > 4 exit, that exit had become load-bearing for the walk's bound.\" I.e., the fix's safety then DEPENDS on the exit(1) \u2014 if the exit were removed/broken, d->ncpus could be 256 \u2192 walk crashes. By moving the capture below the exit, d->ncpus \u2264 4 by construction of the statement order (the assignment happens only on paths where ncpus \u2264 4). Hmm \u2014 actually even below the exit, the bound's safety depends on DEVINIT connecting exactly 0..ncpus-1 \u2014 the connect predicate. It's \"the same number on purpose\". Fine \u2014 the capture move is sound. But is there a wrinkle: `emul.c` maps ncpus 0 \u2192 1, so d->ncpus \u2265 1 \u2014 walk runs at least slot 0. If ncpus could be 0 (machine->ncpus = 0), d->ncpus = 0 \u2192 walk does nothing \u2192 slot 0 (connected!) never gets interrupts. But emul.c maps 0 \u2192 1, so unreachable. OK, READ.\n\n2-4. Three new sessions: B2 (sio5 deliver \u2014 deepseek/grok), C2 (both deliver \u2014 kimi), E (four cpus, cpu3 deliver, others idle \u2014 glm/grok). Are these sound? B2: 1 cpu, MASK0 bit 30, SIO WR1 TX_IE raises line 5 \u2192 device line delivers to CONNECTED slot 0 \u2192 cpu 0 enters VBR+8. Sound, addresses M_MASK30/M_SWONLY. C2: both slots pending \u2192 per-slot latch witnessed. Sound (M_IRQACTIVE0 killed). E: 4 cpus, slot 3 delivery + aliasing check (M_AND1, M_MIN2). Sound. Each new session also passes on HEAD (accept-side control) \u2014 stated: \"every new session -- B2, C2, E -- passes on HEAD\". Good \u2014 the new rows don't false-positive on unfixed HEAD? Wait, that's odd: HEAD is UNFIXED (walk goes to MAX_CPUS, crashes on absent slots). Session E on HEAD: -n 4, MASK3 + SOFT3 \u2192 slot 3 IS connected on 4 cpus \u2192 walk 0..3 all connected \u2192 no crash \u2192 cpu3 delivers. \u2713 passes. Session B2 on HEAD: 1 cpu, MASK0 + SIO line 5 \u2192 slot 0 connected; walk continues to slots 1..3: their enable = MASK1..3 = 0 (never written), sw = 0 \u2192 status & 0 = 0 \u2192 deassert branch: irqActive[0]? = 0 \u2192 no call \u2192 no crash. \u2713 passes (the deassert branch only calls if latch set). Session C2 on HEAD: -n 2, both slots pending \u2192 walk 0..3; slots 2,3: enable 0 \u2192 else-branch, latch 0 \u2192 no call. \u2713 passes. Consistent with \"every new session passes on HEAD\". Good \u2014 internally consistent.\n\nHmm wait, actually \u2014 is that right for B2 on HEAD? status for slot 1: status = interrupt_status machine-wide = bit 30 set (line 5 pending!) \u2014 machine-wide, so slots 1..3 see bit 30 too; enable[1] = MASK1 = 0 \u2192 0 & x = 0 \u2192 else branch \u2192 latch 0 \u2192 no call. \u2713. Right.\n\n5-6. Two disclosed equivalents/debts: M_NULLAB (both-sided NULL guard, EQUIVALENT, warrant-based choice) and M_NOREASSERT (SOFT arm reassert removed, disclosed debt: clock carries delivery). Is the M_NULLAB equivalence claim sound? \"EQUIVALENT on every reachable configuration -- measured 22/22 in killtable/\". Both-sided NULL guard: `if (d->cpu_irq[cpu].interrupt_assert) INTERRUPT_ASSERT(...) else nothing` \u2014 hmm, wait: a both-sided NULL guard means the walk still walks ALL MAX_CPUS slots but skips NULL ones. On reachable configs (ncpus 1..4), unconnected slots have NULL \u2192 skipped; connected slots behave normally. Is that equivalent to the bound? On reachable configs: yes \u2014 slots \u2265 ncpus have NULL \u2192 skipped; slots < ncpus behave identically. One difference: the walk still READS the arrays for unconnected slots (no side effects though \u2014 the guard skips both assert and deassert; the latch write `d->irqActive[cpu] = 0/1` still happens! Wait \u2014 M_NULLAB: \"a both-sided NULL guard instead of the bound\". Where's the guard placed? If the guard wraps only the INTERRUPT_ASSERT/DEASSERT calls, the latch writes still happen for unconnected slots \u2014 but those slots' latches are dead storage, invisible. So behaviorally equivalent on reachable configs. \u2713 sound. Edge: MAX_CPUS > 4 and ncpus \u2264 4: unconnected slots 4..MAX-1 have NULL \u2192 skipped. Equivalent. And a config with ncpus > 4 is unreachable (DEVINIT exits). So EQUIVALENT claim is sound, READ-measured 22/22 (and 27/27 in table 2). OK.\n\nM_NOREASSERT: \"a deleted reassert in the SOFT arm alone (the clock's own reassert carries the delivery; disclosed debt)\". Is this sound? The SOFT_INT store arm calls reassert_interrupts after the store. If removed: the store sets software_interrupt_status[cpu] but no immediate walk; the clock periodically calls luna88k_interrupt_assert \u2192 walk \u2192 delivery eventually. So functionally, delivery still happens (delayed). The probe rows pass (they wait 15 s). Is this a real defect? Latency change \u2014 the sw interrupt wouldn't be delivered until the next clock tick. On real hardware, an IPI should be immediate-ish. The disclosed debt: the probe can't catch it because the clock carries delivery anyway. Hmm \u2014 but WAIT: is the clock guaranteed to call luna88k_interrupt_assert in these sessions? The clock's assert \u2014 \"also the clock\" (premise 1: luna88k_interrupt_assert and deassert are the device lines; also the clock). The clock asserts periodically (timer). In session B (1 cpu, soft1 \u2014 wait, soft1 is slot 1's SOFT on a 1-cpu machine \u2192 NULL assert crash \u2014 that's the HEAD symptom). Hmm, for M_NOREASSERT the walk is BOUNDED (fix v2), so slot 1 isn't walked; the SOFT arm's reassert removed \u2192 delivery of slot 0's soft int relies on the clock \u2192 passes. OK.\n\nBut hmm \u2014 is M_NOREASSERT within the (d) enumeration? It's a CALLER edit (the SOFT arm), not the walk body. The enumeration for Q1: dominating predicate + body reads + joint cells. M_NOREASSERT is outside that enumeration (it was disclosed). Fine.\n\n7. lunadefaultstop LOW \u2014 \"the switch's default: graceful stop plus the unimplemented RESET_CPU0..3 / SOFT_INT_FLAG* arms \u2014 pass-1 F13 said 'file separately' and nobody did\". Filed as a row this round. Sound? It's a filing \u2014 LOW severity. Plausible.\n\n8. lunaabsentmaskdecl LOW documented-only \u2014 \"the absent slots' registers stay storage with readback -- the design hold from pass 1\". Design decision: unconnected slots' MASK/SOFT registers remain guest-writable storage. Filed/documented. Plausible board-model hold.\n\n9. es438: \"its owed mask2/mask3 pair is discharged by this detector's rows -- annotate/close on this round\". Hmm \u2014 es438 owed a mask2/mask3 pair of ROWS (from an earlier round?). The probe v3 has mask1/mask2/mask3 readback rows (\"a declined store to an absent slot (mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks)\"). So es438's owed rows are discharged by the detector's existing rows \u2192 annotate/close. Plausible.\n\nNow, WHICH IS WRONG? The question demands one of the nine. Let me hunt for an internal contradiction in one.\n\nHmm \u2014 think about the M_CALLGUARD note: \"the SIO reaches the walk through the third AND fourth callers\" \u2014 correction of pass-2's grok finding. In table 2, M_CALLGUARD: \"the pass-1 F4 alternative: bound the CALLS\" \u2192 21/23, red rows sio5, siofirst HALT. Wait \u2014 M_CALLGUARD bounds the reassert CALLS (in the store arms?) instead of the walk. Red rows: sio5, siofirst HALT. Hmm \u2014 if the CALLS are guarded/bounded but the walk is NOT bounded (walk still MAX_CPUS), then... the calls happen only for cpu < n? Hmm, \"the pass-1 F4 alternative: bound the CALLS\" \u2014 presumably: don't bound the walk; instead bound where reassert_interrupts is CALLED?? That doesn't prevent the walk from touching unconnected slots when called... Confusing, but the table shows it's NOT a fix (device-line callers: the SIO's assert/deassert calls the walk with no bound \u2192 crash). Red rows sio5, siofirst HALT \u2014 the SIO route crashes. OK consistent.\n\nNow the \"records this round carries\" \u2014 grok's pass-2 finding said \"three of the main loop's 'unchanged' dispositions named rows that are not ledger rows (lunadefaultstop, es438cells, lunaabsentmaskdecl -- `es438` is the row)\". So the ledger row names: lunadefaultstop, lunaabsentmaskdecl, es438. And the records: lunadefaultstop LOW, lunaabsentmaskdecl LOW, es438 annotate/close. OK.\n\nNow \u2014 THE CAPTURE MOVE again. Let me scrutinize: `d->ncpus = devinit->machine->ncpus;` placed below the exit. The exit: `if (ncpus > 4) { ...; exit(1); }` \u2014 hmm, wait, actually what variable does DEVINIT's connect predicate read? \"DEVINIT connects slot 0 always and slots 1..3 under `ncpus >= 2/3/4`\". The connects happen BEFORE the exit? The diff hunk at 1038/1043: the capture is inserted right after the exit(1) block, and BEFORE add_cmmu_for_cpu calls. Where are the INTERRUPT_CONNECTs relative to the capture? If the connects are ABOVE (earlier in DEVINIT), then the capture below them \u2014 fine, order doesn't matter for the capture (it just reads machine->ncpus). The connect predicate presumably reads machine->ncpus directly (not d->ncpus). So the bound and predicate share the source (machine->ncpus) \u2014 \"the same number on purpose\". \u2713.\n\nHmm \u2014 one more check on the fix: is `d->ncpus` assigned BEFORE any reassert_interrupts call can happen? The walk's callers: store arms (guest stores \u2014 after boot), interrupt_assert/deassert (device events \u2014 after boot). During DEVINIT, add_cmmu_for_cpu... no walk calls between struct alloc and the capture? If a device (clock) asserts before the capture... DEVINIT order: connect slot 0, ..., exit check, capture, add_cmmu... If the clock asserts during DEVINIT before the capture, the walk would run with d->ncpus = 0 (memset) \u2192 walk does nothing \u2192 no crash, just a missed assert (would be re-asserted later by the clock). Not a floor breach. OK.\n\nNow, the SECOND question's framing: \"of the nine pass-3 dispositions ..., WHICH IS WRONG?\" \u2014 the packet expects me to find one wrong disposition. Let me think harder.\n\nWait \u2014 count the nine: (1) the capture move; (2,3,4) three new sessions (B2, C2, E); (5,6) two disclosed equivalents/debts (M_NULLAB, M_NOREASSERT); (7,8,9) three filings/annotations (lunadefaultstop, lunaabsentmaskdecl, es438). Total 9. \u2713.\n\nWhich is wrong? Let me examine each for a contradiction with the inlined facts.\n\n(5) M_NULLAB disclosed as EQUIVALENT. Hmm \u2014 is it REALLY equivalent on every reachable configuration? Both-sided NULL guard instead of the bound: walk all MAX_CPUS, guard both assert and deassert with NULL checks. On reachable configs (ncpus 1..4): unconnected slots skipped, latch writes still occur on dead storage. Behavior identical. BUT \u2014 wait: what about INTERRUPT_DEASSERT on a CONNECTED slot whose irqActive latch... no, connected slots have non-NULL pointers. Equivalent. \u2713 sound.\n\nHmm, unless MAX_CPUS < 4?? If MAX_CPUS were, say, 4, then... doesn't matter.\n\n(6) M_NOREASSERT disclosed debt. The claim: \"the clock's own reassert carries the delivery\". Wait \u2014 the CLOCK doesn't \"reassert\" \u2014 the clock calls luna88k_interrupt_assert (an assert, not a reassert call per se, but luna88k_interrupt_assert CALLS the walk \u2014 premise 1: four callers including luna88k_interrupt_assert/deassert). So deleting the SOFT arm's reassert call: delivery still happens on the next clock assert \u2192 walk \u2192 sees software_interrupt_status[cpu] \u2192 delivers. So on reachable configs delivery is delayed but happens. Is M_NOREASSERT a REAL defect (non-equivalent)? Delivery latency for IPIs becomes clock-tick-bound. The probe discloses it as debt (can't catch \u2014 the clock carries delivery within the 15 s window). Sound as a disclosure. Hmm \u2014 but wait: is this disclosure CORRECT? In session C (cpu1 deliver), the clock calls the walk \u2192 delivery to cpu 1 \u2713. In session C2 both pending \u2192 both delivered via clock walks \u2713. OK.\n\nHmm, but hold on \u2014 one more: does the clock actually call luna88k_interrupt_assert in these probes, or is the clock's line ASSERTED once and then deasserted? The clock (dev_luna88k has a clock?) \u2014 \"luna88k_interrupt_assert and luna88k_interrupt_deassert (the device lines; also the clock)\". If the clock's timer fires periodically \u2192 assert \u2192 walk. The claim \"the clock's own reassert carries the delivery\" \u2014 measured 27/27 PASS for M_NOREASSERT confirms the clock does carry it (in the probe environment). OK \u2014 READ from table.\n\n(7) lunadefaultstop: \"the switch's default: graceful stop plus the unimplemented RESET_CPU0..3 / SOFT_INT_FLAG* arms\". Filed LOW. Hmm \u2014 is there an internal contradiction? Pass-1 F13 said file separately; nobody did; this round files it. Fine.\n\n(8) lunaabsentmaskdecl: absent slots' registers stay storage with readback \u2014 design hold from pass 1. Filed LOW documented-only. Consistent with premise 3 and the fix comment (\"The absent slots' MASK/SOFT registers stay guest-writable storage with readback (a board-model question, row lunaabsentmaskdecl)\"). \u2713 consistent.\n\n(9) es438: \"its owed mask2/mask3 pair is discharged by this detector's rows\". Hmm \u2014 WAIT. Let me check: does probe v3 actually have mask2/mask3 readback rows? The contract says \"a declined store to an absent slot (mask1/2/3 readbacks)\". The v3 diff doesn't show mask2/mask3 rows being ADDED (they'd be in session A2/B \u2014 not inlined). The contract v2\u2192v3 diff keeps \"a declined store to an absent slot (mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks)\" \u2014 wait, in the v3 CONTRACT diff, the line \"mask3 readbacks), and the third/fourth MASK labels\" appears unchanged (context). So v2 already claimed mask1/2/3 readback rows. And es438 \"is the row\" per grok's finding. So es438 owed a mask2/mask3 pair \u2014 discharged by the detector's rows. Hmm \u2014 but grok's pass-2 finding said the three named \"rows\" (lunadefaultstop, es438cells, lunaabsentmaskdecl) \"are not ledger rows\" \u2014 es438 IS the row. So es438 was a ledger row with an owed mask2/mask3 pair. If the detector has mask1/2/3 readback rows, the pair is discharged. Plausible \u2014 can't fully verify (the rows aren't inlined), but the contract's claim covers it. Hmm.\n\n(2-4) the three new sessions. Let me scrutinize session E's \"others idle\" row code:\n\n```python\nidle = all(pcs[c] != VBR + 8 for c in (0, 1, 2))\nrow(\"others idle\", \"OK\" if idle else \"BADREGS\", ...)\n```\n\npcs[c] = s.reg(\"pc\", cpu=c). For M_AND1 (cpu_irq[cpu & 1]): slot 3 asserts cpu_irq[1] \u2192 cpu 1 enters VBR+8. pcs[1] == VBR+8 \u2192 idle False \u2192 BADREGS. Table says \"others idle BADREGS (cpu 1 took slot 3's interrupt)\" \u2713 consistent.\n\nBut wait \u2014 session E: cpus 1..3 are parked at PARK (`br .`) with PSR 0x80000000. cpu 0 does the stores then parks. The breakpoint is hit when cpu 3 enters VBR+8 \u2192 cont_until_breakpoint returns. pcs read. If cpu 1 took the interrupt (M_AND1), the breakpoint hits with cpu 1 at VBR+8 \u2192 row cpu3 deliver: pcs[3] != VBR+8 \u2192 BADREGS... but table says cpu3 deliver STOP for M_AND1. Hmm!\n\nM_AND1 red rows: \"cpu3 deliver STOP, others idle BADREGS\". With M_AND1, slot 3's assert goes to cpu_irq[3 & 1] = cpu_irq[1] \u2192 cpu 1 enters the vector. The breakpoint at VBR+8 IS hit (by cpu 1) \u2192 `hit` True, s.dead False. pcs[3] = PARK (cpu 3 still parked) \u2192 cpu3 deliver row: value(...) pcs[3] == VBR+8? No \u2192 BADREGS. But the table says cpu3 deliver STOP! STOP is \"no vector entry before the wait ran out\" \u2014 but the breakpoint WAS hit (by cpu 1). Hmm \u2014 wait, look at the code:\n\n```python\nhit = s.cont_until_breakpoint(15.0)\n...\npcs = [s.reg(\"pc\", cpu=c) for c in (0, 1, 2, 3)]\nif not hit:\n    row(\"cpu3 deliver\", \"STOP\", ...)\n    s.close(); return\nrow(\"cpu3 deliver\", \"OK\" if value(...) else \"BADREGS\")\n```\n\nIf hit (cpu 1 entered), cpu3 deliver \u2192 BADREGS (pcs[3] = PARK). Table says STOP. CONTRADICTION? Unless with M_AND1, cpu 1 does NOT enter... wait: M_AND1: `cpu_irq[cpu & 1]` at ASSERT and DEASSERT. Slot 3: status & enable (MASK3 bit 26, SOFT3) \u2192 assert cpu_irq[3&1]=cpu_irq[1] \u2192 cpu 1 (parked at `br .` with IND... PSR 0x80000000 \u2014 interrupts enabled?) \u2192 cpu 1 enters VBR+8 \u2192 breakpoint hit. So cpu3 deliver should be BADREGS, not STOP.\n\nHmm wait \u2014 but maybe with M_AND1 the LATCH: irqActive[3] set on slot 3; slot 1: enable[1] = MASK1 = 0 (not written in E) \u2192 else branch \u2192 deassert cpu_irq[1] if irqActive[1]... The walk order: cpu 0: enable[0]=MASK0=0 (not written? MASK3 written = MASK0+12; MASK0 itself not written) \u2192 slot 0: status & 0 = 0 \u2192 else, latch 0 \u2192 nothing. Slot 1: enable[1] = 0 \u2192 else, latch[1] 0 \u2192 nothing. Slot 2: enable 0 \u2192 nothing. Slot 3: enable = MASK3 bit 26, sw \u2192 status bit 26 \u2192 assert cpu_irq[1]. So cpu 1 takes it. Breakpoint hit by cpu 1. cpu3 deliver row: hit=True \u2192 value(pcs[3] == VBR+8) \u2192 False \u2192 BADREGS. But table says cpu3 deliver STOP for M_AND1!\n\nUnless... the breakpoint mechanism: `cont_until_breakpoint` \u2014 when cpu 1 hits the breakpoint, the emulator stops ALL cpus? Then pcs[3] = PARK. hit=True. Row = BADREGS. Table says STOP. Contradiction!\n\nHmm wait, let me re-read the table: \"M_AND1: `cpu_irq[cpu & 1]` at ASSERT and DEASSERT | 25/27 FAIL | cpu3 deliver STOP, others idle BADREGS (cpu 1 took slot 3's interrupt)\". Hmm. \"cpu 1 took slot 3's interrupt\" \u2014 so cpu 1 DID enter the vector. Then how is cpu3 deliver STOP (no vector entry within the wait)?? If cpu 1 entered VBR+8, the breakpoint was hit \u2192 cpu3 deliver would be BADREGS (pcs[3] != VBR+8), and others idle would be... pcs[0..2]: cpu 1 at VBR+8 \u2192 idle False \u2192 BADREGS \u2713 (matches). But cpu3 deliver STOP contradicts \"cpu 1 took the interrupt\".\n\nUnless the STOP comes from a DIFFERENT mechanism: hmm, wait \u2014 maybe with M_AND1, cpu 1 is parked at PARK with a breakpoint... no. Let me re-trace: does cpu 1 actually take the interrupt? cpu 1 parked at `br .` (busy loop), PSR 0x80000000 \u2014 interrupt enable bit? For 88k, PSR bit... IND? The probe uses PSR 0x80000000 and says a cpu at VBR+8 with PSR 0x8000000b / EPSR 0x80000000 is the channel. PSR 0x80000000 \u2014 hmm, is the interrupt-enable bit set in 0x80000000? 88100 PSR: bit 31 is... PSR has IND (interrupt disable) at bit 19? Hmm \u2014 the packet says \"PSR 0x8000000b\" at the vector \u2014 so 0x80000000 | 0xb. Whatever \u2014 the probe's convention: parked with PSR 0x80000000 \u2192 interrupts enabled (else delivery rows would never work). \u2713 \u2014 session C works this way (cpu1 deliver OK on baseline).\n\nSo with M_AND1, cpu 1 should enter \u2192 hit \u2192 cpu3 deliver BADREGS. Table says STOP. INCONSISTENCY \u2014 unless... hmm, wait. Let me reconsider: M_AND1 at ASSERT and DEASSERT: slot 3 asserts cpu_irq[1]. But ALSO: the walk for slot 1... no wait. Hmm, actually \u2014 hold on: which slot's pending bit... the SOFT_INT3 store sets software_interrupt_status[3]. The walk: slot 3 \u2192 assert cpu_irq[3&1] = cpu_irq[1] \u2192 INTERRUPT_ASSERT(cpu 1's istruct) \u2192 cpu 1 interrupted. Also slot 1: enable[1] = 0 \u2192 else \u2192 irqActive[1]... the latch is per-slot: irqActive[1] (slot 1's latch) \u2014 0 \u2192 no deassert call. Note: the DEASSERT of cpu_irq[1] would only happen if slot 1's latch were set \u2014 it's per-slot, so slot 1's else-branch doesn't deassert cpu 1's line (which slot 3 just asserted through cpu_irq[1]). Hmm interesting \u2014 so with the alias, slot 1's else branch: `if (d->irqActive[1]) INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(cpu_irq[1&1]=cpu_irq[1])` \u2014 irqActive[1] is slot 1's latch = 0 \u2192 no call. OK so cpu 1 stays asserted \u2192 enters vector \u2192 breakpoint hit.\n\nSo table's \"cpu3 deliver STOP\" for M_AND1 looks WRONG \u2014 should be BADREGS. Unless the breakpoint only triggers for the FOCUSED cpu or... `s.cont_until_breakpoint(15.0)` \u2014 the probe's breakpoint at VBR+8; when ANY cpu reaches it, the emulator halts (gxemul singlestep/breakpoints are per-cpu? In gxemul, breakpoints might be per-cpu!). Hmm \u2014 gxemul debugger: `breakpoint add ADDR` \u2014 is it per-machine or per-cpu? In gxemul, I believe breakpoints are per-CPU (each cpu has its own breakpoints) or global? Actually in GXemul, the debugger commands like \"breakpoint\" are per-CPU (you focus a cpu and add breakpoints to IT). Hmm! If the breakpoint is attached to the FOCUSED cpu (cpu 0 \u2014 `s.send(\"focus 0\")` earlier), then cpu 1 entering VBR+8 does NOT hit the breakpoint \u2192 cont_until_breakpoint times out (15 s) \u2192 hit False \u2192 cpu3 deliver STOP, pcs[3] = PARK... then others idle: pcs[0..2] \u2014 cpu 1 at VBR+8 \u2192 BADREGS \u2713. That MATCHES the table!\n\nBut wait \u2014 then on the BASELINE, session E: cpu 3 enters VBR+8 \u2014 would the breakpoint (on cpu 0?) hit? The probe does `s.send(\"focus 0\")` before stores, then `breakpoint add VBR+8`. If breakpoints are per-cpu (cpu 0's), cpu 3's entry wouldn't hit \u2192 cpu3 deliver STOP on baseline \u2014 but the table says baseline is 27/27 PASS. Contradiction with per-cpu breakpoints. So breakpoints must be GLOBAL (any cpu hitting \u2192 emulator stops). Then M_AND1 \u2192 cpu 1 hits \u2192 BADREGS, contradicting STOP.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 unless with M_AND1 something prevents cpu 1 from entering. Let me re-check: is cpu 1's PSR set with interrupts enabled? Session E sets cpus 1..3: VBR, PSR 0x80000000, pc=PARK. Yes. Hmm.\n\nOR \u2014 maybe with M_AND1, the walk order matters: slot 3's ASSERT happens, but THEN... no, slot 3 is last. Hmm.\n\nOR \u2014 wait, maybe M_AND1's alias also affects the LATCH writes? No, M_AND1 is only cpu_irq[cpu & 1].\n\nHmm, let me reconsider: maybe the alias `cpu & 1` \u2014 slot 3 \u2192 cpu_irq[1] \u2014 INTERRUPT_ASSERT(cpu 1) \u2192 cpu 1 enters VBR+8 \u2192 hits breakpoint \u2192 emulator stops all \u2192 pcs: cpu1 = VBR+8, cpu3 = PARK \u2192 cpu3 deliver = BADREGS (hit=True, value fails). So table should read \"cpu3 deliver BADREGS, others idle BADREGS\" \u2192 25/27 \u2713 score matches (2 red) but the RESULT WORD for cpu3 deliver is stated as STOP. Score 25/27 is consistent either way (2 red rows). So the defect is only in the red-row WORD (STOP vs BADREGS), not the score. Hmm \u2014 is that a \"defect\"? The table's reading column says \"cpu 1 took slot 3's interrupt\" \u2014 which implies cpu 1 entered \u2014 which implies the breakpoint was hit \u2014 which implies cpu3 deliver = BADREGS not STOP. So the table is internally inconsistent on the WORD for M_AND1's cpu3 deliver. Minor, but it IS an inconsistency in the packet AS STATED. Hmm \u2014 but is it one of the NINE dispositions? The nine dispositions are: capture move, three new sessions, two disclosed equivalents/debts, three filings. The M_AND1 row is a killtable row, not one of the nine. Hmm.\n\nWait \u2014 maybe I should reconsider whether \"cpu3 deliver STOP\" could be right. What if, with M_AND1, cpu 1 does NOT take the interrupt because... the busy-loop `br .` at PARK \u2014 cpu 1 executes `br .` forever. INTERRUPT_ASSERT on cpu 1 \u2192 cpu 1 vectors to VBR+8. That requires cpu 1's PSR interrupt-enable. PSR 0x80000000 \u2014 hmm, for M88K: PSR bit 31 (0x80000000) is... PSR: bits 31..26 = mode bits (kernel mode etc.), IND is bit 19 (0x80000). Hmm! If IND (interrupt disable) is bit 19, then PSR 0x80000000 has IND CLEAR \u2192 interrupts ENABLED. And at the vector, PSR becomes 0x8000000b (0xb = bits 0,1,3 \u2014 serial mode bits etc.). OK so 0x80000000 = interrupts enabled. \u2713 (session C relies on this).\n\nSo cpu 1 WOULD take it. So \"cpu3 deliver STOP\" is wrong; should be BADREGS. BUT \u2014 hmm, wait: does the emulator's breakpoint stop only the cpu that hit it and continue others? `cont_until_breakpoint` returns hit=True when any cpu hits. Either way hit=True.\n\nHmm, hold on, wait. Let me re-read session E's code once more:\n\n```python\nhit = s.cont_until_breakpoint(15.0)\nif s.dead: ...\npcs = [s.reg(\"pc\", cpu=c) for c in (0, 1, 2, 3)]\nif not hit:\n    row(\"cpu3 deliver\", \"STOP\", ...)\n    s.close(); return\nrow(\"cpu3 deliver\", \"OK\" if value(s, \"E cpu3 pc == VBR+8 ...\", pcs[3], VBR + 8) else \"BADREGS\")\nidle = all(pcs[c] != VBR + 8 for c in (0, 1, 2))\nrow(\"others idle\", ...)\n```\n\nWith M_AND1 (cpu 1 enters): hit=True \u2192 cpu3 deliver = BADREGS; others idle = BADREGS. Table: \"cpu3 deliver STOP, others idle BADREGS\". So the WORD for cpu3 deliver in the table is inconsistent with the code + the reading's own parenthetical. Unless gxemul breakpoints are per-cpu AND session E adds the breakpoint while focused on cpu 0 \u2192 only cpu 0's entry hits \u2192 with M_AND1 cpu 1 enters, no hit \u2192 STOP \u2713, and \"others idle\" computed from pcs \u2014 cpu 1 at VBR+8 \u2192 BADREGS \u2713 \"cpu 1 took slot 3's interrupt\" \u2713. And on BASELINE: cpu 3 enters but breakpoint is cpu-0-scoped \u2192 no hit \u2192 cpu3 deliver STOP \u2192 baseline NOT 27/27. \u2717. Contradiction again \u2014 UNLESS cont_until_breakpoint has another detection... no.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 unless in gxemul, `breakpoint add` is machine-global (any cpu). Then baseline works and M_AND1 gives BADREGS/BADREGS. The table says STOP/BADREGS. Either way there's an inconsistency SOMEWHERE in session E's design vs the table:\n\n- Global breakpoints: baseline 27/27 \u2713, M_AND1 should be cpu3 deliver BADREGS (table says STOP \u2717).\n- Per-cpu breakpoints (on focused cpu 0): M_AND1 STOP \u2713 matches table, but baseline cpu3 deliver would STOP \u2717 (baseline is 27/27).\n\nWait \u2014 per-cpu: what if the probe focuses each parked cpu and adds the breakpoint to each? The inlined code only shows `s.send(\"breakpoint add ...\")` once after `focus 0`. Hmm \u2014 but sessions C/C2 (which work on baseline) also add the breakpoint after focus 0, and cpu 1's entry IS detected (session C: cpu 1 enters its vector, pc == 0x40008 observed). So breakpoints MUST be global (cpu 1's entry detected while focused on cpu 0). Therefore M_AND1's cpu 1 entry \u2192 hit \u2192 cpu3 deliver BADREGS. Table's STOP is WRONG.\n\nHmm hold on, wait. Session C: \"cpu 0 steps MASK1 ... parks; breakpoint VBR+8; `continue`\" \u2192 \"cpu 1 enters its vector (pc == 0x40008), IND set, cpu 0 still parked\". So yes \u2014 the breakpoint catches cpu 1's entry while focus is cpu 0 \u2192 breakpoints are global. CONFIRMED from packet.\n\nSo with M_AND1 in session E: cpu 1 enters VBR+8 \u2192 global breakpoint hit \u2192 hit=True \u2192 cpu3 deliver row = BADREGS (pcs[3]=PARK \u2260 VBR+8). Table says STOP. INCONSISTENT.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 is it possible that with M_AND1, cpu 1 does NOT enter because of the DEASSERT side? Walk order slot 0..3: slot 3 asserts cpu_irq[1]. No later slot deasserts it (slot 3 is last). Slot 1's else-branch: irqActive[1] \u2014 hmm wait, M_AND1's latch is still per-slot (irqActive[cpu], unmodified). Slot 1: status&enable=0 \u2192 else \u2192 if (irqActive[1]) \u2192 0 \u2192 no deassert. \u2713 cpu 1 stays asserted \u2192 enters. Yes.\n\nSo the M_AND1 table row's \"cpu3 deliver STOP\" is internally inconsistent with (a) session C proving global breakpoints, (b) the reading's own parenthetical \"cpu 1 took slot 3's interrupt\", (c) session E's code path (hit=True \u2192 BADREGS branch). The SCORE 25/27 is unaffected (2 red either way). So this is a reporting defect, not a scoring defect. Hmm \u2014 should this drive the verdict? The chain closes on \"zero floor breaches surviving adjudication\" \u2014 a floor breach is presumably a surviving defect in fix/probe. A mislabeled red-row word in the killtable is a records defect... The second question asks which of the NINE dispositions is wrong \u2014 the M_AND1 row isn't one of the nine.\n\nHmm, hmm. Let me re-examine the nine again for the intended \"wrong\" one. Let me re-read the E session's description in the probe docstring: \"session E (`-n 4', FREE-RUNNING) cpus 1..3 parked with VBR/PSR set; MASK3 + SOFT_INT3 from cpu 0 \u2192 four cpus / cpu3 deliver / others idle \u2014 M_AND1 (`cpu_irq[cpu & 1]': slots 2/3 alias onto 0/1) and M_MIN2 (the bound clamped to two): the LAST slot delivers to cpu 3 and to nobody else\".\n\nOK so now \u2014 WAIT. I want to recheck M_MIN2: \"d->ncpus = ncpus > 2 ? 2 : ncpus\" \u2014 on 4 cpus \u2192 2 \u2192 walk slots 0,1 only \u2192 slot 3 never walked \u2192 cpu 3 never delivered \u2192 no vector entry \u2192 breakpoint NOT hit \u2192 hit=False \u2192 cpu3 deliver STOP \u2713 (table: cpu3 deliver STOP \u2713). pcs[3] = PARK \u2192 others idle: cpus 0..2 \u2014 no one entered \u2192 idle OK. So M_MIN2: 1 red (cpu3 deliver), 26/27 \u2713 consistent.\n\nAnd M_AND1: cpu 1 enters \u2192 hit \u2192 BADREGS/BADREGS. Table says STOP/BADREGS. The table's M_AND1 word is wrong. Score consistent.\n\nHmm OK. But which of the NINE is wrong? Let me think about the es438 disposition more. \"es438: its owed mask2/mask3 pair is discharged by this detector's rows -- annotate/close on this round.\"\n\nHmm \u2014 grok's pass-2 finding: \"three of the main loop's 'unchanged' dispositions named rows that are not ledger rows (`lunadefaultstop`, `es438cells`, `lunaabsentmaskdecl` -- `es438` is the row)\". So the ledger row is `es438` (not es438cells). And es438 owed a mask2/mask3 pair. The detector has mask1/mask2/mask3 readback rows. So es438's debt is discharged \u2192 close. Hmm, plausible.\n\nBut WAIT \u2014 do the mask2/mask3 rows actually exist in probe v3? The v2\u2192v3 diff shows only the CONTRACT line \"a declined store to an absent slot (mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks)\" as CONTEXT (unchanged between v2 and v3) \u2014 wait, actually, let me re-read the contract diff:\n\n```\n- bound never assigned (session B), the bound hardcoded to 1 (session C), a declined store to an absent slot (mask1/mask2/\n- mask3 readbacks), and the third/fourth MASK labels.  Does NOT catch: ...\n+ bound never assigned (session B), the bound hardcoded to 1 (session C) or clamped to 2 (session E), slots 2/3 aliased onto 0/1\n+ (session E), a device line masked out of the walk or a walk of software-pending slots only (session B2), the per-slot latch\n+ collapsed onto slot 0 (session C2), a declined store to an absent slot (mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks), and the third/fourth MASK\n+ labels.  Does NOT catch: ...\n```\n\nSo v2 already had mask1/mask2/mask3 readback rows (claimed). v3 keeps them. And the base is 27 rows: v2 had 20 rows; v3 adds B2 (1), C2 (1), E (3) = 5 new \u2192 25?? But base is 27! Hmm! 20 + 5 = 25 \u2260 27. Wait \u2014 \"27 rows\" in the header: \"the CLOSING pass on the pass-2 repair (fix v2 + probe v3, 27 rows)\". And killtable2 BASE 27/27. And the v2-era measurements: \"measured 20/20\" (M_NULLAB in v2, corrected to \"measured 22/22 in killtable/\"). Hmm \u2014 so v2 was 20 rows; killtable (pass-2 table) was 22 rows?? \"measured 22/22 in killtable/\" \u2014 so by the time of killtable (pass 2's table?), the probe had 22 rows? Confusing. Let me recount v3's rows: 27. New sessions in v3: B2 (1 row: sio5 deliver), C2 (1 row: both deliver), E (3 rows: four cpus, cpu3 deliver, others idle) = 5 new rows. 27 - 5 = 22 \u2192 v2-final had 22 rows (matching \"measured 22/22 in killtable/\"). And the \"20/20\" was an EARLIER v2 state (the docstring's stale prediction \u2014 \"The header's stale predictions (20/20, 20/20 + 8/8) were corrected\"). OK consistent: v2 started at 20 rows, grew to 22 during pass 2 (killtable/), v3 = 27.\n\nNow session C's v2\u2192v3 diff line: \"measured 19/20, cpu1 deliver alone red\" \u2014 hmm, 19/20?? If v2-final was 22 rows, why 20? \"M_HARD1 (`d->ncpus = 1' hardcoded -- the clause-(d) null repair: ... measured 19/20, cpu1 deliver alone red)\". Hmm \u2014 this is in the SESSION C description \u2014 maybe the 20 refers to a session-C-era measurement when the probe had 20 rows. M_HARD1 with 22-row probe: red rows would be cpu1 deliver (C), both deliver (C2 didn't exist then)... hmm, in v2 (22 rows): M_HARD1 red: cpu1 deliver + any 2+-cpu row = cpu1 deliver alone? 22-row v2: sessions A2, B, C (7 rows), D \u2014 with -n 2 rows: cpu1 deliver, cpu1 shadow?, cpu0 parked? M_HARD1 in killtable2 (27 rows): red = both deliver, cpu1 deliver, cpu3 deliver (3 red, 21/24 \u2014 wait denominator 24 for M_HARD1: 27-24=3 rows lost; 24 ran, 3 red \u2192 21 OK \u2713).\n\nHmm \u2014 the \"19/20\" for M_HARD1 in v2: 20 rows ran, cpu1 deliver alone red \u2192 19 OK. So in the 20-row era (early v2? or v1?), M_HARD1 was ALREADY caught by cpu1 deliver (not a TOTAL ESCAPE). And the v2 docstring CLAIMED \"TOTAL ESCAPE ... measured 20/20\" \u2014 which was WRONG, corrected in v3 to \"19/20, cpu1 deliver alone red\". So the v2 docstring had a stale/wrong claim, corrected in v3. Fine \u2014 \"The header's stale predictions (20/20, 20/20 + 8/8) were corrected\". Hmm wait, but that says the HEADER's stale predictions were corrected \u2014 and this 19/20 correction is in the session-C description. OK, part of the same corrections. Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 WHICH OF THE NINE IS WRONG? Let me think about the capture move once more. Actually \u2014 WAIT. Let me reconsider the capture-site claim: \"Captured BELOW the `ncpus > 4' exit so the bound is <= MAX_CPUS by construction\". Hmm \u2014 \"\u2264 MAX_CPUS\" \u2014 the exit is `ncpus > 4`, so below it ncpus \u2264 4. For the bound to be \u2264 MAX_CPUS, we need MAX_CPUS \u2265 4. If MAX_CPUS = 4, fine. The comment says \"the bound is <= MAX_CPUS by construction\" \u2014 with the exit at 4 and MAX_CPUS = 4, ncpus \u2264 4 = MAX_CPUS \u2713. OK.\n\nBut hmm \u2014 WAIT: is MAX_CPUS really 4? If MAX_CPUS were 4, then the v2-era M_REVERT (walk to MAX_CPUS) on a 4-cpu machine walks 0..3, all connected \u2192 fine. The premises say DEVINIT connects slots 1..3 under ncpus \u2265 2/3/4 and exits if ncpus > 4 \u2014 consistent with MAX_CPUS = 4. Also \"slot set 0..3\" in the enumeration. OK.\n\nNow, let me look for the INTENDED defect. The question says \"WHICH IS WRONG?\" \u2014 with the count named (nine). Maybe the intended answer: the es438 disposition is wrong because... hmm. Or maybe the intended answer is about the CONTRACT's equivalence claim. Or maybe none is wrong and the honest answer is \"none \u2014 but here's what I can't settle\". The panel instructions reward honest \"cannot settle\" over confident guessing. But the question is phrased as if one IS wrong (\"WHICH IS WRONG?\"). It could be a test: the correct answer might be \"none of the nine is wrong as stated\" \u2014 or the packet contains exactly one internal contradiction among the nine.\n\nLet me re-scan the nine with fresh eyes:\n\n1. **The capture move** (capture below the ncpus>4 exit). Internally consistent. Sound. \u2713.\n\n2. **Session B2** (sio5 deliver): 1 cpu, MASK0 bit 30, SIO WR1 TX_IE. Checks: M_MASK30 killed (sio5 deliver STOP \u2713 \u2014 wait: M_MASK30 masks bit 30 out of status \u2192 line 5 pending but status bit 30 cleared \u2192 no delivery \u2192 sio5 deliver STOP \u2713 26/27). M_SWONLY: skips slots without sw pending \u2192 slot 0 has NO sw pending (only device line 5) \u2192 continue \u2192 no delivery \u2192 STOP \u2713. Good. B2 passes on HEAD \u2713 (slot 0 connected; walk 1..3: enable 0 \u2192 else, latch 0 \u2192 no crash). \u2713 sound.\n\n   Hmm wait \u2014 one check on B2: the SIO stores: `store(s, SIOCMD0, 0x01)` then `store(s, SIOCMD0, 0x02)` \u2014 \"WR1 select, then TX_IE\". Hmm \u2014 SIOCMD0 is the SIO command register address. Storing 0x01 then 0x02 \u2014 the comment says \"WR1 select, then TX_IE -- sio_tx_pending raises line 5\". Plausible (z8530-style: WR0 select WR1, then next store goes to WR1). The packet doesn't inline dev_sio behavior \u2014 premise not fully checkable, but it's the probe's own mechanics, measured rows confirm (M_MASK30 red means on baseline line 5 delivered). READ from table.\n\n3. **Session C2** (both deliver): -n 2, MASK0+MASK1 bit 26, SOFT0+SOFT1. Both cpus must enter. M_IRQACTIVE0 killed: with latch collapsed to [0]: walk slot 0: status&enable \u2192 assert cpu_irq[0] if !irqActive[0] (0) \u2192 assert, irqActive[0]=1. Slot 1: status&enable \u2192 if (!irqActive[0]) \u2192 1 \u2192 NO assert for cpu 1! \u2192 cpu 1 never enters \u2192 both deliver STOP \u2713 (table \u2713). Sound. Also catches the ORDER: \"the first arrival is parked off the vector, then continue again\" \u2014 the probe handles observing the second arrival \u2713 (premise 4).\n\n   Hmm wait \u2014 with M_IRQACTIVE0, cpu 0 DOES enter (first arrival parked off vector), cpu 1 doesn't \u2192 entered={0} \u2192 STOP \u2713.\n\n4. **Session E**: as analyzed \u2014 design sound; the M_AND1 table WORD issue is a killtable reporting defect, not a session defect. Hmm \u2014 but actually wait, let me recheck whether the M_AND1 row really is inconsistent, because if the breakpoint IS global, M_AND1 gives hit=True \u2192 the code rows cpu3 deliver as BADREGS... The table says STOP. One of them is wrong. UNLESS \u2014 hmm \u2014 WAIT: with M_AND1, does cpu 1 actually enter VBR+8? Let me re-examine: `INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->cpu_irq[cpu & 1])` \u2014 M_AND1 replaces `cpu_irq[cpu]` with `cpu_irq[cpu & 1]` at ASSERT and DEASSERT. Slot 3 \u2192 cpu_irq[1] \u2014 cpu 1's interrupt struct \u2014 assert \u2192 cpu 1 vectors. BUT: hold on \u2014 was cpu 1's PSR set BEFORE the interrupt? Yes: cpus 1..3 parked with VBR/PSR set first. \u2713. So cpu 1 enters \u2192 global breakpoint hit \u2192 hit=True \u2192 \"cpu3 deliver\" = OK if pcs[3]==VBR+8 else BADREGS \u2192 pcs[3] = PARK \u2192 BADREGS. Table: STOP. INCONSISTENT \u2014 the table's M_AND1 row contradicts the inlined session-E code IF breakpoints are global (which session C's v2 description establishes). Hmm, unless \"STOP\" in the table's shorthand means something looser... The RESULT words are defined: OK / HALT / BADREGS / STOP / NOSTART. STOP = \"no vector entry before the wait ran out\". With cpu 1 entering, there WAS a vector entry within the wait. So STOP is wrong; BADREGS is right. This is a real internal inconsistency in Table 2's M_AND1 row \u2014 READ+REASONED. But again: not one of the NINE dispositions; it's a defect in the killtable's red-row words. It could flip a naive adjudication of M_AND1's reading but the score (25/27 FAIL) is right either way. I should report it as a secondary defect. Hmm, hmm.\n\n5. **M_NULLAB equivalence disclosure**: sound (argued above). \u2713.\n\n6. **M_NOREASSERT debt disclosure**: \"a deleted reassert in the SOFT arm alone (the clock's own reassert carries the delivery; disclosed debt)\". Hmm \u2014 WAIT. Let me scrutinize this harder. The SOFT arm's reassert removed \u2192 delivery carried by \"the clock's own reassert\". But the clock route is ALSO disclosed as not steppable/host-timer-driven \u2014 \"the clock/timer route (host-timer driven, not steppable -- same loop as sio5)\" is in the DOES-NOT-CATCH list. So the probe RELIES on the clock (to carry delivery in M_NOREASSERT) while also disclosing it can't control the clock. Is that a contradiction? Not really: the clock fires often enough that within 15 s it walks. Measured 27/27 PASS for M_NOREASSERT. Hmm \u2014 but here's a thought: is M_NOREASSERT actually non-equivalent in a way that MATTERS? Yes: without the SOFT arm's walk call, an IPI is delivered only on the next clock tick \u2014 a latency regression. Disclosed as debt \u2713 honest.\n\n   Hmm \u2014 but WAIT, actually: is the M_NOREASSERT disclosure one of the \"two disclosed equivalents/debts\"? Yes: \"two disclosed equivalents/debts\" = M_NULLAB (equivalent) + M_NOREASSERT (debt). OK.\n\n7. **lunadefaultstop LOW**: \"the switch's `default:` graceful stop plus the unimplemented RESET_CPU0..3 / SOFT_INT_FLAG* arms\". Filed. Hmm \u2014 is filing it as LOW right? It's a pre-existing upstream gap (unimplemented register arms). LOW plausible. \u2713.\n\n8. **lunaabsentmaskdecl LOW documented-only**: the absent slots' MASK/SOFT registers stay guest-writable storage with readback. Hmm \u2014 is this the right call? Board-model question: on real hardware, MASK1..3 registers exist (the LUNA-88K has 4 CPUs max; the registers exist regardless). Keeping them storage-with-readback mirrors hardware. Design hold from pass 1. \u2713 plausible.\n\n9. **es438 annotate/close**: \"its owed mask2/mask3 pair is discharged by this detector's rows\". Hmm \u2014 the detector's mask2/mask3 readback rows exist (contract). So es438's owed pair is discharged. But WAIT \u2014 grok's pass-2 finding says the main loop's \"unchanged\" dispositions \"named rows that are not ledger rows (`lunadefaultstop`, `es438cells`, `lunaabsentmaskdecl` -- `es438` is the row)\". So the LEDGER row is es438, and the disposition was to annotate/close es438 this round. Consistent.\n\nHmm. So which of the nine is WRONG? Let me re-read the second question: \"Second question, with the count named: of the nine pass-3 dispositions (the capture move, three new sessions, two disclosed equivalents/debts, three filings/annotations -- lunadefaultstop, lunaabsentmaskdecl, es438), WHICH IS WRONG?\"\n\nMaybe the intended catch: the count is wrong?? \"of the nine pass-3 dispositions (the capture move [1], three new sessions [2,3,4], two disclosed equivalents/debts [5,6], three filings/annotations [7,8,9])\" \u2014 1+3+2+3 = 9 \u2713. The count is right.\n\nHmm \u2014 OR the intended catch is one of the dispositions is NOT a pass-3 disposition (it's a pass-2 disposition)? E.g., the capture move was made in pass 2's repair (fix v2)... no \u2014 fix v2 IS the pass-2 repair; pass 3 reviews it. The \"pass-3 dispositions\" = dispositions made in THIS packet. Hmm.\n\nOR \u2014 the intended catch: \"two disclosed equivalents/debts\" \u2014 are there really TWO? M_NULLAB (equivalent) and M_NOREASSERT (debt) \u2014 yes two. Hmm.\n\nOR \u2014 the intended catch: one of the three filings is wrong \u2014 e.g., es438's disposition. Let me think: es438 \"owed mask2/mask3 pair\" \u2014 but is that discharged by THIS detector's rows? The detector's mask rows are \"mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks\" \u2014 but WAIT: are the mask2/mask3 rows in probe v3, or were they in a DIFFERENT detector? The contract says the probe catches \"a declined store to an absent slot (mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks)\". Hmm \u2014 but hold on: \"a DECLINED store\"? The readback rows check that a store to an absent slot's MASK is NOT declined (stays storage with readback \u2014 lunaabsentmaskdecl). So mask1/2/3 readback rows exist in the probe. es438's owed pair (mask2/mask3) \u2286 {mask1, mask2, mask3} \u2192 discharged. \u2713.\n\nHmm, hmm. Let me look at the \"records this round carries\" section again very carefully:\n\n\"File as rows: `lunadefaultstop` LOW (the switch's `default:` graceful stop plus the unimplemented RESET_CPU0..3 / SOFT_INT_FLAG* arms -- pass-1 F13 said \"file separately\" and nobody did), `lunaabsentmaskdecl` LOW documented-only (the absent slots' registers stay storage with readback -- the design hold from pass 1).  `es438`: its owed mask2/mask3 pair is discharged by this detector's rows -- annotate/close on this round.  The ledger witness field becomes `probe:regress/luna_absentcpu_probe.py` at closure.\"\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, \"the ledger witness field becomes `probe:regress/luna_absentcpu_probe.py`\" \u2014 but grok's pass-2 finding said \"the ledger's witness field is still `none`\" \u2014 and this round sets it. But \u2014 the probe file: the diff shows TWO files: `regress/artifacts/lunaabsentcpu/luna_absentcpu_probe.v2.py` \u2192 `luna_absentcpu_probe.v3.py`, and the brief's context says the detector is `regress/luna_absentcpu_probe.py`. So there's the canonical `regress/luna_absentcpu_probe.py` and versioned artifacts. The witness field points to the canonical. Presumably the canonical is updated to v3 (frozen md5). Can't verify from packet \u2014 minor.\n\nOK here's another angle: maybe the intended WRONG disposition is the **capture move** \u2014 because of a subtle flaw: the capture is below the exit, but is it below the CONNECTS? \"DEVINIT connects slot 0 always and slots 1..3 under ncpus >= 2/3/4; later in DEVINIT ncpus > 4 prints and exit(1)s.\" \u2014 connects FIRST, then exit, then capture. Fine. The bound is assigned after connects \u2014 no walk call in between (walk callers are guest-triggered + device events; DEVINIT doesn't call the walk... unless a device asserts during DEVINIT \u2014 clock starts later). OK fine.\n\nHmm, what about: `d->ncpus = devinit->machine->ncpus;` \u2014 but premise 2 says \"emul.c maps ncpus 0 -> 1\". So machine->ncpus \u2265 1 always. \u2713.\n\nHmm, what about the SECOND part of the fix comment: \"It walked all MAX_CPUS and called the NULL interrupt_assert of an unpopulated slot: on the default one-cpu machine a guest store of 0xfc000000 to INT_ST_MASK1 followed by a store to SOFT_INT1 (or any device line whose bit that mask enables) ended the emulator process with a segmentation fault -- measured, regress/luna_absentcpu_probe.py.\"\n\nCheck: 0xfc000000 = bits 26..31 (0xfc000000 = 1111 1100 ... 0 \u2014 bits 26-31 set: 0x04000000 (26), 0x08000000 (27), 0x10000000 (28), 0x20000000 (29), 0x40000000 (30), 0x80000000 (31) \u2014 sum = 0xfc000000 \u2713). So MASK1 = all six bits \u2192 then SOFT_INT1 \u2192 slot 1: status = 0 (no device) | bit 26 (sw) = bit 26; enable = 0xfc000000 \u2192 nonzero \u2192 assert cpu_irq[1] \u2192 NULL \u2192 segfault \u2713. Consistent. \u2713.\n\nOK here's yet another angle on Q1 \u2014 maybe the intended clean-sweep: **the `else` branch's DEASSERT on a connected slot** \u2014 disclosed as not caught (\"no row observes a post-clear deassert free-running: handlers are `br .` with IND set and never rte\"). So a mutant breaking the DEASSERT path (e.g., `if (d->irqActive[cpu])` \u2192 `if (0)`, or removing the DEASSERT call) sweeps. Is that in the (d) enumeration? The enumeration: \"the body-read fields ... `irqActive[cpu]`, `cpu_irq[cpu]`; joint cells where the body tests them ({slot} x {pending: sw, device line, none} x {irqActive})\". The joint cell {connected slot} \u00d7 {pending: none} \u00d7 {irqActive: SET} \u2014 that's the DEASSERT path: a connected slot with a previously-asserted latch that becomes unpending \u2192 DEASSERT. No row observes it (disclosed). So a mutant deleting the DEASSERT call (or inverting the latch test) is a clean sweep BY THE PACKET'S OWN DISCLOSURE. But it's DISCLOSED \u2014 disclosed debt doesn't count as a \"floor breach surviving adjudication\"? Hmm \u2014 the question asks: \"Does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration of the SHIPPED COMPOSITE?\" \u2014 the enumeration includes irqActive[cpu] and the joint cells {pending: none} \u00d7 {irqActive}. The deassert path IS in the enumeration. And the packet ITSELF discloses it's not caught (\"a connected slot's DEASSERT ... the `irqActive[cpu] = 0` clear itself (same reason)\"). So YES \u2014 a clean-sweep mutant exists in the enumeration, and the packet DISCLOSES it: e.g., M_NODEASSERT: delete `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->cpu_irq[cpu]);` \u2014 sweeps 27/27, non-equivalent (a connected slot's line never deasserts \u2192 the cpu never sees the interrupt clear... well, IND set at handler, never rte, so the probe can't tell; but a real guest that rte's would re-take the interrupt forever \u2014 actually if the line stays asserted, rte \u2192 immediate re-entry \u2192 the guest thinks the device never cleared the interrupt \u2192 real defect). So the (d) enumeration contains at least the disclosed deassert cell \u2014 the honest answer: the enumeration includes cells the probe has ALREADY disclosed as uncatchable (deassert/clear), so \"zero clean sweeps in (d)\" is FALSE as literally asked \u2014 unless disclosed cells are excluded from \"the enumeration\". Hmm \u2014 the question: \"Does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration of the SHIPPED COMPOSITE (fix v2 + probe v3)?\" \u2014 if the answer intended \"no, close\", then the disclosed cells must be OUT of (d). But the enumeration as WRITTEN includes \"joint cells where the body tests them ({slot} \u00d7 {pending: sw, device line, none} \u00d7 {irqActive})\" \u2014 {pending: none} \u00d7 {irqActive: set} is EXACTLY the disclosed-uncatchable deassert cell. So as WRITTEN, the answer is YES: the deassert cell admits clean sweeps, e.g.:\n\n- M_NODEASSERT: remove the `if (d->irqActive[cpu]) INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(...)` call \u2014 sweeps (no row observes a deassert).\n- M_LATCHCLEAR: `d->irqActive[cpu] = 0;` \u2192 `= 1;` hmm \u2014 wait: else-branch sets latch 0; if set to 1: next walk with pending: `if (!d->irqActive[cpu])` \u2192 1 \u2192 skip ASSERT \u2192 the slot never re-asserts after a clear... but the probe never rte's, so within a row, does the walk run twice with the latch in the wrong state? The clock calls the walk repeatedly! Session B2: line 5 pending, clock walks repeatedly: first walk asserts (latch 0\u21921). Later walks: status&enable nonzero \u2192 `if (!irqActive)` \u2192 latch=1 \u2192 skip \u2192 fine, no deassert needed. Delivery still OK \u2192 sweeps. And non-equivalent? With latch-clear broken (=1), a deassert scenario: slot pending \u2192 latch 1; then device clears (status&enable=0) \u2192 else \u2192 deassert called (latch was 1) \u2192 line deasserted, latch set to 1 (broken) \u2014 wait the mutant sets it to 1 instead of 0 \u2014 then next pending: `if (!irqActive[cpu])` \u2192 false \u2192 NO ASSERT \u2192 delivery LOST after a clear-then-repend cycle. Real defect. And the probe: never observes (handlers never rte, IND set). Sweep.\n\nHmm \u2014 but BOTH of these are exactly the disclosed \"Does NOT catch: a connected slot's DEASSERT ... the `irqActive[cpu] = 0` clear itself (same reason)\". So they're DISCLOSED. The question's enumeration, though, INCLUDES those cells. So there's a tension: the question asks \"does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) enumeration\" and the packet's own disclosure says YES for the deassert/clear cells. So either:\n(a) The intended answer: NO clean sweep \u2014 because the disclosed cells are excluded (the enumeration implicitly means UNDISCLOSED cells), or\n(b) The intended answer: YES \u2014 the deassert cell, but it's disclosed, so it's debt not a breach.\n\nThe question says: \"If you find one, give the exact edit and the row that should have caught it (a predicted sweep is a measurement owed -- say so); if none, CLOSE with your own denominator.\"\n\n\"a predicted sweep is a measurement owed\" \u2014 i.e., if I PREDICT a mutant sweeps, the panel owes a measurement of it. So finding one \u2192 ONE MORE REPAIR? Hmm \u2014 the verdict options: CLOSE THE CHAIN / ONE MORE REPAIR: <which> / DO NOT SHIP.\n\nNow \u2014 my per-device-line finding (lines 1-4,6 unwitnessed) is NOT disclosed: the contract CLAIMS \"a device line masked out of the walk\" is CAUGHT (via B2) \u2014 but that's only line 5. The enumeration says \"interrupt_status (per device line)\" \u2014 six lines. The probe witnesses line 5 only. A mutant masking any OTHER line's bit out of the device status read sweeps 27/27 AND is non-equivalent. Is it disclosed? NO \u2014 the contract claims device-line masking is CAUGHT. So THIS is an UNDISCLOSED clean-sweep in the (d) enumeration. That's a floor breach: the probe's contract overclaims; the composite doesn't cover the per-device-line cells it enumerates.\n\nWait \u2014 but hold on. Can the mutant `status = d->interrupt_status & ~(1 << 26)` (mask line 1's device bit) actually sweep? Let me re-verify against ALL 27 rows. Rows I know: soft1 (B), siofirst (B), sio5 (B), slot0 deliver (A2/B), mask1/2/3 readbacks, survive, two cpus, cpu1 setup, mask1 land, cpu1 deliver, cpu1 shadow, cpu0 parked (C), nodeliver ctl (D), sio5 deliver (B2), both deliver (C2), four cpus, cpu3 deliver, others idle (E). That's ~19 named; 27 total (some unnamed in packet). Which rows raise device lines other than 5? \"siofirst\" \u2014 SIO first? maybe \"the SIO reaches the walk through the third AND fourth callers\" \u2014 siofirst might be: SIO line raised BEFORE the soft store (so the FIRST walk through the device caller hits the absent slot) \u2014 on 1 cpu with MASK1 armed? Hmm: \"siofirst HALT\" on HEAD/M_REVERT \u2014 siofirst crashes the unfixed walk \u2192 it must make the walk reach an UNCONNECTED slot: 1 cpu, MASK1 armed (enable[1] \u2260 0) + a device line pending \u2192 slot 1: status&enable \u2260 0 \u2192 assert cpu_irq[1] \u2192 NULL \u2192 crash. WHICH device line? If siofirst uses SIO line 5 (bit 30) with MASK1 = 0x40000000 \u2014 then it's line 5 again. If it uses another line... unknown. The packet doesn't say. So: does ANY row deliver device line 1,2,3,4,6? From the packet: only line 5 is named (B2's bit 30, sio5). The clock's line is raised in the background in every free-running session \u2014 which line is the clock? If the clock's line is, say, line 2 (bit 27), and NO MASK ever enables bit 27, the clock's assert delivers nothing and can't witness a masked bit 27. Hmm \u2014 BUT WAIT: does any row enable a mask bit corresponding to the clock's line? The rows use 0x04000000 (bit 26) and 0x40000000 (bit 30). If the clock's line were line 1 (bit 26)... then in sw rows, bit 26 is enabled \u2014 and the clock's assert would deliver bit 26 even without the SOFT arm... hmm, that would make soft1 row pass even with the SOFT reassert removed \u2014 consistent with M_NOREASSERT passing! Interesting \u2014 but ALSO: if the clock's line is bit 26, then in session C (MASK1 bit 26, no SOFT store? no \u2014 C HAS the SOFT_INT1 store) \u2014 can't distinguish. Hmm \u2014 in session D (control: same as C WITHOUT the SOFT_INT1 store): \"NO vector entry within the wait\" \u2014 if the clock's line were bit 26, the clock's periodic asserts would deliver bit 26 to slot 1 (MASK1 enables it) \u2192 vector entry \u2192 nodeliver ctl would FAIL on baseline. Baseline is 27/27 \u2192 nodeliver ctl OK \u2192 the clock's line is NOT bit 26 (or the clock never asserts in the window \u2014 but M_NOREASSERT's pass says it does). So the clock's line \u2209 {26} and its bit is never enabled in any row \u2192 the clock's line is unwitnessed too.\n\nSo: device lines witnessed by delivery rows: line 5 only. Lines 1,2,3,4,6 (and the clock's line): no delivery rows. UNLESS siofirst/sio5 rows in session B use a different line \u2014 sio5 by name is line 5. \"siofirst\" \u2014 hmm, could be \"SIO first\" ordering (line 5 before soft1). Probably line 5.\n\nBUT \u2014 the counter-argument: is a per-line masking mutant REACHABLE as a mutation of the SHIPPED FIX? The enumeration is of the depended-on surface \u2014 the walk's body reads interrupt_status. A mutant `status = d->interrupt_status & ~(1 << 27)` (drop line 2) \u2014 non-equivalent (line 2 delivery lost), sweeps the probe (no row raises line 2 with its bit enabled). This is squarely in \"(per device line)\". So: YES, a clean-sweep mutant exists \u2014 e.g., `status = d->interrupt_status & ~(1 << 27);` \u2014 predicted 27/27 PASS; the row that should have caught it: a 1-cpu row arming MASK0 with bit 27 and raising device line 2 \u2014 IF the probe can raise line 2. CAN it? The probe drives the SIO (line 5). Other lines come from other devices (lan, scsi, kbd?) \u2014 the LUNA-88K's six device lines map to specific devices; the probe would need to trigger those devices. If the probe can't deterministically raise lines 1-4/6, the honest disposition is: the per-line cells for lines 1-4,6 are UNWITNESSABLE by this probe \u2192 the CONTRACT's \"Catches: a device line masked out of the walk\" is TRUE only for line 5 \u2192 the contract overclaims \u2192 the enumeration's per-line cells are disclosed-debt-able, but they are NOT disclosed. THAT's the defect: an undisclosed coverage gap in the contract's \"Catches\" list.\n\nHmm wait \u2014 actually, let me re-read the contract's claim once more: \"Catches: ... a device line masked out of the walk or a walk of software-pending slots only (session B2)\". The claim \"a device line masked out of the walk\" \u2014 with the parenthetical (session B2) \u2014 a careful reader could read it as \"a device line [i.e., THE device line exercised] masked out\" \u2014 but the natural reading is universal (\"a device line\" = any). M_MASK30's kill demonstrates line 5 only. So the contract's universal claim is unproven for 5 of 6 lines. This is a real, packet-internal finding: the CATCHES claim overreaches the measurement. Whether it's a \"floor breach\" \u2014 hmm. The floor: \"zero floor breaches surviving adjudication\". A contract overclaim on an enumerated cell = a defect in the shipped composite's disclosure. The remedy: either add rows (if lines are raisable) or move the per-line cells (except line 5) to the disclosed-debt list. That's a REPAIR to the probe's contract (and possibly rows) \u2192 ONE MORE REPAIR: the per-device-line cells of the (d) enumeration (lines 1-4,6 unwitnessed; contract overclaims \"a device line masked out\").\n\nHmm, but hold on \u2014 is that TOO aggressive? Let me reconsider: is the per-device-line cell really part of what the fix DEPENDS ON? The fix = the bound. The probe must catch mutations of the fix's repair surface AND the walk it bounds... The (d) enumeration given in the question explicitly includes \"the body-read fields `interrupt_status` (per device line)\". So the panel itself enumerates it. And asks: does a clean sweep exist in that enumeration? My answer: YES \u2014 for device lines other than 5 (and the sw bit via the DEVICE path \u2014 bit 26's device read is re-added by the SOFT arm so the device-bit-26 read is unwitnessed too \u2014 actually wait, bit 26: a mutant masking bit 26 from the DEVICE read: sw rows re-add it \u2192 pass; line-1-device rows: none \u2192 sweep. So bit 26's device-path cell is also unwitnessed. And bits 27,28,29,31: no rows at all. So FIVE of SIX per-line cells (line 1 via device path, lines 2,3,4,6 entirely) admit masking mutants that sweep).\n\nAnd is such a mutant non-equivalent? Yes \u2014 real devices raise those lines (the six device lines exist; luna88k_interrupt_assert is called with line 1..6 by real devices). A guest with line 2 pending and MASK enabling bit 27 loses the interrupt. Real defect. So: clean-sweep mutant EXISTS in (d). Predicted sweep \u2192 \"a measurement owed\" \u2014 I must SAY that: the predicted sweep needs a measurement (run M_MASK27 etc. through killtable2).\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 but also the DEASSERT cells (disclosed). The question's enumeration includes {pending: none} \u00d7 {irqActive} cells \u2014 disclosed as not caught. If the panel counts disclosed cells as out-of-scope for \"clean sweep\" (since they're disclosed), then my per-line finding is the only UNDISCLOSED one. The deassert disclosure is itself one of the \"two disclosed equivalents/debts\"... hmm \u2014 no: the two disclosed equivalents/debts are M_NULLAB and M_NOREASSERT. The deassert/clear non-coverage is a DISCLOSED DOES-NOT-CATCH item, not one of the nine dispositions. OK.\n\nSo Q1 answer: YES \u2014 one clean-sweep family in (d): per-device-line masking of interrupt_status for lines other than 5 (bits 26-device-path, 27, 28, 29, 31). Exact edit: `status = d->interrupt_status & ~(1 << 27);` (or equivalently M_MASK30 generalized). Predicted 27/27 PASS \u2014 a measurement owed. The row that should have caught it: a 1-cpu free-running row arming MASK0 with that line's bit and raising that device line (B2's shape, per line) \u2014 IF the line is raisable; if not raisable by any steerable device, the cell must move to the disclosed-debt list and the CONTRACT's \"Catches: a device line masked out of the walk\" must be narrowed to \"line 5\". Since the contract as shipped CLAIMS the catch, the composite misstates its coverage \u2192 verdict: ONE MORE REPAIR (contract/rows for per-line cells), not CLOSE.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 hold on. Let me reconsider whether the clock could witness other lines... The clock's line: raised periodically (host-timer). Its bit is in interrupt_status machine-wide in EVERY session. If a row enabled the clock's bit in a MASK, the clock's line would deliver \u2014 no row does (masks used: bit 26, bit 30, 0xfc000000 in the FIX COMMENT's example \u2014 not a row). Wait \u2014 the fix comment's 0xfc000000 example: \"a guest store of 0xfc000000 to INT_ST_MASK1 followed by a store to SOFT_INT1\" \u2014 that's the BUG description, not a probe row. Probe rows use 0x04000000 and 0x40000000. So no row enables the clock's bit. OK.\n\nHmm \u2014 but WAIT, one more consideration: maybe the probe rows in session A2/B (not fully inlined) DO use other bits! The packet doesn't inline session A2/B's rows fully. soft1: 1-cpu, MASK1 store + SOFT1 store (crashes HEAD). Which MASK1 value? If MASK1 = 0xfc000000 (all bits), then the row enables bits 26-31 \u2014 and the crash happens on SOFT1 store \u2192 the walk sees status bit 26 (sw) & enable \u2192 crash. But device lines 2-4,6: no row RAISES them (a MASK bit alone doesn't raise a line). To witness a masked-out line, a row needs the line RAISED (device event) + its MASK bit enabled + a connected slot. Only the SIO (line 5) is raised deliberately; the clock (unknown line) raised incidentally with its bit never enabled. So regardless of MASK values in unseen rows, no line other than 5 (and the sw bit) is ever BOTH raised AND enabled. Hmm \u2014 unless the clock's line's bit IS enabled by some row's mask... if soft1's MASK1 = 0xfc000000 and the clock's line is, say, line 2 (bit 27): slot 1 is UNCONNECTED on 1 cpu \u2014 with the FIX, slot 1 isn't walked at all \u2192 clock's line never delivers to slot 1. Slot 0's enable (MASK0) \u2014 is MASK0 ever written in session B? If a row wrote MASK0 = 0xfc000000 on 1 cpu and the clock's line were, e.g., bit 27, then the clock would deliver bit 27 to slot 0 \u2192 a \"clock route\" delivery \u2014 the packet DISCLOSES \"the clock/timer route (host-timer driven, not steppable)\" as not-caught \u2014 meaning the probe can't steer it, but if it delivers incidentally, rows might still observe... I can't settle which line the clock uses or session A2/B's mask values from the packet. HONESTY: I cannot settle whether lines other than 5 are incidentally witnessed via the clock. The single fact that would settle it: the clock's interrupt line number (and session A2/B's mask constants). Hmm \u2014 but even IF the clock's line is incidentally witnessed (say line 2), lines 1,3,4,6 remain unwitnessed. Six device lines, one steerable (5), at most one clock line \u2014 at least three lines remain unwitnessed no matter what. So the finding stands: at least 3 of 6 per-line cells are unwitnessed, and the contract claims \"a device line masked out of the walk\" is caught. The finding is robust to the unknowns. GOOD.\n\nLet me also double-check the {slot} dimension: slots witnessed: 0 (B2, A2?), 1 (C, C2, soft1-crash), 2 (mask2 readback \u2014 storage only; delivery to slot 2? NO row delivers to slot 2! Session E delivers to slot 3 only; mask2 readback is a store/readback row, not a delivery row). Hmm! {slot 2} \u00d7 {pending} delivery cell: -n 3 machines? NO session runs -n 3! Sessions: A2 (1?), B (1), B2 (1), C (2), C2 (2), D (2), E (4). Slot 2 delivery on a 3-cpu machine: UNWITNESSED. A mutant that skips slot 2 (e.g., `for (cpu = 0; cpu < d->ncpus; cpu++) { if (cpu == 2) continue; ... }`) \u2014 wait, that's M_SKIP-something. Table: M_SKIP1 (slot 1 skipped) \u2014 caught by C/C2 (cpu1 deliver, both deliver red, 23/25). A hypothetical M_SKIP2 (slot 2 skipped): which row red? Slot 2 delivery happens only on -n 3 \u2014 no session! So M_SKIP2 sweeps 27/27! Is M_SKIP2 in the enumeration? \"{slot} \u00d7 ...\" \u2014 the slot set 0..3 \u2014 YES: \"its slot set 0..3\". A mutant `if (cpu == 2) continue;` in the walk: on 1/2/4-cpu machines, slot 2 is either absent (1,2) or... on 4 cpus (session E), slot 2: MASK2 not written, enable[2]=0 \u2192 else-branch \u2192 nothing observable. So E doesn't witness slot 2 either! So M_SKIP2 sweeps 27/27 and is non-equivalent (a 3-cpu machine's cpu 2 gets no interrupts). CLEAN SWEEP \u2014 in the {slot} dimension! Hmm!! And is it disclosed? The contract's catches: \"the walk reaching an unconnected slot by any route... slots 2/3 aliased onto 0/1 (session E)...\" \u2014 nothing about skipping slot 2 on a 3-cpu machine. NOT disclosed. The probe never runs -n 3! The enumeration's {slot} cells: slot 0 (witnessed: B2 etc.), slot 1 (C/C2), slot 2 (only as absent-slot storage: mask2 readback \u2014 on 1-cpu machines! not as a CONNECTED slot), slot 3 (E). So {slot 2, connected, pending} \u2014 UNWITNESSED. M_SKIP2 = clean sweep. Exact edit: `if (cpu == 2) continue;` at the top of the loop (or `cpu == 2` skipped any way). Predicted 27/27 PASS \u2014 measurement owed. The row that should have caught it: a session F (-n 3): MASK2 + SOFT_INT2 \u2192 cpu 2 enters VBR+8 (E's shape with n=3). That's a REAL gap: the enumeration says \"{slot} \u00d7 ...\" \u2014 slot 2's connected-pending cell has no row.\n\nHmm wait \u2014 actually also check: could slot 2 be witnessed in E via... MASK3+SOFT3 only. cpus 0..2 idle \u2014 slot 2's enable = 0 \u2192 nothing. Right, unwitnessed.\n\nSimilarly slot 3 on -n 4: witnessed \u2713 (E). Slot 1: \u2713 (C, C2). Slot 0: \u2713 (B2, A2?). Slot 2: \u2717. So M_SKIP2 sweeps. ALSO: a bound mutant `d->ncpus = ncpus == 3 ? 2 : ncpus` (clamp 3\u21922): swept (no -n 3 session). Same cell. And `cpu < d->ncpus` \u2192 `cpu < d->ncpus && cpu != 2`. All sweep.\n\nSo I have TWO clean-sweep families in (d): (i) per-device-line masking beyond line 5; (ii) slot-2-on-a-3-cpu-machine (no -n 3 session). Both predicted sweeps; both non-equivalent; both in the stated enumeration; neither disclosed. Also (iii) the deassert cells \u2014 but those ARE disclosed (so not \"surviving adjudication\" as breaches? they're disclosed debt).\n\nWait, but hmm: is (ii) really in the enumeration? \"the dominating predicate `cpu < d->ncpus` (its constant's source, its bound both ways, its slot set 0..3)\" \u2014 \"its slot set 0..3\" \u2014 the predicate's slot set is 0..ncpus-1 \u2286 0..3. A skip-2 mutant keeps the predicate but drops slot 2 from the SET. Yes \u2014 \"its slot set\" is enumerated. And \"{slot} x {pending...}\" joint cells \u2014 slot 2 \u00d7 pending. So yes, in-enumeration.\n\nHmm, and is M_SKIP2 non-equivalent on REACHABLE configurations? Reachable: ncpus 1..4. On -n 3: slot 2 connected, walk skips it \u2192 cpu 2's interrupts lost. -n 3 is reachable (-n is 1..256, DEVINIT allows \u22644). So NON-EQUIVALENT on a reachable config, and the probe has no -n 3 session \u2192 sweep. SOLID.\n\nSimilarly, hmm, let me also check {pending: device line} \u00d7 {slot: 1,2,3} cells: device-line delivery to slots 1..3 \u2014 B2 delivers line 5 to slot 0 only. E delivers SOFT (bit 26 via sw) to slot 3. C/C2: sw to slots 0,1. So a mutant that drops DEVICE-line delivery for slots > 0... e.g., M_SWONLY kills device delivery for ALL slots (caught via B2's slot 0). But a mutant like \"device lines deliver only to slot 0\": status = interrupt_status only for cpu==0, else sw-only: hmm \u2014 status is machine-wide; a mutant `if (cpu != 0) status = 0; if (sw) status |= bit26;` \u2014 then slot 1: status = bit26 (if sw) \u2192 C passes (sw), C2 passes, E passes (slot 3: sw \u2192 bit 26) \u2014 device lines never tested on slots 1..3 \u2192 sweep. Non-equivalent (device line to cpu 1 lost). In-enumeration ({slot} \u00d7 {pending: device line}). Another family. But honestly the slot-2 and per-line findings suffice; the general statement: the {slot} \u00d7 {pending} cross is only sparsely witnessed (slot 0 \u00d7 device \u2713 (B2), slot 0 \u00d7 sw \u2713, slot 1 \u00d7 sw \u2713, slot 3 \u00d7 sw \u2713, slot 2 \u00d7 anything \u2717, slot 1/2/3 \u00d7 device \u2717).\n\nSo Q1's honest answer: YES, clean-sweep mutants exist in (d). The cleanest, most undeniable: **M_SKIP2** (`if (cpu == 2) continue;`) \u2014 no -n 3 session exists; predicted 27/27; owed row: session F (-n 3, MASK2+SOFT_INT2, cpu 2 enters VBR+8). Also per-line masking beyond bit 30 (M_MASK27 etc.) \u2014 predicted sweeps; owed rows per raisable line, else a contract narrowing. And the deassert cells are disclosed (not breaches).\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, now let me reconsider: is the intended answer maybe \"none \u2014 CLOSE\"? The packet is framed as the CLOSING pass, \"SHORT BY DESIGN\", with the contract claiming full coverage of the enumeration... The nine dispositions include three new sessions specifically to close pass-2 escapes. The question \"WHICH IS WRONG?\" (of the nine) suggests the author EXPECTS the panel to find one wrong disposition. And Q1 \"does a clean-sweep exist\" \u2014 the author might expect \"no\" (close) with the deassert cells disclosed... But my job: audit premises as stated, find real defects. The slot-2 gap is REAL from the packet: sessions are -n 1, 2, 4 only (READ: session list), the enumeration includes the slot set 0..3 and {slot} cells (READ), -n 3 is reachable (READ: -n 1..256, DEVINIT allows \u22644), no row delivers to slot 2 (READ: E uses slot 3; mask2 readback is storage-only; C/C2 slots 0/1; B2 slot 0). Therefore M_SKIP2 is a predicted sweep \u2014 REASONED, with the measurement owed. This is exactly what the question asks for (\"give the exact edit and the row that should have caught it (a predicted sweep is a measurement owed -- say so)\").\n\nAnd Q2: which of the nine is wrong? Given my analysis, candidates:\n- The M_AND1 table word (not one of the nine).\n- Hmm, among the nine: is one of the THREE NEW SESSIONS wrong? Session E is the glm/grok answer \u2014 it covers slot 3 and the alias \u2014 but NOT slot 2 (no -n 3). Is session E \"wrong\"? It does what it claims (kills M_AND1, M_MIN2, M_HARD1 partially \u2014 M_HARD1 red includes cpu3 deliver). E is sound for its purpose. The GAP (slot 2) is a MISSING session, not a wrong one. Hmm.\n- The capture move: sound.\n- M_NULLAB equivalence: sound.\n- M_NOREASSERT debt: sound-ish.\n- lunadefaultstop: LOW filing \u2014 sound.\n- lunaabsentmaskdecl: LOW documented-only \u2014 sound.\n- es438: annotate/close \u2014 \"its owed mask2/mask3 pair is discharged by this detector's rows\". Hmm!! WAIT. Let me check this ONE carefully. es438 owed a **mask2/mask3 pair**. The detector's rows: \"a declined store to an absent slot (mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks)\". Hmm \u2014 but WHERE are the mask2/mask3 readback rows? On how many cpus? The mask rows check storage-with-readback for ABSENT slots \u2014 mask2/mask3 are absent on 1- and 2-cpu machines. Session B (1 cpu) would host mask1/2/3 readbacks \u2014 all three absent. So the detector's mask2/mask3 rows exist (per the contract) \u2192 es438's pair discharged. Hmm, seems fine...\n\nUNLESS \u2014 the point is that es438's owed pair was about something else... I don't have es438's origin inlined (pass-1 row). Can't settle. Hmm.\n\nLet me look at lunadefaultstop once more: \"the switch's `default:` graceful stop plus the unimplemented RESET_CPU0..3 / SOFT_INT_FLAG* arms\". Hmm \u2014 RESET_CPU0..3 and SOFT_INT_FLAG* \u2014 these are register ARMS in the switch (device register read/write dispatch). Unimplemented arms \u2192 what do they do? Fall to default? or break? \"the switch's default: graceful stop\" \u2014 the default arm stops gracefully. Unimplemented arms (RESET_CPU0..3, SOFT_INT_FLAG*) \u2014 if they hit the default \u2192 graceful stop \u2192 a guest store to RESET_CPU0 stops the machine?! On REAL hardware, RESET_CPUn resets that cpu. An unimplemented arm that STOPS the emulator on a legitimate guest access is... a behavior bug (the emulator dies/quits on guest IPI-reset). Filed LOW. Is LOW right? Eh \u2014 board-model severity judgment; the panel filed LOW. Can't adjudicate from packet.\n\nHmm, OK \u2014 let me now think about what the AUTHOR planted. This is pass 3 of a repair chain; the packet is elaborate and mostly self-consistent. The question structure: \"If you find one [clean-sweep], give the exact edit and the row that should have caught it...; if none, CLOSE with your own denominator. Second question...: of the nine pass-3 dispositions..., WHICH IS WRONG?\" \u2014 the second question PRESUPPOSES one is wrong (\"WHICH IS WRONG?\" not \"IS ANY WRONG?\"). So the author believes exactly one disposition is wrong. Let me hunt again with that prior.\n\nNine: (1) capture move; (2) B2; (3) C2; (4) E; (5) M_NULLAB-equivalent disclosure; (6) M_NOREASSERT debt disclosure; (7) lunadefaultstop; (8) lunaabsentmaskdecl; (9) es438.\n\nRe-examine (5): M_NULLAB \"EQUIVALENT on every reachable configuration -- measured 22/22 in killtable/; the choice is warrant-based\". Hmm \u2014 wait: \"measured 22/22 in killtable/\" \u2014 but table 2 (killtable2) shows M_NULLAB 27/27 PASS. The contract v3 cites the OLD killtable (22/22) rather than killtable2 (27/27)?? The diff: `+(EQUIVALENT on every reachable configuration -- measured 22/22 in killtable/; the choice is warrant-based),` \u2014 hmm, that's in the v3 docstring, and killtable2's M_NULLAB is 27/27. The docstring cites killtable/ (pass-2's table) \u2014 stale-ish but not wrong (both support equivalence). Meh.\n\nRe-examine (6): M_NOREASSERT \"disclosed debt: the clock's own reassert carries the delivery\". Hmm \u2014 WAIT. Something's off. If the SOFT arm's reassert is deleted, delivery of a software interrupt relies on the CLOCK calling the walk. But WHEN does the clock call it? luna88k_interrupt_assert(clock's line) \u2014 the clock asserts its line periodically (timer tick). Each assert \u2192 walk. So within 15 s, delivery happens. Measured 27/27 PASS. But hold on \u2014 is the clock's assert actually periodic, or ONE-SHOT at init? \"the clock/timer route (host-timer driven...)\" \u2014 periodic. And M_NOREASSERT's 27/27 confirms delivery still occurs (else soft rows would STOP). Fine \u2014 the disclosure is empirically supported by the table. \u2713 sound.\n\nHmm \u2014 re-examine (4) session E's row \"four cpus\": `four = (\"cpu3\" in s.buf[mark:]) or (\"cpu 3\" in s.buf[mark:]); row(\"four cpus\", \"OK\" if four else \"BADREGS\", ...)`. Reads the `machine` listing for cpu3. Fine. But \u2014 hmm \u2014 \"four cpus\" OK on M_NOASSIGN (bound 0): the machine listing still names cpu3 (the machine HAS 4 cpus regardless of d->ncpus) \u2192 OK \u2713 consistent (M_NOASSIGN's reds don't include four cpus \u2713). And on M_MIN2: four cpus OK \u2713 (listing has cpu3). \u2713.\n\nRe-examine (2) B2's row semantics \u2014 \"sio5 deliver\": on M_CALLGUARD (bound the CALLS), red rows: \"sio5, siofirst HALT\" \u2014 sio5 is a SESSION B row (v2) and sio5 deliver is B2's row. Table M_CALLGUARD: \"21/23 | sio5, siofirst HALT\". Hmm \u2014 TWO red rows named \"sio5\" and \"siofirst\" \u2014 where's \"sio5 deliver\"? M_CALLGUARD bounds the reassert CALLS (in store arms?) \u2014 the SIO's luna88k_interrupt_assert caller still calls the walk unbounded \u2192 walk reaches absent slot 1 (if MASK1 armed?) \u2192 HALT. In session B, siofirst: SIO line raised FIRST (before soft1?) on a machine with MASK1 armed? Hmm \u2014 the names: soft1, siofirst, sio5 (session B rows). M_CALLGUARD red: sio5 + siofirst. So session B has a sio5 row (SIO line 5 on... 1 cpu, MASK0 bit 30? \u2014 that's B2's shape \u2014 wait, but B2 is NEW in v3 (deepseek/grok found v2 had NO such row: \"v2 had no such row (deepseek, grok, pass 2)\"). So session B's \"sio5\" row is DIFFERENT from B2's \"sio5 deliver\" row: session B's sio5 = SIO line 5 to an ABSENT slot? (MASK1 bit 30 on 1 cpu \u2192 walk hits slot 1 \u2192 HALT on HEAD \u2713). And \"siofirst\" = SIO raised before the soft store (ordering) \u2192 HALT on HEAD \u2713. OK \u2014 session B (1 cpu) rows: soft1 (MASK1+SOFT1 \u2192 crash), siofirst (SIO before soft \u2192 crash), sio5 (MASK1 bit 30 + SIO line 5 \u2192 crash), slot0 deliver?, mask1/2/3 readbacks, survive... These are the PRE_FIX_SYMPTOM rows: HEAD scores 20/22 with soft1+siofirst HALT. Hmm \u2014 but if session B ALSO has a sio5 row that crashes HEAD, why isn't sio5 red on HEAD? HEAD: 20/22, red = soft1, siofirst only. So on HEAD, sio5 did NOT crash?? Hmm \u2014 maybe sio5 is a row that ARMS line 5 without enabling its bit in any absent slot's mask (e.g., MASK0 bit 30 on 1 cpu \u2192 slot 0 connected \u2192 delivers OK on HEAD; walk continues 1..3: enable 0 \u2192 no crash \u2713). Then why is sio5 red on M_CALLGUARD?? M_CALLGUARD: \"the pass-1 F4 alternative: bound the CALLS\" \u2014 the fix alternative: instead of bounding the walk, bound the reassert CALLS in the store arms (only call for cpu < n?) \u2014 hmm, but the walk itself still walks MAX_CPUS when called from the DEVICE callers \u2192 crash when a device line has an enabled bit in an absent slot's mask. sio5 red on M_CALLGUARD: sio5's session-B shape must arm an absent slot's mask with bit 30 AND raise line 5 \u2192 walk (from the SIO caller) \u2192 slot 1 assert \u2192 NULL \u2192 HALT. But then sio5 would ALSO crash on HEAD! Contradiction? HEAD: sio5 OK (not red). Hmm!\n\nUnless sio5 (session B) arms MASK0 bit 30 (slot 0, connected) \u2014 on HEAD: walk 0..3, slot 0 delivers \u2713, slots 1..3: enable 0 \u2192 fine \u2192 OK. On M_CALLGUARD: the CALLS are bounded \u2014 which calls? \"bound the reassert CALL bounded in the store arms instead of the walk (sio5)\" (from the contract's catch list \u2014 session B2 catches this!). Wait \u2014 the contract v3: \"the reassert CALL bounded in the store arms instead of the walk (sio5)\" \u2014 caught by session B2's sio5 deliver row! Hmm \u2014 so M_CALLGUARD is killed by B2's sio5 deliver (device caller \u2192 walk unbounded... no wait). Hmm, let me re-think M_CALLGUARD: alternative fix = bound the reassert CALLS (in the four callers? or the store arms?) instead of bounding the walk. If the calls are guarded (`if (cpu < ncpus) reassert()` \u2014 no, reassert isn't per-cpu...). Hmm \u2014 \"the reassert CALL bounded in the store arms\" \u2014 the store arms call reassert_interrupts(); the alternative: make the store arms NOT call it (or call a bounded variant) \u2014 but the WALK itself still walks MAX_CPUS when the DEVICE callers (luna88k_interrupt_assert/deassert) invoke it \u2192 any device line with an armed absent-slot mask \u2192 crash. B2: 1 cpu, MASK0 bit 30, SIO line 5 raised \u2192 luna88k_interrupt_assert \u2192 walk \u2192 slot 0: delivers \u2713 (so sio5 deliver row: does it crash or pass on M_CALLGUARD?). Table M_CALLGUARD red rows: \"sio5, siofirst HALT\" \u2014 hmm, \"sio5\" not \"sio5 deliver\". And the contract says the call-bounded alternative is caught by (sio5) \u2014 B2's row name is \"sio5 deliver\"! The table's M_CALLGUARD row says red: \"sio5, siofirst\" \u2014 if \"sio5\" here means B2's \"sio5 deliver\" row, the row name is abbreviated. But wait \u2014 B2's sio5 deliver row: on M_CALLGUARD, would it HALT or STOP? M_CALLGUARD (bound the calls): the walk is UNBOUNDED (MAX_CPUS) whenever called; the store arms don't call it (or call it bounded \u2014 the walk itself is still unbounded!). B2: MASK0 bit 30 store (store arm \u2014 call bounded/absent \u2192 no crash), SIO WR1 TX_IE \u2192 sio_tx_pending \u2192 luna88k_interrupt_assert(line 5) \u2192 walk (UNBOUNDED) \u2192 slot 0: enable bit 30 & status bit 30 \u2192 assert \u2713 (cpu 0 enters VBR+8 \u2192 breakpoint hit \u2192 row OK?) \u2014 then walk CONTINUES: slot 1: status bit 30 & enable[1]=0 \u2192 0 \u2192 else \u2192 latch[1]=0 \u2192 no call \u2192 no crash. Slots 2,3 same. So B2 on M_CALLGUARD: sio5 deliver OK?! But the table says sio5 HALT on M_CALLGUARD. CONTRADICTION?\n\nHmm wait \u2014 maybe M_CALLGUARD's shape is different: \"bound the CALLS\" = the four callers' calls to reassert_interrupts are guarded by `cpu < ncpus`?? But reassert isn't per-cpu... Or maybe: the alternative fix = guard INTERRUPT_ASSERT/DEASSERT calls inside the walk with `if (cpu < d->ncpus)` \u2014 i.e., move the bound to the CALLS INSIDE the walk body (walk still iterates MAX_CPUS but only calls assert/deassert for connected slots): that's... M_GUARDASSERT (\"bound on the ASSERT call only\") \u2014 21/22, soft1 HALT. And \"M_CALLGUARD (the pass-1 F4 alternative: bound the CALLS)\" \u2014 bound the calls TO reassert_interrupts at the CALL SITES (the four callers): the store arms and the device callers get `if (something) reassert()`. Hmm \u2014 if ALL FOUR callers' calls are bounded... how? The calls aren't per-slot. Maybe: `luna88k_interrupt_assert` checks `if (line's slot...)`. I think M_CALLGUARD = at each of the four call sites, wrap with a guard that only calls when ncpus \u2265 2 (or per the store's cpunr < ncpus?). For the store arms: `if (cpunr < ncpus) reassert()` \u2014 hmm, but the walk walks ALL slots when called from slot 0's store... The pass-2 grok note: \"the SIO reaches the walk through the third AND fourth callers\" \u2014 the SIO's assert/deassert call the walk. So even if the STORE arms' calls are guarded, the SIO callers still call the walk \u2192 walk (unbounded, MAX_CPUS) \u2192 if any absent slot has enable&status \u2260 0 \u2192 crash. Which row arms an absent slot's mask AND has a device line pending? THAT must be session B's \"sio5\" row: MASK1 (absent slot 1) bit 30 + SIO line 5 raised \u2192 SIO caller \u2192 walk \u2192 slot 1: bit30 & enable \u2192 assert NULL \u2192 HALT. And on HEAD, sio5 \u2192 HALT TOO! But HEAD's reds are only soft1, siofirst (20/22). So sio5 would be red on HEAD \u2014