u < d->ncpus` \u2014 that's M_GUARDASSERT-ish (bound on the ASSERT call only \u2192 soft1 HALT \u2014 wait, M_GUARDASSERT: soft1 HALT on a 1-cpu machine: MASK1+SOFT1: walk: slot 1: status&enable \u2260 0, ASSERT call guarded (cpu < 1 \u2192 skip) \u2192 no crash from ASSERT... but then the LATCH: `d->irqActive[1] = 1` still set; NEXT walk (clock): status&enable \u2260 0 \u2192 skip ASSERT again... no crash?! But table says M_GUARDASSERT soft1 HALT. Hmm \u2014 unless the guard is only on the ASSERT call and NOT the DEASSERT: slot 1: first walk: assert skipped, latch = 1. Then SOFT_INT1 CLEAR? soft1 row: store SOFT_INT1 then...? The soft1 row on M_GUARDASSERT HALTs \u2014 maybe the row CLEARS the soft int later \u2192 walk: slot 1: status&enable = 0 now \u2192 else \u2192 `if (irqActive[1]) INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(cpu_irq[1])` \u2014 UNGUARDED deassert \u2192 NULL \u2192 CRASH. THAT's why ASSERT-only guard crashes at soft1! And \"M_NULLA (ASSERT-only NULL guard) | 21/22 | soft1 HALT\" \u2014 hmm, M_NULLA = ASSERT-side NULL guard: `if (d->cpu_irq[cpu].interrupt_assert) ASSERT` \u2014 a NULL CHECK on the assert pointer only: slot 1: assert skipped (NULL), latch set... then deassert later (clear) \u2192 unguarded \u2192 NULL deassert \u2192 crash at soft1 \u2713 consistent (\"sio5clr did not kill M_NULLA (the host had already died at soft1)\" \u2014 pass-2 note \u2713).\n\nOK so M_GUARDASSERT = bound on the ASSERT call only \u2192 soft1 HALT via the deassert path \u2713 consistent.\n\nM_CALLGUARD = bound at the CALL SITES (the four callers): the store arms' calls bounded/removed \u2192 the SIO callers (3rd and 4th) still call the walk \u2192 hmm, but then B2 on M_CALLGUARD: SIO line 5 \u2192 walk \u2192 slot 0 delivers \u2192 OK, not HALT. STILL contradicts. UNLESS the bound at the call sites is per-slot somehow... OR M_CALLGUARD's shape: `reassert_interrupts` calls REPLACED by a bounded variant at the STORE arms, and the DEVICE callers' calls REMOVED entirely?? Then B2: SIO line 5 \u2192 no walk call \u2192 line 5 never delivered \u2192 sio5 deliver STOP (not HALT). Table says HALT. Hmm.\n\nOR \u2014 M_CALLGUARD: the calls bounded = `if (ncpus > 1) reassert()` \u2014 on 1-cpu machines NO calls at all?? Then B2: no delivery \u2192 STOP. Not HALT.\n\nI can't reconstruct M_CALLGUARD's exact shape from the packet \u2014 its red rows (sio5, siofirst HALT) are just READ to me. Is there an internal inconsistency? On HEAD, siofirst HALTs; on M_CALLGUARD, siofirst HALTs too \u2713 plausible (both leave the walk unbounded via the SIO caller with an armed absent mask \u2014 so siofirst DOES arm an absent slot + raise a line! But then HEAD's siofirst HALT \u2713 \u2014 and HEAD's sio5 (session B) \u2014 hmm, wait \u2014 if siofirst arms an absent slot's mask and raises the SIO, then session B's sio5 row might be a DIFFERENT shape (MASK0 bit 30 \u2192 connected slot \u2014 passes HEAD \u2713). OK so session B has BOTH: siofirst (absent-slot armed + SIO \u2192 crash on HEAD) and sio5 (slot 0 armed + SIO \u2192 OK on HEAD, the \"sio5\" row maybe = \"the SIO reaches the walk through the 3rd/4th callers\" witness: delivery to slot 0 via device line \u2713 \u2014 wait, but that's what B2's sio5 deliver does! And pass-2 said \"v2 had no such row (deepseek, grok, pass 2)\" \u2014 v2 had no row proving a DEVICE line delivers to a CONNECTED slot. So session B's sio5 row is NOT a device-line-delivery row.", "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 360990065066}
