regress\artifacts\pcc2defaultkill\pass4_seats\kimi.txt:1287:    2. `wide wr val` (write value len 4) — mirror: read value len 4 = `wide rd srvd` counts "(len 4) -- served 0x00" — the VALUE there is 0x00 constant (unreachable by guest write) — so the read-arm wide value channel is rowed in NAME but its channel cannot vary (that's exactly what M_LASTBYTE exploited... no wait, M_LASTBYTE was data[len-1] on the read announce; `wide rd srvd` reads four ZERO bytes so a byte-selection mutant can't be seen there. That hole was closed by `srvd half text` at len 2. But for the wide READ value channel: is there a row serving a nonzero wide read? No! The only wide read rows are `wide rd` (val 0x0) and `wide rd srvd` ("(len 4) -- served 0x00"). A mutant printing `data[1]`, `data[2]`, or `data[3]`... would still say 0x00. Or a read-announce mutant printing `data[0]` where the wide read's first byte is 0 — can't vary. Hmm — but the mirror question: the wide-read VALUE channel rowed with all-zero bytes = channel cannot vary = NEITHER effectively? The brief discloses end-span; but a nonzero wide read is NOT rowed. Actually wait — can a guest put a nonzero byte at 0x30..0x33? PRTICR block is uncased and writes are declined, so array stays 0. A nonzero wide read would need a cased word arm at an uncased... no. Actually the ld.w must be word-aligned; wide reads at uncased offsets: candidates 0x28 (IEERR — uncased), 0x30 (PRTICR — uncased)... any uncased 4-aligned offset whose interior could hold nonzero via a cased arm? The cased arms are at 0x00 CHIPID (ro), 0x02 GENCTL, 0x04 T1CMP, 0x08 T1COUNT?, 0x0c T2CMP, 0x10 T2COUNT, 0x1c SCCERR... A wide read at 0x04 is cased (whole word). What about a wide read at 0x14? 0x14 is in NAMED18 (0x14 = PSCALECNT presumably), uncased — bytes 0x14..0x17. 0x15,0x16,0x17 — are those cased? INWORD = 05,06,07,09,0a,0b,0d,0e,0f,11,12,13 — so 0x15,0x16,0x17 are not in-word; they're in NAMED18? NAMED18 has 0x14,0x18,0x19,... so 0x15,0x16,0x17 must be... wait the union of NAMED18 ∪ INWORD ∪ UNNAMED = 43 uncased offsets. 0x15: not in NAMED18 (14,18,19,23,25,28,2a,2b,2c,30,31,32,33,34,36,37,38,3a), not in INWORD, must be in UNNAMED? UNNAMED = 20,21,22,24,26,29,2d,2e,35,39,3b,3c,3d. 0x15 not there! Hmm, so 0x15 must be cased? The four counter/compare words: T1CMP 0x04-0x07, T1COUNT 0x08-0x0b, T2CMP 0x0c-0x0f, T2COUNT 0x10-0x13. So 0x15,0x16,0x17 are part of... 0x14 = PSCALECNT? Hmm, if PSCALECNT is a 32-bit word at 0x14-0x17 and it's uncased... whatever. The point: wide-read value channel is all-zero in the rows. A mutant on the read announce printing data[len-1] at len 4 passes `wide rd srvd` (0x00 either way) — but M_LASTBYTE at len 4 is caught by... `srvd half text` is len 2. So `data[len-1]` red only via srvd half text. Fine, the mutant class is rowed at len 2. The mirror of `wide wr val` (which separates idata from data[0] at len 4 on the write arm) on the read arm: a len-4 read whose bytes differ AND are pinned — NOT rowed (wide rd srvd's bytes are all zero; the channel can't vary). Is that NEITHER or disclosed? The contract discloses... let me check: the DISCLOSED paragraph mentions end-spanning wide read, M_RELOAD, len 8, single-offset scoping, per-instance, debug()-for-fatal(). It does NOT disclose "the len-4 read value channel is pinned only at an all-zero offset". Hmm, wait — but actually is there a mutant that this leaves live? A clause-(d) mutant on the read announce value at len 4: e.g., print data[3] instead of data[0] — passes (0 either way at PRTICR). But that same mutant at len 1: srvd val text would print data[0]=0x34... a mutant that prints data[3] ALWAYS would break srvd val text (data[3] of a len-1 read — the memcpy only filled 1 byte; data buffer pre-zeroed per #95 → prints 0x00 ≠ 0x34 → red). A mutant that prints data[len-1]: len 1 → data[0] same, len 2 → caught by srvd half text, len 4 → invisible. So `data[len-1]` is caught. A mutant conditioned `if (len == 4) print data[3] else data[0]`? That's a len-4-conditioned value mutant — passes 46 rows? It changes only the len-4 read announce value: wide rd srvd reads 0x00 regardless (data[3]=0). So it passes! But is that "clause (d)"? Clause (d) seems to be "mutants of the new arm's logic" (the repair diff). A len-conditioned value swap is a weird mutant. The floor question asks: does a clause-(d) mutant exist that sweeps 46 rows clean? Candidate: a value-channel mutant conditioned on len 4 on the read arm — since no len-4 read serves a nonzero byte. Hmm, but is that in the same class as M_LASTBYTE (byte-selection)? M_LASTBYTE (data[len-1]) is killed at len 2. A `data[2]`-for-len-4-only mutant is narrower — it's a per-width scoping, akin to the disclosed "single-offset scoping" (#392 shape). The contract discloses per-offset scoping but NOT per-width scoping of the value channel... Actually wait: it does sort of — "the disposition is value-pinned at ONE offset per class (IEICR/IEERR, 0x06, 0x21, plus 0x28 at len 4 and 0x06 at len 2)". Value-pinned at 0x28 at len 4 on the WRITE arm (wide wr val). On the READ arm at len 4: value-pinned at PRTICR but with all-zero bytes — the pin can't distinguish byte selection. Hmm, so is the len-4 read value channel "rowed"? `wide rd srvd` pins the text "(len 4) -- served 0x00" — a constant-value mutant (print 0) passes that row, but is caught by srvd val text / srvd half text at other widths. A wrong-byte mutant at len 4 only: not caught anywhere. So per the mirror question, `wide wr val`'s mirror cell (read-arm wide value with a varying channel) is... arguably NEITHER — the len-4 read rows exist but their value channel cannot vary, which is exactly the failure mode the pass-3 M_LASTBYTE repair identified and fixed at len 2 but the len-4 read cell remains unvaried. Hmm wait, actually, can they make it vary? Is there any 4-aligned uncased offset whose bytes a guest can make nonzero? Writes are declined everywhere uncased. The only way bytes get nonzero is via a cased word arm — but a ld.w at a cased word offset hits the cased arm itself. A wide read spanning cased+uncased? ld.w at 0x02? Not word-aligned (0x02 is 2-aligned only). ld.w at 0x06? Not word-aligned (0x06 mod 4 = 2). m88k enforces alignment for ld.w? Yes, misaligned access would trap. So no nonzero wide read is possible at an uncased offset. The channel CANNOT vary at len 4 reads, period. So the mirror cell is structurally unrowable — like M_RELOAD. But is it DISCLOSED? The contract's "served value at len 1 and len 2" and "widths 1, 2, 4 in both directions" claims width 4 both directions rowed. The value channel at len-4 read is text-pinned only at zero. This is a "NEITHER" finding for the mirror of wide wr val — unless one argues the zero pin still catches constant/wrong-byte mutants via other rows. Hmm. Actually wait — let me re-check `wide rd srvd`: "0x30 (len 4) -- served 0x00"? The row counts "(len 4) -- served 0x00". A mutant `data[0] -> data[1]` prints 0x00 too (all zero). So yes, byte-selection at len-4-read-only passes. But hold on — is that a clause-(d) mutant? Clause (d) = mutants of the announce/latch logic in the arm. A len-4-conditioned byte-selection on the read announce is a plausible clause-(d) mutant of the value channel. It's the SAME class as M_LASTBYTE, scoped to len 4. The repair closed M_LASTBYTE with a len-2 row but left the len-4 cell unvaried — because it cannot vary (no nonzero wide-readable bytes exist). So the right disposition is DISCLOSED-as-unrowable (like M_RELOAD), but the contract doesn't disclose it — it claims "widths 1, 2 and 4 in both directions" are closed cells. Hmm, "srvd half text" gives len-2 read value 0x56; wide rd srvd gives len-4 read value 0x00 (non-varying). So the claim "served value at len 1 and len 2" is precise — and the "widths 1,2,4 in both directions" claim is about the width LABEL and fate, which is true (wide rd srvd pins the width label and first byte). A byte-selection mutant at len 4 only: print data[3]: announce "served 0x00" — passes. So a clause-(d) mutant sweeping 46/46 exists in derivation: `if (len == 4) fatal(... data[3]) else fatal(... data[0])`. Wait, but does it really sweep? Rows touching len-4 read announce: wide rd srvd only. data[3] at PRTICR = 0 → "served 0x00" ✓ green. All other rows unaffected. So YES — 46/46. Is this within the disclosed exclusions? The exclusions list: M_RELOAD, len 8, end-span reads, single-offset scoping, per-instance latch scope, debug()-for-fatal(). "Byte selection within a wide read at len 4" is NOT in the exclusions. The contract says "the announce names the FIRST served byte of a wide read, labelled with the width" — that was in the OLD contract disclosure (the 36-row one: "the announce names the FIRST served byte of a wide read"). In the new contract that sentence seems gone... In the new DISCLOSED paragraph I see: "an END-SPANNING wide read ... no row drives it; ...". The old 36-row disclosure had "the announce names the FIRST served byte of a wide read, labelled with the width" — the new one doesn't include that sentence but has "served value at len 1 and len 2" in the denominator... hmm, actually the table-4 denominator says "served value at len 1 and len 2" — implicitly excluding len 4? No wait, it says "the announce's five channels (fate, count, direction, width, value) on BOTH arms" are closed. Value channel on the read arm at len 4 is NOT closed (can't vary). So the denominator's "five channels on BOTH arms" overstates: value on read arm is closed at len 1,2 and text-pinned-but-unvariable at len 4.
regress\artifacts\pcc2defaultkill\pass4_seats\kimi.txt:1414:    Hmm, actually wait. For F2, is a len-4-scoped byte-selection mutant "clause (d)"? Clause (d) appears to be "the announce/latch logic in the arm" mutants. `data[0] -> data[len-1]` was clause-(d)-shaped (M_LASTBYTE). Conditioning on len==4 is a scoping — like M_BACKEDLOW scoped member 6 by offset range, or the disclosed single-offset scoping. The round's own standard: M_LATCHOFF conditioned the latch on offset CLASS and it was treated as a legit breach. Conditioning a value mutant on width CLASS is the same move on the width axis. The contract discloses per-OFFSET scoping but not per-WIDTH scoping of the value channel. So F2 is a legit NEITHER finding too. Though — admittedly — the value at len 4 CANNOT be nonzero, so the only thing the mutant corrupts is which zero it prints. It prints "served 0x00" either way → text unchanged?? WAIT. If all four bytes are 0, then data[0]=data[3]=0 → the announce is IDENTICAL. The mutant isn't even observable in principle. So F2 is vacuous: no observable difference exists, so there's nothing to row. It's exactly the M_RELOAD situation (two operands that cannot differ under any reachable state) → should be DISCLOSED as unrowable-by-construction, but arguably the channel doesn't exist at all. Hmm — so F2 weakens: if data[k] are all equal, "printing data[3] instead of data[0]" is not a distinguishable mutation — like M_RELOAD where reload==data[0] except in a race. For M_RELOAD there EXISTS a reachable state (running counter) where they differ, hence the race disclosure. For len-4 read byte-selection: is there ANY reachable state where bytes differ? Wide read at uncased 4-aligned offset: all such offsets are uncased-only blocks where every byte is 0 (writes declined, no cased arm spans them, no tick writes them — tick writes T1COUNT/T2COUNT at 0x08-0x13 which are cased at word level... wait, 4-aligned offsets 0x08, 0x0c, 0x10 are CASED word arms (T1COUNT 0x08? T2CMP 0x0c, T2COUNT 0x10) — ld.w there hits the case, not default. So under any reachable state, len-4 default reads serve 0x00000000 with all bytes equal. The mutant is unobservable BY CONSTRUCTION — not even a race. So there's no cell to close; the channel literally doesn't exist at len 4. But then the contract's claim "the announce's five channels on BOTH arms" closed is harmless here because there's nothing to observe. I think F2 demotes to a wording note, not a breach. The brief's own M_RELOAD disclosure logic covers "indistinguishable operands" — and a byte-selection at len 4 is indistinguishable always. OK — F2 = not a finding (or a minor note). Good thing I thought it through.
regress\artifacts\pcc2defaultkill\pass4_seats\kimi.txt:1476:    Hmm OK wait, I want to be careful about materiality. Does this matter for the DEFECT (host kill)? The detector's job is to catch re-kills and disposition changes. A len-2-write-announce-text mutant doesn't reintroduce the host kill, doesn't change disposition — it's announce cosmetics. But the pass-3 standard was exactly this strict: M_WRVAL0 (announce value cosmetics on the write arm) was treated as a breach worth closing, because the announce is the observable contract and because the class closure principle says close consecutive cells of a class in one diff. The (write, len 2) announce cell is a consecutive cell of the SAME class pass 3 closed (write-announce value; width label). By the round's own floor standard, it's a breach-shaped hole. The right verdict: GO-WITH-CHANGES (add the one row; also reword the denominator/disclosure to scope the width parity claim). Given the panel's own history (passes that found one more cell → repair in-round), NO-GO would also be defensible under "the floor question asked of the composite: does a clause-(d) mutant exist that sweeps clean? YES → not close". Hmm. The brief says Q6: "If you say close, say what the denominator excludes; if you say not close, name the cell and the edit." I name cell and edit → that's "not close" on the floor question → but the verdict options are GO/NO-GO/GO-WITH-CHANGES for the repair/brief, not exactly close/not-close. The repair diff itself (product: two comment sentences; detector: ten rows) is sound as far as it goes; the composite detector still has one unrowed, undisclosed announce cell reachable by a two-token clause-(d) edit. Under this round's own floor rule, that's a "do not ship the zero as final" — i.e., NO-GO on closure, but the fix is one appended row. Previous passes in this campaign treated "found a sweep" as NOT CLOSE (pass 2, pass 3) and repaired in-round. Since I'm a reviewer, not the operator, the verdict that maps