regress\artifacts\pcc2defaultkill\pass3_seats\ollama_glm.burn.txt:1:{"model": "glm-5.3", "created_at": "2026-09-05T13:23:20.894229367Z", "response": "", "thinking": "Let me work through this carefully. I'm a \"packet-fed seat\" \u2014 I cannot read files, run commands, or open a repository. I must reason from the inlined text alone. I need to:\n\n- VERDICT LINE FIRST\n- Label each claim READ (from the packet) or REASONED\n- Never label anything MEASURED\n- Audit premises as stated; find self-contradictions or derivations that don't follow\n- Name premises that can't be settled from the packet and say what single fact would settle them\n- Not pad\n\nThe brief is about #501 pcc2defaultkill, PASS 3, reviewing a repair diff. Three questions:\n\n1. Is the repair right \u2014 is `data[0]` always the byte the guest received (memcpy path and end-span zero-fill path), and which accepted disposition above is wrong, by number?\n\n2. From table 3 and its predictions: does any mutant's result contradict its prediction; is any of the six pass-2 mutants NOT 23/23 on the pass-2 instrument; is any cell of the closure still open \u2014 name the edit?\n\n3. Should #501 close and commit? Carry the denominator: which mutant shapes, which cells, what is excluded.\n\nLet me carefully analyze.\n\n**The repair diff analysis:**\n\nThe diff changes the default: arm of dev_pcc2.c:\n\n- Comment changes (three sentences: \"no guest reaches\" -> \"no inspected OpenBSD driver path reaches\"; \"the array's default, not a computed value\" -> \"the array's current byte... the tick's value for a counter interior\"; \"four arms up\" -> \"above\").\n\n- Write announce: `0x%x: 0x%x -- declined. (once)` -> `0x%x (len %d): 0x%x -- declined. (once)` with `(int)len` added.\n\n- Read announce: was `0x%x -- served 0x%02x. (once)` printing `d->pcctwo_reg[relative_addr]`; now prints `0x%x (len %d) -- served 0x%02x. (once)` with `data[0]`.\n\nThe premise says: \"`data` was filled by the memcpy above the switch (or zero-filled on the #119 end-span path, where `data[0]` is then truthfully 0x00).\"\n\nQuestion 1 asks: is `data[0]` always the byte the guest received (memcpy path and end-span zero-fill path)?\n\nKey question: is `data[0]` the byte served for a WRITE? For a write, no byte is \"served\" to the guest \u2014 the guest sends a byte. But the read announce only prints on reads. The write announce prints `idata`.\n\nWait, actually \u2014 the memcpy above the switch. The typical GXemul device access pattern: the device's access function receives `data` pointer. For writes, data contains what the guest wrote; for reads, the emulator reads from the device's array into data and then serves it to the guest. Hmm, actually in GXemul, `dev_pcc2` style access functions typically have a signature like:\n\n```c\nvoid dev_pcc2_access(struct cpu *cpu, struct memory *mem, uint64_t relative_addr, int writeflag, ...)\n{\n    uint8_t data[4] = ...;\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but actually in GXemul (the real gxemul), device access functions often do:\n\n```c\nsize_t rs = ...;\nif (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) memcpy(..., data, rs);\nelse { memcpy(data, ..., rs); }\n```\n\nThe packet says: \"READ keeps the byte the memcpy served\" \u2014 so the memcpy serves reads from `d->pcctwo_reg` into `data` before the switch. So on a read, `data[0]` is the byte from the array \u2014 which is the byte the guest receives. On the end-span zero-fill path (#119), data is zero-filled and data[0] is 0x00, which is the byte the guest receives (truthfully).\n\nNow, is `data[0]` always the byte the guest received on a read?\n\nSub-issues:\n\n1. **Endianness / wide reads.** If the read is `len > 1` (e.g., len 4), the memcpy would copy `len` bytes from the array into `data`. Then `data[0]` is only the first byte. For a little-endian guest, a 4-byte read at offset X returns bytes X..X+3, and `data[0]` is byte X \u2014 the low byte. The comment in the diff says \"a wide read is labelled\" via `(len %d)`. So the announce