latch bit is set BEFORE the announce (look at the diff: `d->warned_unimpl |= 1ULL << relative_addr;` then the announce). So the first access announces, subsequent accesses don't. The `sameoff val` row tests the VALUE channel on the latched path: same offset, first access (announce), then a read-back that reads the array... wait, let me re-read.\n\n\"`sameoff val` (wants 0x0, reads 0x5a)\" \u2014 for mutant M_BACKED2 (member 6 on the LATCHED path, i.e., the write actually stores idata to the array on the latched path?). Hmm, M_O2 is \"member 6 on the LATCHED path\" \u2014 i.e., the mutant makes the write a backed store (stores to the array) instead of declining, on the path after the latch. Then `sameoff val` does: write at offset (announce fires, latch set), then read back at the same offset \u2014 the read sees the stored value 0x5a instead of 0x0 (the array default/current). So the row detects a backed store.\n\n3. **Is data[0] correct on the memcpy path?** The question is whether the memcpy happens BEFORE the switch for reads. Premise 2 says \"`data` was filled by the memcpy above the switch\". READ: \"data was filled by the memcpy above the switch (or zero-filled on the #119 end-span path...)\". So on a normal read, data[0] = the byte from the array at the time of the memcpy \u2014 the byte served. On the end-span path, data[0] = 0x00, and that's what the guest receives. So yes, data[0] is the byte served, per the packet's own premises.\n\nBut wait \u2014 can I verify from the diff that the memcpy is above the switch? No \u2014 the diff only shows the arm. The premise asserts it. I should flag: \"the memcpy above the switch fills `data` before the arm\" is READ (asserted in premises 1/2) but not verifiable from the packet's diff; the single fact that would settle it is the lines above the switch in dev_pcc2.c (the memcpy and the end-span zero-fill). I cannot check it. The packet explicitly says premises are \"each a QUESTION if you can check it\" \u2014 I can't,