regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:294:  Now Q2 (smallest re-opening edit that passes the obvious detector): If fix = memset(buf,0,sizeof buf) + detector "bytes are deterministic/known", then re-opening edits: change sizeof(buf)→bufdepth (PATH B reopens silently because bytes beyond bufdepth stale again — but wait, detector that checks "destination deterministic across runs" might still pass since stack is deterministic-ish per run... the vacuity concern). Or: move memset AFTER the PUT switch but before memory_rw (memset still present, now it erases the colour arms' fills — changes behaviour, detector might catch wrong colours... actually that breaks correct modes too, so detector would catch). The sharpest answer: `memset(buf, 0, bufdepth)` keeps a memset, correct size for PATH A, and reopens PATH B — and a detector built only around PATH A (destination bytes deterministic) stays green. That's exactly the brief's own option 2, and the brief is right to flag it. Another: `uint8_t buf[8] = {0};` at declaration — wait that fixes both. `memset(buf, 0, 4)` — constant size covering the combine width but not the depth-8 write: PATH A at bufdepth 8 reopens (buf[4..7] stale), PATH B stays fixed (combine reads ≤ 4). A "bytes not stale" detector that only exercises depth-4 PUT + depth-1 GET stays green. So the smallest re-open is `memset(buf, 0, 4)` or `memset(buf, 0, bufdepth)` depending on which path the detector watches. The row expected to stay green: whichever detector row only covers one path. The detector therefore must witness BOTH paths (0x1320 PUT and 0x1020 GET) — that's the design consequence. Good.
regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:302:  Q6 (one round or two): sgirandomcolour — PUT default arm fills buf[0..3] with random() then fatal (no exit! fatal doesn't exit — check: fatal() in this fork... in upstream GXemul fatal() prints only. Let me confirm fatal semantics: misc.h — fatal() is a macro printing and NOT exiting; exit is explicit. Yes: line 308-313, default: fills random, then fatal(...) with NO exit, falls to the memory_rw write. So YCRCB PUT writes 4 random bytes + 4 uninit bytes at depth 8). sgirandomcolour's defect: nondeterminism from random(). Shared oracle "destination deterministic across runs": for sgirebufuninit PATH A, the uninit bytes are typically deterministic across runs (same stack history) — so a determinism oracle may NOT catch PATH A at all! That's a sharp point AGAINST merging: uninit-stack reuse is usually deterministic run-to-run (same execution path → same stale bytes), whereas random() varies. So the proposed shared oracle (determinism across runs) is WEAK for sgirebufuninit and is exactly the "passes by luck"/vacuity shape the brief warns about. So the letter is right: separate rounds; sgirandomcolour needs its own oracle (determinism across runs suits random() fine, but not uninit). Actually wait — determinism across runs suits sgirandomcolour (random varies → nondeterministic → caught). For sgirebufuninit, determinism across runs does NOT suit it. The brief says "a single oracle — the destination is DETERMINISTIC across runs — plausibly covers both". That's wrong for PATH A/B in the typical case (stack reuse is deterministic); it only catches the leak if stale bytes vary run-to-run (ASLR doesn't change stack CONTENT reuse; content is prior frames' data — deterministic for deterministic emulation). So the shared oracle is weaker than a purpose-built known-stale-content oracle for sgirebufuninit. Recommend: keep separate (the letter is right), OR if merged, the detector must use the known-content oracle, not determinism. Also note: fixing PATH A/B with memset makes the output deterministic AND zero — and a determinism oracle passes trivially post-fix but doesn't pin the fix vs. luck. Right.
regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:779:  Also note the probe comment documents an important precedent: the oracle-discipline (height load-bearing, vacuity-by-construction measurement) — consistent with this brief's concerns.
regress\artifacts\sgirebufuninit\pass1_panel\kimi.txt:918:  **Keep the letter: separate.** The proposed shared oracle — "destination deterministic across runs" — is **vacuous for sgirebufuninit and only toothed for sgirandomcolour, proven from the tree**: `srandom(tv_sec ^ getpid() ^ tv_usec)` runs at startup (`src/core/main.c:688-691`), so `random()` varies per run, but stale-stack reuse under a fixed guest program is run-*deterministic* — the buggy build passes a determinism oracle. One shared oracle that is weak for one member is the vacuity floor's problem, as the brief itself says. `sgirandomcolour` also has no witness and the `fatal()` at :313 falls through to the write (confirmed — `fatal` does not exit), so it is reachable but unwitnessed. Separate rounds; if ever merged, the detector needs the value-oracle anyway, which is two oracles in everything but name.
