regress\artifacts\pass2_7a41b3f\seats\grok.txt:124:**Negate-True vacuity does not change what a live HEAD score means.** An inequality greens on `"absent"` / `"died"` / `"0x0"`. The equality siblings (`rgb chan`, `kill width`, `swpctx ptbr`, `walk off`) red on those same states, so the **probe** still fails and the detector does not escape. A HEAD run that actually printed `0xbbccdd00` / `IOC_CONF: 0x0` / `0xbbbb2222` / `VAL_A` was a real observation of the defect. What those rows’ notes overclaimed was that they were themselves controls. The later positive restatements (`kill notzero`, `swpctx notflags`, `walk stored` / `walk mapped`) are L4 in the witnesses, and they are already in the working tree.
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:291:  But wait — the packet says the oracle was re-pinned first, committed build measured RED (7/8, preserved as prefix_repinned_RED.out), then code changed. And the defect disclosure says the vacuity was found in "the first table" and repaired. So there might have been an earlier killtable with 1x1 where M_NOLATCH escaped. The current killtable dir has the repaired probe. The note in the current probe (lines 189-196) documents exactly this repair. OK.
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:293:  So the timeline in the packet is consistent: prefix_repinned_RED.out is with the repinned (post-first-table?) probe... Hmm, actually wait. prefix_repinned_RED.out has the note "LATCH: exactly one announcement, not one per pixel" while the killtable BASE.out has "LATCH: one announcement for FOUR pixels, not one each". So prefix_repinned_RED.out was run with an intermediate probe (repinned oracle, 1x1 rectangle? or 1x4?). It got diag once = 1 on the pre-fix build — with a 1x4 rectangle an unlatched announce would print 4 times. So the intermediate probe must have been 1x1 (vacuous), i.e., prefix_repinned_RED.out predates the vacuity repair. That's consistent with the disclosure: the first table found the vacuity, then the probe was repaired, then the final table (the one in killtable/) was run with the 1x4 probe.
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:486:  One more check on the vacuity repair: with the 1x4 rectangle, do four rows really rewrite the same byte? LIN_A: ofs = x & 4095, x=0 always (x1=0, x2=0 → only x=0), y goes 0..3 but ignored. tile_nr = x >> 12 = 0. tileptr = 0x80000000 | (re_linear_a[0] << 12) = phys 0x10000. ofs = 0. So all four pixels PUT to phys 0x10000 with bufdepth 4 (BUFDEPTH_32 → (mode>>8)&3 = 2 → bufdepth 4). So each PUT writes 4 bytes at the same address; the value written each time is `color` = fg (post-fix) — same value each time → final value fg. Oracle survives. Confirmed by BASE.out row 7: 0x77665544. And the diag count: 4 GET declines → unlatched would print 4, latched prints 1. Confirmed by M_NOLATCH (4) vs BASE (1).
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:599:  - Check the witness probe vs current probe drift: witness probe md5 a734... differs from the current probe 1dae... — expected, since the oracle was re-pinned and the rectangle reshaped. The witness_20260906/probe.frozen.py is the intermediate (repinned, 1x1?) version. prefix_repinned_RED.out note for diag once says "LATCH: exactly one announcement, not one per pixel" and got=1 on the PRE-fix build. Wait — on the pre-fix build the announce was UNLATCHED, so with a 1x4 rectangle it would print 4 times. prefix_repinned_RED.out got=1 means the intermediate probe drew 1x1. That's consistent with the disclosure that the vacuity was found in the FIRST table (which used the 1x1 probe) and repaired after. So the timeline: witness (7 rows, 1x1, PASS on HEAD) → re-pinned oracle (8 rows, still 1x1: prefix_repinned_RED 7/8 — only `lin_b is fg` red; diag once vacuously green at 1) → first table (M_NOLATCH escaped 8/8, M_RANDOM mis-built) → probe repaired to 1x4, M_RANDOM rebuilt → final table (the one in killtable/: all mutants killed). 
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:601:  Hmm — one wrinkle: the packet says "the committed build measured RED under the new expectations (7/8, preserved as prefix_repinned_RED.out), and only then was the code changed". The re-pin at that point still had the vacuous latch row, but the re-pinning row that matters (`lin_b is fg`) was present and red. The vacuity repair came later and only strengthened the instrument. Also the final 8/8 post-fix is with the repaired probe — BASE.out. So the ordering obligations hold.
regress\artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:673:  - Test-first chain: `head_79cd12e.out` (7/7 original witness) → `prefix_repinned_RED.out` (committed binary, 7/8, red exactly on the new inverting row `lin_b is fg`) → post-fix 8/8. The 1×1→1×4 vacuity repair is documented in the probe itself (`probe.frozen.py:189-197`), and the height-not-width reasoning is sound: with x=0 and LIN_A's `ofs = x & 4095`, four rows hit the same byte, so the value oracle survives — confirmed by BASE showing `0x77665544`. Shipped probe == frozen probe (md5 1daecf60).
