regress\artifacts\sgimtedepth4dead\grok_7g1_20260909.txt:9:Clause (1) and clause (2) are an OR. For M4 both fail, so “(e)” is cheap and right. For B/C they come apart, and pass-1 quoted only clause (2). The switch *is* the dispatch that reaches `if (depth == 32)` at all. `#488`’s canonical clause-(d) form is `if (X)` → `if (X && Y)` on a dominating predicate; `&& dst_tlb == MTE_TLB_A` is that shape, it is guest-reachable with one `MTE_MODE` store, and every shipped row programs TLB_A (`MODE_TILED_FILL = 0x200`, DST_BUF bits clear). Dest[0] of A’s tile cannot move under a B-only skip, so if the labels are floor cells the detector is vacuous against that mutant *by construction* — the CHANNEL line already knows that at writing time. Sibling parity names the labels as siblings. `#516`’s restatement of (e) as “unreachable, or a capability the tree lacks” would also refuse this disclosure: B and C need neither. And I adopted the cheaper reading over the seat that enumerated the space, which is the bias the docket already named against me.
