regress\artifacts\sgirergbroundtrip\glm_BURN_thinking_20260907.txt:69:- M1_verbatim (clause b): 4/6, 4/8. "verbatim" — presumably the mutant keeps the original code verbatim somewhere? "clause b" — the L1-L4 disposal scheme references clauses (a)-(e)? We see "clause (d)" = floor breaches (kill table coverage) and "clause (e)" = falsifiability/completeness claims. M1_verbatim at 4/6: kills 2 rows — same signature as pre-fix code. Presumably M1 is the unfixed code (original GET), which kills rgb chan + rgb not corrupt → 4/6, 4/8. Consistent: the unfixed code is a killable mutant. Good.
regress\artifacts\sgirergbroundtrip\glm_BURN_thinking_20260907.txt:241:- rgb alive: process survival during the RGB session. Subsumed by...? It exists to catch a fix that crashes on the RGB path specifically. live catches a crash in the FIRST session (or the whole probe?). If "rgb alive" is a separate session's liveness, a mutant crashing only in the RGB session kills rgb alive but maybe not live (if live's session runs first and completes). Non-vacuous: it catches RGB-session-specific crashes that live (global liveness?) might miss — depends on session ordering, not inlined. Can it stay green under mutants it exists to catch? A crash-mutant: rgb alive reds ✓. But note a subtle vacuity risk: if alive is a GLOBAL variable ("alive" printed at row time — `row("rgb alive", "alive" if alive else "died", "alive",` — it prints the CURRENT value of `alive` at row-emission time. If the crash happens AFTER this row prints but BEFORE the last row, rgb alive stays green while the probe died later — and rows-emitted would catch the missing later rows (loud). OK.
regress\artifacts\sgirergbroundtrip\glm_BURN_thinking_20260907.txt:247:Also for Q5: "rgb alive" — `row("rgb alive", "alive" if alive else "died", "alive",` — its "channel" is a Python-side flag, not a guest load. If `alive` is set by an earlier session and this row merely PRINTS it, then "rgb alive" adds nothing over "live" (same flag) — potential vacuity: two rows printing the same variable. live: `printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died"` vs rgb alive: `"alive" if alive else "died"`. Both read the same `alive` flag! If `alive` is a single global set once (e.g., after the liveness session), then "rgb alive" is literally the same fact printed twice — VACUOUS as an independent witness: whatever kills "live" kills it, and whatever doesn't, doesn't. UNLESS `alive` is updated per-session (e.g., re-tested before this row). The packet says "its channel is the destination word read back through a guest load, except rgb alive (process survival)" — "rgb alive" = process survival — but of WHICH process? If the probe runs ONE guest session total... the rows seem to come from multiple sessions (samemode is its own session per the gate comment "every other session"). If each row = separate guest session, `alive` at row-print time reflects that row's session → rgb alive witnesses the RGB session's survival specifically → non-vacuous. Not determinable from packet — the scoping of `alive` (per-session vs global) is not inlined. Name it: if `alive` is a global set once by the liveness session and never re-evaluated, "rgb alive" is a duplicate of "live" and vacuous; settle by inlining how `alive` is assigned per row/session.
regress\artifacts\sgirergbroundtrip\glm_BURN_thinking_20260907.txt:249:That's a real potential vacuity — good Q5 content.
regress\artifacts\sgimtedepth4dead\codex_7g1_20260909.txt:3:Arguing against my pass-1 position first: 72 counted admitted inputs, without establishing that the rule requires those distinctions. The source-selector acceptance check does not distinguish A from B from C within its accepted region. The fill/copy test occurs later. The two guarded assignments do not test those choices. Moreover, three labels sharing a block do not constitute three individually dominating predicates: the common entry condition is membership in A/B/C. Thus ordinary control-flow analysis alone does not justify my enumeration. My earlier claim that `fill b top` covered B was also wrong for the named detector.
