regress\artifacts\sgirergbroundtrip\glm_BURN2_thinking_20260907.txt:305:Also (b) "the copy silently falls back": if TLB_A setup fails and the source falls back to linear silently — then the GET is still a REAL RGB GET (of the linear source) producing RGB_FIXED — the row would pass without ever exercising the tiled path. The §C2 corrections show the failure mode is "decline" (FG_VAL), not "fallback" — but that's two observed instances, not a proof of the mode space. From the packet I can't rule out fallback; the settling fact: whether the TLB_A setup path has any silent fallback to linear (e.g., a default case in a switch on tlb_mode, or the `tlb_mode <= 2 && tile_nr >= 256` bound falling back). Note Q2's premise lists `tlb_mode <= 2 && tile_nr >= 256` as a dominating predicate — a mutant that conditions the fix on `tlb_mode > 3`... M12_linear conditions on `linear` = `tlb_mode > 3` per B2. Hmm wait: "repair conditioned on `linear` (`tlb_mode > 3`)" — so `linear` ≡ tlb_mode > 3. And the bound predicate is `tlb_mode <= 2 && tile_nr >= 256` — for tlb_mode == 3, neither linear nor the bound... there's a THIRD tlb_mode regime?? tlb_mode ∈ {≤2, 3, >3}. linear = tlb_mode > 3. The bound check triggers for tlb_mode ≤ 2 with tile_nr ≥ 256. What is tlb_mode == 3?! A THIRD addressing regime that NO ROW DRIVES (the packet never mentions a row with tlb_mode == 3, and B2 says the repair used TLB_A — is TLB_A tlb_mode ≤ 2? or == 3?). Hmm — this is a genuinely important enumeration point for Q2/Q3: the packet's own predicates imply tlb_mode has at least three regimes (≤2, 3, >3), and the rows (as named/inlined) cover linear (>3) and "tiled" (TLB_A — presumably ≤2 or ==3, unspecified). If TLB_A is tlb_mode ≤ 2 and no row drives tlb_mode == 3, then a mutant conditioned on `tlb_mode != 3`... wait, but mutants edit the FIX, and the fix's guard is the color_mode switch — the fix line executes whenever case DE_MODE_TYPE_RGB is reached in a GET. The conditioning mutants add predicates. A mutant conditioning the fix on `tlb_mode != 3` (or on `!(tlb_mode==3)`) would only be caught by a row driving tlb_mode == 3. If no row drives it, that's a third-axis-shaped hole — but only if tlb_mode==3 is a regime the body actually distinguishes. From the packet: the body's predicates listed include the `tlb_mode <= 2 && tile_nr >= 256` bound — so tlb_mode==3 IS distinguished by the bound predicate (it's the regime where the bound doesn't apply and linear is false). If the GET body behaves differently at tlb_mode==3 vs ≤2 (the bound return), then a fix-conditioning mutant on that predicate is a real unrowed cell. HOLD ON — but does the FIX's execution depend on tlb_mode at all? The fix is one line inside `case DE_MODE_TYPE_RGB:`. Whether the line executes depends on reaching the case — which requires passing the earlier guards (coordinate guard, bound, validity gate). A conditioning mutant adds a predicate like `if (tlb_mode == 3) buggy else fixed`. To catch it you need a session where the RGB GET executes at tlb_mode == 3. If TLB_A (the tiled row's source) is tlb_mode ≤ 2 (where the bound can apply), and no row drives tlb_mode == 3, the mutant survives. From the packet I cannot tell what TLB_A's tlb_mode is. SETTLING FACT: the tlb_mode value the `rgb tiled` session drives, and whether any row drives tlb_mode == 3. This is a concrete, named, checkable hole — EXACTLY what Q2/Q3 asks for. 
